export const KNOWLEDGE_CHUNKS: { doc: string; ref: string; text: string; weight: number }[] = [{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 2","text":"A NOTE FROM ALLAN\n\nBuild the system\nbefore you open.\n“Okay na ’yan. Bukas na tayo.” It is easy to mistake an open door for a business\nthat is ready to operate. The hard part starts when rent, stock, payroll, permits\nand customer expectations arrive at the same time.\nIn 2012, I opened a Japan Surplus Appliance store in Puerto Princesa, Palawan.\nThere was no similar store in the city. I had a direct line to an importer in\nManila, a cost advantage and a business model inspired by hardware stores that\nhad lasted for decades.\nThe store still closed. Sales were slow, while rent, utilities and the cost of stock\nsitting on shelves kept running. A promising idea did not give me the runway or\noperating system I needed to sustain it.\nThat experience changed how I think about starting a business. Opening takes\nmoney, permits and energy. Building a business also takes cash discipline, clear\nprocesses and work your team can repeat without calling you for every decision.\nDiskarte is a real strength: the ability to improvise, adapt and find a way\nforward. Give it a structure. Document what works. Know your numbers. Set up\ncompliance before an inspection. Build checklists your people can use when you\nare not there.\nThis guide is for Filipino owners opening or strengthening a micro-to-small,\nbrick-and-mortar business: a café, salon, water refilling station, small shop,\nminimart or similar operation. Work through it before major commitments. If\nyou have already opened, use it to find and close the gaps.\nThe goal is a business with enough structure to carry the everyday work. Start\nwith the next unfinished step, complete it properly, and keep going.\nTHE PRINCIPLE\n\nOpening a business is the start. A repeatable system helps you sustain it.\n\nMORE THAN A CHECKLIST\n\nThis guide is part of PromdiPreneur’s mission to help Filipino\nentrepreneurs build better business systems. Visit us to learn\npractical ways to improve processes, discover digital tools,\nexplore implemented systems, or ask a business question.\npromdipreneur.com\n\nSCAN TO\nVISIT\n\n2","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 3","text":"READ • DECIDE • DO\n\nHow to use this book\n01 Start with the decision in front of you\nUse the contents page to choose a chapter. Read Chapter 1 before committing\ncapital, then work through the remaining stages. Some registration and\npremises steps overlap; confirm your LGU’s sequence and prerequisites before\nsigning or paying.\n\n02 Read one action card at a time\nEach item has a clear action, the risk it addresses, a practical way to do it, and a\ncompletion test. The chapter checklist lets you mark progress and return to the\nright page quickly.\n\n03 Keep proof, not just a tick\nSave the calculation, signed document, confirmation, training record or tested\nprocedure. “In process” is still open. Write the next action, owner and due date\non your opening dashboard.\n\n04 Pause at a verification note\nRules and procedures can differ by location, industry, taxpayer category and\nemployee coverage. Check the specific agency, current issuance and your\ncircumstances. A planning example is not a legal threshold or a guaranteed cost.\n\n05 Use the templates with professional review\nThe appendix gives editable starting frameworks. Replace every bracketed field,\nresolve the choices and have the relevant professional review the completed\ndocument before it is signed, sent or submitted.\nA TICK MEANS DONE\n\nUse the chapter checklists to track progress. Keep proof of completion in one\naccessible business folder.","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 4","text":"READ THIS BEFORE YOU ACT\n\nPlan carefully.\nVerify the details.\nThis is a practical planning guide, not legal, accounting or compliance advice.\nGovernment requirements, fees and procedures change, and LGU requirements\nvary. Consult the relevant agency and a licensed Philippine professional before\nacting on a consequential compliance, tax, lease or employment decision. The\nsource notes identify key rules to check; they do not certify that a business or\ncompleted template is compliant. Publication review: this design update does\nnot independently validate the source’s legal and regulatory references.\nRecheck all government requirements, rates, fees, deadlines and links against\ncurrent official sources before publication or action.\n\nWhat the labels mean\nDO THIS\n\nThe action you can work on now.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nThe practical risk this step addresses.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nA specific approach, example or operating habit.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe evidence that lets you close the task.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE\n\nA requirement, rate, deadline or legal point to confirm for your business.\nKey rules and official references appear near the back. Rates, fees and local\nrequirements should be rechecked before you act.\nTemplate fields are intentionally left in [BRACKETS] for you and your adviser to\ncomplete.","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 5","text":"YOUR ROUTE THROUGH THE BOOK\n\nContents\n01 Before You Register 8\n02 Navigate Business Registration 25\n03 Before You Rent 43\n04 Capital & Logistics 61\n05 Before You Hire 78\n06 Before You Sell 96\n07 Before You Spend Your First Peso 114\nYour opening dashboard 6\nTemplates & practical tools 131\nOfficial sources & verification notes 155\nBuild your next system 159\nTap a chapter title to jump there. Each chapter also has a linked checklist so you\ncan find an individual item.","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 6","text":"MAKE THE WORK VISIBLE\n\nYour opening dashboard\nUse this page for your next decisions. Keep a separate task list if your team\nneeds more room.\nBusiness / proposed location\n________________________________________________________________\nTarget opening window\n________________________________________________________________\nCash available for opening and runway\n________________________________________________________________\nBiggest unresolved risk\n________________________________________________________________\n\nYour next three actions\n01 ACTION / EVIDENCE NEEDED\n________________________________________________________\nOwner: ___________________ Due: ___________________\n\n02 ACTION / EVIDENCE NEEDED\n________________________________________________________\nOwner: ___________________ Due: ___________________\n\n03 ACTION / EVIDENCE NEEDED\n________________________________________________________\nOwner: ___________________ Due: ___________________\n\nBefore you commit more money\nAsk: Is the demand credible? Can the cash plan sustain a slow start? Can this\nlocation be permitted? What will be hard or expensive to reverse?","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 7","text":"A QUICK NUMBERS REFERENCE\n\nThree numbers\nworth knowing\nUse these alongside Chapter 1. Write down the assumptions, then test them\nagainst real demand and cash timing.\n01 OPENING CAPITAL\n\nOne-time opening costs + operating runway + emergency buffer\n\nList permits, fit-out, equipment and initial stock. Then model the slow-start\nperiod. The planning approach in this book uses three zero-revenue operating\nmonths and a 20%–30% buffer on opening costs plus runway. Adjust the\nassumptions to the actual risks.\n02 UNIT CONTRIBUTION\n\nSelling price per unit − variable cost per unit\n\nUse the amount left after the costs that move with each sale. This contribution\npays fixed operating costs. If prices, products or margins differ, use a realistic\nweighted sales mix with your accountant.\n03 OPERATING BREAK-EVEN\n\nMonthly fixed operating costs ÷ contribution per unit\nThen divide monthly break-even units by operating days.\n\nUse your actual operating days. Compare the resulting daily sales target with\nobserved demand. Track loan-principal payments, owner drawings, stock\npurchases and other cash timing separately in the cash forecast; accounting\nprofit and available cash are different checks.\n\nKeep both views\nThe profit view asks whether the business earns enough. The cash view asks\nwhether money is available when each payment falls due. Review both before\ntaking on a fixed commitment.","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 8","text":"CHAPTER 01 / BEFORE YOU REGISTER\n\nCHAPTER 01\n\nBefore You Register\n\nThe foundation and idea checklist\n\nBefore you file a form, sign a lease, or pay for fit-out, check whether the\nbusiness can work. Registration makes your decisions official. It does not prove\nthat customers will buy, that the location is right, or that your capital will last.\nThese fifteen checks help you test the idea, understand the numbers, and\nprepare to run the business. Be honest about what you know and what still\nneeds evidence. A problem found on paper is usually easier to fix than one found\nafter opening.\n\nStart here\n• Start with demand, startup capital, and your daily break-even target.\n• Check the location, business structure, and name before making expensive\ncommitments.\n• Finish with a one-page business concept that brings your decisions together.\n\nBEFORE YOU MOVE ON\n\n• Demand is supported by observations of actual buying behavior.\n• Capital covers pre-opening costs, runway, and a buffer.\n• Daily break-even, 12-month scenarios, and 13-week cash flow are written\ndown.\n• The structure, name, location, suppliers, and operating coverage have been\nchecked.\n• Your one-page concept and Month 3, 6, and 12 targets are ready.","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 9","text":"CHAPTER 01 / BEFORE YOU REGISTER\n\nCHAPTER 01 / YOUR CHECKLIST\n\nMark your progress\nCheck an item only when the work is complete and the evidence is saved.\n☐ 1.01 Test demand with real buying behavior\n☐ 1.02 Build your capital estimate, including a buffer\n☐ 1.03 Calculate your daily break-even target\n☐ 1.04 Choose the business structure deliberately\n☐ 1.05 Audit competitors within two kilometers\n☐ 1.06 Define the customer you will serve first\n☐ 1.07 Verify zoning before committing to the space\n☐ 1.08 Check the name before building the brand\n☐ 1.09 Project the first twelve months\n☐ 1.10 Separate business and personal money\n☐ 1.11 Get supplier terms in writing\n☐ 1.12 Plan for daily operations and your absence\n☐ 1.13 Forecast cash week by week\n☐ 1.14 Set numerical checkpoints for months 3, 6, and 12\n☐ 1.15 Put the business concept on one page\n\nKeep in mind\nThe foundation is stronger when the visible spending follows the thinking. Count\ndemand before signing a lease. Know your break-even target before setting\nprices. Separate the money before the first sale. Decide when weak results will\ntrigger a review.\nYou do not need certainty about every future event. You need clear assumptions,\nenough evidence to challenge them, and a plan for responding when reality\nchanges. Keep this work together; the next chapters build on it.","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 10","text":"CHAPTER 01 / BEFORE YOU REGISTER\n\nCHAPTER 01 • CHECKLIST 1.01\n\nTest demand with real buying\nbehavior\nDO THIS\n\nSpend at least two weeks checking demand around your intended location\nbefore making a major commitment. Look for people who could become paying\ncustomers, then compare what you observe with the sales your business will\nneed.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nEncouragement from friends, family, and barkada can help you get started. It\ndoes not tell you how often strangers will buy or how much they will spend. A\nmilk tea shop can attract plenty of praise and still struggle if nearby foot traffic\ndoes not turn into enough daily purchases.\nYou need evidence that connects the idea to an actual place, price, and buying\nroutine. Keep friendly feedback separate from your demand estimate so it does\nnot make the numbers look stronger than they are.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nDo a “shadow count”: stand near the proposed location and manually count foot\ntraffic at different times. Include early morning, lunch, merienda, and early\nevening. Record the time and the count so you can compare visits.\nVisit the nearest similar business. Observe transaction time, queue length, and\ncustomer behavior. Look at how many passersby stop to buy, rather than\nassuming every passerby is a prospect.\nIf there is no similar business nearby, find out why. The gap may be an\nopportunity, but it may also point to weak demand or a location problem. Use\nthe evidence in your break-even check in Item 3.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou have two weeks of observations, a clear picture of likely buyers, and a\ndemand estimate supported by buying behavior rather than encouragement\nalone.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 11","text":"CHAPTER 01 / BEFORE YOU REGISTER\n\nCHAPTER 01 • CHECKLIST 1.02\n\nBuild your capital estimate, including\na buffer\nDO THIS\n\nCalculate the money you need to open and stay open before spending. Use three\ncolumns: pre-opening costs, operating runway, and an emergency buffer. Fund\nall three, or change the plan before committing.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nIt is easy to count equipment, opening inventory, fit-out, and the first month’s\nrent. Less visible costs can use up the money just as quickly: rent during fit-out,\npermits and compliance, employer contributions, and the cost of correcting early\nmistakes.\nSales may take time to build. If all your capital goes into opening day, even a\npromising business can run out of cash before it develops a regular customer\nbase. Budgeting for slow sales gives you room to learn and adjust.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nColumn 1: Pre-opening costs. List every one-time cost before opening, including\npermits, fit-out, equipment, and initial inventory. Check whether your landlord\ngives you a rent-free fit-out period; do not assume one.\nColumn 2: Operating runway. Set aside three full months of projected operating\nexpenses, calculated at zero revenue. Include rent, payroll, utilities, supplier\ncommitments, and applicable SSS, PhilHealth, and Pag-IBIG costs.\nColumn 3: Emergency buffer. Add 20%–30% of Columns 1 and 2 combined. A\n30% allowance is the more cautious end of this planning range.\nIf available capital cannot cover the three columns, delay, save more, reduce\nfixed costs, or restructure the business model.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour estimate includes opening costs, three zero-revenue months, and a\n20%–30% buffer, with a realistic funding source for the total.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nCheck current government fees and employer-contribution requirements before\nfinalizing the budget.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 12","text":"CHAPTER 01 / BEFORE YOU REGISTER\n\nCHAPTER 01 • CHECKLIST 1.03\n\nCalculate your daily break-even target\nDO THIS\n\nWork out how many units you must sell each day to cover your costs before you\nsign a lease or accept another fixed commitment. Then compare that target with\nthe demand evidence from Item 1.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nBreak-even turns a general hope into an operating question: can this business\nsell enough, at this price, on an ordinary day? You need that answer to judge the\nlocation, pricing, and product mix.\nA busy opening day can hide a weak model. The useful test is whether an\naverage Tuesday can support the business. Finding that the target is unrealistic\nnow gives you time to adjust rent, staffing, pricing, or the offer before those\ndecisions become expensive to reverse.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nAdd your fixed monthly operating costs, including rent, the fixed portion of\nutilities, salaries, and mandatory employer contributions. Keep the assumptions\nvisible so you can update them later.\nFor each item, subtract its variable cost from its selling price. The amount left is\nits contribution toward fixed costs. If you sell several products, use an average\nbased on the mix you expect to sell.\nDivide monthly fixed costs by average contribution per item to estimate monthly\nbreak-even units. Divide by 26 operating days if that is your schedule; otherwise\nuse your actual planned days.\nAlso allow for loan payments in your cash planning. Ask your accountant to\nseparate cash obligations from accounting expenses so you do not confuse\noperating break-even with cash available.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou can state your monthly and daily sales targets, explain the assumptions, and\nshow why the location could realistically deliver them.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nHave your accountant check contribution margins, fixed costs, and the separate\ncash impact of loan payments.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 13","text":"CHAPTER 01 / BEFORE YOU REGISTER\n\nCHAPTER 01 • CHECKLIST 1.04\n\nChoose the business structure\ndeliberately\nDO THIS\n\nDecide on your business structure before starting registration. Compare sole\nproprietorship, One Person Corporation (OPC), and a regular corporation\nagainst your ownership plans, liability exposure, financing, and ability to\nmanage ongoing compliance.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA sole proprietorship is commonly used by single-owner businesses, but the\nbusiness does not provide the same legal separation from its owner as a\ncorporation. Business debts and claims can expose personal assets.\nA corporation or OPC can provide a separate legal entity, with additional\nrecords, filings, and costs. That protection has limits; it should not be treated as\na guarantee that an owner can never be personally liable.\nChanging structure later may involve new registration work and changes to\ncontracts, accounts, and records. Make the decision while you can compare\noptions without the pressure of an opening date.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nStart with three questions: Who will own the business? What could go wrong\nfinancially or operationally? What annual compliance work can you realistically\nmaintain?\nFor example, a single-owner, brick-and-mortar business with startup capital\nbelow ₱1 million may consider sole proprietorship. The amount alone does not\nsettle suitability; compare its risks and obligations with the other structures\nbefore deciding.\nIf you expect to borrow, bring in investors, or operate in a field with significant\nliability exposure, such as food service, personal care, or construction, speak\nwith a corporate lawyer before registering.\nAsk specifically about the OPC option introduced under the Revised Corporation\nCode of 2019. Confirm eligibility, capital rules, exceptions, and ongoing\nobligations.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou have chosen a structure, understand its personal-risk and compliance\nimplications, and have resolved any legal questions before filing.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm current OPC eligibility, capital exceptions, and the legal and annual\ncompliance implications of each structure.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 14","text":"CHAPTER 01 / BEFORE YOU REGISTER\n\nCHAPTER 01 • CHECKLIST 1.05\n\nAudit competitors within two\nkilometers\nDO THIS\n\nVisit similar businesses within a 2-kilometer radius of your intended location.\nFind one customer need you can meet measurably better, then turn that\nadvantage into a clear operating standard.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA busy competitor may show that demand exists. It does not prove that the area\ncan support another business offering the same thing. New entrants can end up\ncompeting for the same customers without enough sales for everyone.\nCompetition itself is not a reason to stop. Entering without understanding it\nmakes your decisions harder. You need to know what nearby businesses charge,\nwhere they perform well, and what customers still struggle to get.\nYour business does not have to be radically different. A practical improvement\nthat matters to a specific customer can be a useful starting position.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nVisit as a regular customer and order something. Observe price points, service\nspeed, cleanliness, staff behavior, peak hours, and the kinds of customers who\nbuy. Take notes soon after each visit while the details are fresh.\nCompare businesses using the same questions. Where is service consistently\nslow? What useful product is missing? Which part of the experience falls short of\nwhat your intended customer needs?\nChoose one gap your team can reliably address. Write it in plain language and\nmake it specific enough to check in daily operations.\nUse that promise in your opening message, then build the staffing, stock, and\nservice routine needed to deliver it. Avoid promising an advantage your\noperating model cannot sustain.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou have a competitor comparison, one clear customer-relevant advantage, and\na practical way to deliver and measure it.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 15","text":"CHAPTER 01 / BEFORE YOU REGISTER\n\nCHAPTER 01 • CHECKLIST 1.06\n\nDefine the customer you will serve\nfirst\nDO THIS\n\nWrite one sentence describing your primary customer. Include their age range,\nspending behavior or income bracket, daily routine, and the problem your\nbusiness solves. Use it to guide opening decisions.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\n“Everyone” gives you little help when choosing products, prices, hours, or a\nlocation. “People who want affordable food” is still too broad to tell you which\nfood, what price, or when those people need it.\nA wide product range can feel safer because it appears to appeal to more people.\nIt can also scatter your attention and make it harder to become consistently\ngood at the offer that brings customers back.\nChoose a primary customer for the opening plan. You can learn from other\nbuyers later without making every possible customer a priority from the start.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nUse this example as a model: “Office workers and BPO employees aged 22–35\nwho want a fast, affordable lunch within walking distance of their building, with\nan option beyond a carinderia or mall food court.”\nThat sentence gives you a basis for decisions about menu size, preparation time,\nprice points, opening hours, packaging, and walking distance. Rewrite it until it\ndoes the same for your business.\nNow review your proposed products, location, hours, and opening message. Ask\nhow each choice helps this person. If a choice does not serve that customer,\ndecide whether it belongs in the opening plan or can wait.\nUse actual observations from your demand checks to refine the description\nrather than relying only on the customer you hope to attract.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour customer sentence is specific enough to explain your main choices about\nproducts, prices, location, hours, and marketing.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 16","text":"CHAPTER 01 / BEFORE YOU REGISTER\n\nCHAPTER 01 • CHECKLIST 1.07\n\nVerify zoning before committing to\nthe space\nDO THIS\n\nAsk the relevant LGU office whether the exact address can legally support your\nintended business activity. Get written confirmation before signing a lease or\ncommitting to fit-out.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nAn attractive space can still be the wrong place to operate. Local zoning may\nclassify it as residential, commercial, industrial, or mixed-use, with conditions\nthat affect which activities are allowed.\nPrevious tenants and nearby businesses are useful background, but they do not\nestablish that your proposed use is permitted. A landlord’s assurance that\nanother tenant had a permit cannot replace confirmation from the LGU.\nIf a zoning problem appears after you have paid for improvements, resolving it\nmay cost time and money or leave you with a space that cannot support your\nbusiness. Make this a location check, not a final paperwork check.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nVisit or contact your LGU’s Zoning Administrator, Zoning Office, or Office of the\nBuilding Official and ask which office handles the confirmation you need. Give\nthe exact address and describe the planned activity clearly.\nRequest the applicable zoning certification or written determination. Ask about\nthe current documents, fee, and processing time rather than assuming the visit\nwill be free or finished in a day.\nIf the property is residentially zoned but has housed commercial tenants, ask\nwhether a valid variance, exemption, or reclassification exists and whether it\ncovers your proposed activity.\nKeep the written response in your location file. Resolve any conditions before\nrelying on the space in your launch budget or timetable.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou have written LGU confirmation for the exact address and activity, and any\nconditions or required approvals are clear before you commit.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the zoning office, documents, fee, processing time, and conditions for\nthe exact address and business activity.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 17","text":"CHAPTER 01 / BEFORE YOU REGISTER\n\nCHAPTER 01 • CHECKLIST 1.08\n\nCheck the name before building the\nbrand\nDO THIS\n\nCheck your proposed name through DTI, SEC, and IPOPHL before ordering\nsigns, packaging, or other branded materials. Resolve potential conflicts before\nyou invest in recognition.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA name can feel personal and memorable while already being used or protected\nby another business. Discovering a conflict after you have printed materials and\nbuilt a social following creates avoidable replacement work and expense.\nBusiness-name registration and trademark protection serve different purposes.\nA name accepted by one registration system should not be treated as clearance\nacross every other system.\nIf the business may grow into a larger brand or a franchise, consider trademark\nprotection early. The cost of changing a name grows as more customers,\nsuppliers, and materials come to depend on it.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nCheck DTI’s current business-name search for sole proprietorship records. Use\nthe official DTI website to reach the live service rather than relying on an old\nportal address.\nCheck the SEC’s current company-name verification service for corporations\nand OPCs. Look beyond identical spelling when assessing potentially confusing\nnames.\nSearch the Intellectual Property Office of the Philippines trademark records for\nthe name and relevant similar marks. Ask for professional advice if the results\nare unclear or the brand will be central to expansion.\nThe Philippines generally uses a first-to-file trademark system, but trademark\nrights and objections have legal qualifications. Discuss filing early rather than\nassuming that using a name first guarantees protection or that every later filing\ndefeats earlier rights.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou have checked all three systems, recorded any concerns, and decided\nwhether to seek trademark advice or file before investing further in the name.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nUse the current official DTI, SEC, and IPOPHL search services; seek trademark\nadvice if similar names or rights are unclear.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 18","text":"CHAPTER 01 / BEFORE YOU REGISTER\n\nCHAPTER 01 • CHECKLIST 1.09\n\nProject the first twelve months\nDO THIS\n\nBuild a 12-month financial projection in three scenarios before accepting fixed\ncosts. Write down your assumptions about customers, average spend, product\ncosts, and monthly expenses, then see when each scenario becomes viable.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA projection helps you test the decisions behind the business. It does not\npromise that the year will unfold as planned. Even rough numbers are useful\nwhen you can see the assumptions and challenge them.\nWithout a model, you may know that the bank balance is falling without knowing\nwhich decision needs attention. With one, you can compare actual results with\ncustomer volume, pricing, costs, and timing to see where the gap started.\nThe first version can be simple. Its value comes from making the reasoning\nvisible and updating it as better evidence arrives.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nBuild a conservative case with traffic and sales 30% below your shadow-count\nestimate. Build a base case that matches the estimate, and an optimistic case\n20% above it.\nUse a realistic conversion from foot traffic to paying customers in every case.\nRun all three through the same cost structure and identify when cash flow\nbecomes positive.\nTreat an optimistic case that still does not break even within nine months as a\nstrong prompt to review the model. Use nine months as a planning warning\npoint and adjust it to the economics of your business.\nReview heavy fixed costs, low prices, weak margins, or unrealistic volume\nassumptions. Make changes on paper and rerun the scenarios before using your\ncapital to test them.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou have three monthly scenarios, visible assumptions, and a clear explanation\nof what must happen for the business to become viable.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 19","text":"CHAPTER 01 / BEFORE YOU REGISTER\n\nCHAPTER 01 • CHECKLIST 1.10\n\nSeparate business and personal\nmoney\nDO THIS\n\nSet up a dedicated account permitted for your business use before the first\ntransaction. Route business money through it, record every movement, and\nestablish a regular owner’s draw appropriate to your structure.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nWhen household and business money share the same account or wallet, it\nbecomes difficult to tell whether the business pays its own way. Personal savings\ncan quietly cover operating gaps while daily sales make things feel “okay\nnaman.”\nClear separation lets you see what the business earns, spends, borrows, and\nreceives from you. It also gives your accountant a cleaner record and helps you\nmake decisions using the actual business position.\nThis discipline matters while the business is small. Waiting until it stabilizes can\nleave months of mixed transactions to reconstruct later.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nAsk a suitable bank or financial service which account supports your business\nstructure and intended transactions. A rural bank or a digital provider may be\npractical, but verify the account’s terms and requirements rather than assuming\na personal savings account is suitable.\nIf considering providers such as Maya Business or UnionDigital, check their\ncurrent products, eligibility rules, and permitted uses directly before choosing\nan account.\nChoose a fixed amount and regular schedule for owner withdrawals instead of\ntaking cash whenever a personal expense appears. Have your accountant\nexplain the correct treatment for your business structure.\nRecord money you put into the business separately from customer sales. Record\nbusiness expenses and owner withdrawals clearly too. The point is to make\nevery peso entering or leaving the business traceable.\nDONE WHEN\n\nBusiness transactions have a separate, suitable account and clear records, with\na regular withdrawal routine and no unexplained mixing of personal spending.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the account permits your business use and that the withdrawal method\nsuits your business structure.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 20","text":"CHAPTER 01 / BEFORE YOU REGISTER\n\nCHAPTER 01 • CHECKLIST 1.11\n\nGet supplier terms in writing\nDO THIS\n\nConfirm key supplier terms before using them in your pricing or inventory plan.\nKeep the written record and identify a backup for every input your business\ncannot go without for more than 48 hours.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA friendly conversation can leave important details unresolved. Minimum\norders, lead times, payment terms, return policies, and price changes all affect\nhow much stock and cash you need.\nFor example, a water refilling station may plan around a three-day chemical\ndelivery. If the real lead time becomes seven to ten days during peak season, the\nbusiness can run short just as customers begin relying on it.\nA supplier promise is more useful when both sides can refer to the same terms.\nYou do not need to wait for a problem before asking for clarity.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nGet written confirmation by formal letter, email, or a documented text exchange.\nFor each key supplier, cover four essentials: unit price and minimum order\nquantity; payment terms, including COD, net-15, or net-30; delivery lead time in\nnormal and peak periods; and the return or replacement policy for defective or\ndamaged deliveries.\nAlso ask how and when prices can change, because your selling price may\ndepend on those inputs. Update your costing when confirmed terms differ from\nthe original assumption.\nIdentify at least one alternative supplier for each critical input you cannot\noperate without for more than 48 hours. Check that the alternative can actually\nsupply what you need.\nKeep these details where the person ordering stock can find them.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour pricing and stock plan use confirmed supplier terms, and each critical input\nhas an identified backup source.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 21","text":"CHAPTER 01 / BEFORE YOU REGISTER\n\nCHAPTER 01 • CHECKLIST 1.12\n\nPlan for daily operations and your\nabsence\nDO THIS\n\nCheck whether you have the time, people, and routines to operate the business\nafter opening day. Write down how it will work during the first 90 days and\nduring a two-week absence.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nOpening is a project with a budget, deadline, and visible finish line. Operating\nrequires daily repetition: checking stock, serving customers, managing cash,\nsupporting staff, and handling problems.\nBeing good at preparing the opening does not automatically mean those routines\nare ready. If you cannot be present and no trained person or clear process can\ntake over, small decisions can quickly stall the business.\nYou are looking for an operating plan that ordinary employees can follow. It\nshould not depend on a family member knowing what you meant or on you\nanswering every phone call.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nAnswer three questions in writing. First: can you be physically present for the\nfirst 90 days? If not, who will manage, and when will that person be found and\ntrained?\nSecond: do you have, or can you finish before opening, a daily operations\nchecklist that a non-family employee can follow without asking you about every\ndecision? Include what must happen and who is responsible.\nThird: what happens if you are sick or unavailable for two weeks? Identify who\ntakes over routine work and which decisions need escalation.\nIf an answer is “I’ll work it out later,” treat that part of the operating model as\nunfinished. Build the missing coverage or routine into pre-opening preparation\nwhile you still have time to train and test it.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou have documented first-90-day coverage, a usable daily checklist, and a\ntwo-week absence plan with a responsible person for essential work.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 22","text":"CHAPTER 01 / BEFORE YOU REGISTER\n\nCHAPTER 01 • CHECKLIST 1.13\n\nForecast cash week by week\nDO THIS\n\nBuild a 13-week cash-flow forecast before opening and update it every week\nafterward. List expected cash receipts and payments by the week they will\nactually arrive or fall due.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nSales and available cash can move on different schedules. A business can record\nrevenue and still struggle to pay rent, wages, suppliers, and government\ncontributions when those payments become due.\nCustomer payment delays, stock purchases, and seasonal slowdowns can leave a\ngap between earning sales and having usable money in the account. Several\nlarge obligations falling in the same week can make that gap more serious.\nThe cash drawer at closing time does not show all the commitments already\nmade. A forecast helps you see those commitments before they become urgent.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nCreate one column for each of the next 13 weeks. Start with available cash, add\nthe cash you expect to collect, and subtract known payments. Carry the ending\nbalance into the following week.\nInclude expected customer receipts and other committed inflows. List rent,\npayroll, suppliers, loan payments, taxes, and contributions according to their\nactual timing. Avoid treating an unpaid sale as cash already available.\nFlag weeks where outflows exceed inflows, then check whether the opening\nbalance can cover the gap. Plan deliberately for any shortfall rather than hoping\nthe next week’s sales arrive early.\nUse the forecast to test the runway in Item 2. Update it each week as collection\ndates, orders, and obligations change, so it remains a working tool rather than\nan opening exercise.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou can identify upcoming tight-cash weeks, explain how the obligations will be\ncovered, and maintain a weekly update routine.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nCheck that forecast inflows reflect collection dates and that payment dates\nmatch the business’s actual obligations.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 23","text":"CHAPTER 01 / BEFORE YOU REGISTER\n\nCHAPTER 01 • CHECKLIST 1.14\n\nSet numerical checkpoints for months\n3, 6, and 12\nDO THIS\n\nDefine what acceptable progress looks like at Month 3, Month 6, and Month 12\nbefore opening. Use numbers that help you decide whether to continue as\nplanned, adjust the model, or consider closing.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\n“May tao naman” and “okay pa naman ang benta” describe a feeling without\nshowing whether the business is meeting its needs. A reference point makes\nperformance easier to evaluate honestly.\nWritten checkpoints can prompt a review while there is still time and money to\nmake changes. They also reduce the temptation to move the goalposts each time\nresults disappoint.\nThese targets should fit your business and financial model. They are decision\ntools, not promises that every business must grow at the same speed.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nMonth 3: set the minimum average daily sales volume that would justify\ncontinuing without major restructuring. Connect it to your demand assumptions\nand daily break-even target.\nMonth 6: set the revenue or cash-flow level that represents break-even, or a\nclearly defined distance from it. State which measure you will use.\nMonth 12: set the profitability threshold that would make the business viable\nand sustainable.\nUse this early-review trigger if suitable: when your Month 3 daily-sales target is\nmissed by more than 25% for two consecutive weeks, conduct a formal business\nreview.\nUse that review to examine what is not working and why. Look at the evidence,\ndecide what to change, and avoid treating encouragement alone as a corrective\nplan.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour three checkpoints are written in numbers, linked to your model, and paired\nwith a clear trigger for a structured review.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 24","text":"CHAPTER 01 / BEFORE YOU REGISTER\n\nCHAPTER 01 • CHECKLIST 1.15\n\nPut the business concept on one page\nDO THIS\n\nWrite a one-page Business Concept Summary before spending. Bring your\ncustomer, offer, location, advantage, capital, and targets into one clear\ndocument. Print it, keep it visible, and review it monthly.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nThe page gives you a baseline for everyday decisions. It makes the idea specific\nenough to test, explain, and revisit when real conditions change.\nWithout a clear baseline, a business can drift through small decisions: adding\nproducts that weaken its focus, cutting prices without checking margins, or\nhiring for roles nobody defined. Each choice may seem reasonable alone while\nthe overall model becomes harder to run.\nThe document is useful even if no investor or bank ever sees it. Write it for\nyourself and the people who need to understand the business.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nCover six things. 1. What you sell and the specific price points. 2. Your primary\ncustomer in one sentence. 3. Your location and why it serves that customer. 4.\nYour competitive advantage in three lines or fewer. 5. Your startup capital\nrequirement and where the money will come from. 6. Your Month 3 and Month\n12 numerical targets from Item 14.\nKeep the underlying monthly operating-cost estimate available alongside the\npage so the concept stays connected to the cost of running it.\nRead the page monthly against actual results. When reality differs, identify\nwhich assumption changed and what needs adjustment. Update the plan\ndeliberately rather than letting the business change direction without a clear\ndecision.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour one-page summary is complete, consistent with the earlier checks, and\neasy to use as a monthly reference for decisions.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 25","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02\n\nNavigate Business\nRegistration\nYour government-registration action plan\n\nRegistration involves several agencies, documents, and dependencies. Plan the\nroute before making office visits. Ask which steps can run together, which\napprovals depend on earlier documents, and which services are available online\nor through your LGU’s integrated process.\nUse this chapter as a preparation guide, then get the current checklist for your\nexact address, activity, and business structure. Requirements, fees, processing\ntimes, and online systems can change. Verify them with DTI or SEC, your\nbarangay and BPLO, BFP, BIR, and any relevant industry agency.\nAllow time for inspections, corrections, printing, and follow-up. A well-prepared\napplication can save return trips, but no single national timetable fits every\nbusiness or LGU.\n\nStart here\n• Get the current agency and LGU checklists before planning your visits.\n• Build your master document folder and map dependencies, online steps, and\nparallel tasks.\n• Keep the opening date conditional on the approvals and operating documents\nyour business needs.\nBEFORE YOU MOVE ON\n\n• The chosen name or entity registration is complete.\n• Local permits and supporting clearances match the address and activities.\n• BIR registration, compliant invoices, and bookkeeping arrangements are\nready.\n• Applicable employer and industry requirements are complete.\n• Approvals, payment records, expiry dates, and renewal responsibilities are\norganized.","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 26","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02 / YOUR CHECKLIST\n\nMark your progress\nCheck an item only when the work is complete and the evidence is saved.\n☐ 2.01 Start with the correct name or entity registration\n☐ 2.02 Confirm how to obtain barangay clearance\n☐ 2.03 Declare the right activities and address\n☐ 2.04 Map supporting clearances before making visits\n☐ 2.05 Prepare for the fire-safety inspection\n☐ 2.06 Complete the formal zoning requirement\n☐ 2.07 Plan around the local renewal calendar\n☐ 2.08 Prepare employer registrations before staff start\n☐ 2.09 Complete BIR registration before commencing business\n☐ 2.10 Choose tax options with a CPA\n☐ 2.11 Arrange compliant invoices before the first sale\n☐ 2.12 Set up and register the books of accounts\n☐ 2.13 Check industry-specific permits\n☐ 2.14 Prepare one master document folder\n☐ 2.15 Make compliance a condition of opening\n☐ 2.16 Keep proof of every registration payment\n\nKeep in mind\nRegistration is easier to manage when you arrive with the right documents,\nunderstand the dependencies, and start independent steps in parallel. Use one\nfolder and one sequence map instead of relying on memory between offices.\nKeep the opening date tied to readiness. Save the approvals, understand their\nconditions, and carry the same recordkeeping discipline into renewals and daily\noperations. Preparation is the first system you build for the business.","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 27","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02 • CHECKLIST 2.01\n\nStart with the correct name or entity\nregistration\nDO THIS\n\nComplete the registration appropriate to your chosen structure: DTI\nbusiness-name registration for a sole proprietorship, or SEC registration for a\ncorporation or OPC. Keep the certificate ready for the steps that depend on it.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nThis certificate is an important foundation document for later applications and\naccount opening. Starting a downstream application without a document it\nrequires can lead to a rejected submission or another trip.\nThe exact sequence may differ where online or integrated government services\nare available. Check the current process rather than assuming that every task\nmust happen at a separate office.\nName or entity registration is one part of the registration stack. Continue\nplanning the local, tax, employer, and industry-specific requirements needed\nbefore operation.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nFor a sole proprietorship, reach DTI’s current online business-name service\nthrough its official website. Choose the territorial scope that fits the\nbusiness-name coverage you need. Review the scope definitions and current fee\nschedule before paying.\nDownload the issued certificate, check the details, and save both digital and\nprinted copies for your master folder.\nFor a corporation or OPC, use the SEC’s current registration route for that\nentity type. Confirm which incorporation documents, declarations, and other\nattachments apply. Regular corporations and OPCs do not necessarily submit\nidentical documents.\nCheck the incorporation documents your route requires, including the Articles of\nIncorporation, any applicable By-Laws, and financial or officer declarations. Ask\nwhether a Treasurer’s Affidavit is required rather than copying an older\nchecklist. Prepare the confirmed documents before submitting.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe correct certificate is issued, its details are accurate, and you have copies\nplus a clear list of the next applications that need it.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the current DTI territorial fees and entity-specific SEC document\nchecklist through the official registration services.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 28","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02 • CHECKLIST 2.02\n\nConfirm how to obtain barangay\nclearance\nDO THIS\n\nAsk your barangay or BPLO how barangay business clearance is handled in the\ncurrent local process. If a separate clearance is required, secure it early enough\nto avoid holding up the business-permit application.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA missing clearance can stop an application even when the rest of your\ndocuments are ready. An inspection, if required, can also add time because it\ndepends on staff availability and access to your premises.\nSome local processes integrate the barangay step into a broader application.\nConfirm the route first so you do not make an unnecessary separate trip or\nassume a requirement has been completed for you.\nThe goal is a complete application with a realistic schedule. Use local\ninstructions for your address rather than copying another owner’s experience\nfrom a different barangay.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nAt the start of your registration week, ask for the current written checklist, fee\nschedule, payment method, and any inspection requirement.\nCheck whether you need your DTI or SEC certificate, valid government ID, and\nproof of the business address, such as the signed lease or an appropriate utility\nbill. Confirm the copy requirements and whether two 2 × 2 photos are needed.\nAsk whether a cedula, also called a Community Tax Certificate, applies and\nwhere to obtain it. Confirm the required format and copy count before visiting.\nIf an inspection is needed, arrange premises access and ask how to schedule it.\nOnce the clearance is issued or recorded through the integrated process, keep\nthe evidence with your other registration documents.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou know the correct local route, have met the applicable barangay\nrequirements, and can document completion for the next stage.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm whether barangay clearance is separate or integrated, plus current\ndocuments, inspections, fees, and payment methods.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 29","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02 • CHECKLIST 2.03\n\nDeclare the right activities and\naddress\nDO THIS\n\nMake sure the Mayor’s Permit application accurately describes the business\nactivities you will conduct at the specific address. Review the full business\nmodel before completing the form.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA local business permit generally relates to a location and declared activities. A\nlater move or a new line of business can require an update, amendment, or a\ndifferent application under the LGU’s rules.\nFor example, adding food service to a retail store may introduce requirements\nthe original retail application did not cover. It is easier to identify those\nimplications before launching the extra activity.\nListing activities carelessly can cause problems in either direction: leaving out\nsomething you intend to do, or including speculative activities that bring\nrequirements you did not plan to meet. Ask the BPLO how to describe your\nactual opening model.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nWrite down each activity you intend to conduct now and any near-term addition\nyou are seriously planning. Bring that list to the Business Permits and Licensing\nOffice and confirm what should be declared.\nAsk what each activity changes: clearances, inspections, documentary\nrequirements, and fees. If a planned future activity should be handled later,\nrecord the amendment process and complete it before starting that activity.\nPrepare the applicable Barangay Business Clearance, DTI or SEC certificate,\nlease contract, and cedula if required. Some LGUs also request a simple floor\nplan or premises sketch, so check before the visit.\nConfirm what would be required if you relocated, even nearby. Keep the answer\nwith your permit records instead of assuming the existing permit automatically\nfollows the business.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe application matches the intended address and activities, the supporting\ndocuments are ready, and you understand how to handle a later change.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nAsk the BPLO which activities to declare and how later activity or address\nchanges must be handled.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 30","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02 • CHECKLIST 2.04\n\nMap supporting clearances before\nmaking visits\nDO THIS\n\nGet the BPLO’s complete requirements for your business type and address, then\nturn them into a simple sequence. Mark which steps depend on another\napproval and which can proceed at the same time.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nThe business permit may depend on work by several offices, each with its own\ndocuments, inspections, and schedule. One missing clearance can delay the final\npermit even after you have finished the other steps.\nA map helps you avoid discovering that missing step on the day you expected to\ncollect the permit. It also shows where fit-out readiness, site access, or\nequipment installation affects the timetable.\nUse the local checklist as your starting point. The combination of clearances can\ndiffer by business activity, building, and municipality, and some LGUs provide\nintegrated or online processing.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nBefore filling out forms or moving between offices, request a written checklist\nfrom the BPLO. Ask which requirements involve zoning, BFP, sanitary\ninspection, the Building Official, and, where applicable, an environmental office.\nNext to each requirement, note the documents, submission route, fee, inspection\nneeds, expected lead time, and prerequisite. Identify steps you can start in\nparallel.\nFor example, zoning and sanitary work may be able to progress together,\ndepending on the local process. Ask rather than assuming they will both finish in\none day.\nCoordinate fire-safety requirements with fit-out. Ask BFP when the premises\nmust be accessible and which equipment and layout conditions must be ready\nfor inspection. Build those dependencies into the plan before scheduling the\nvisit.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou have one complete sequence with parallel tasks, dependencies, inspection\nreadiness, and a way to track every required clearance.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the complete local clearance checklist, dependencies, inspection\nreadiness, and current processing times.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 31","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02 • CHECKLIST 2.05\n\nPrepare for the fire-safety inspection\nDO THIS\n\nAsk the local BFP office for the fire-safety requirements that apply to your\nbusiness, occupancy, floor area, and layout. Obtain the right equipment and\nprepare the premises before arranging the inspection.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nRushed purchases can lead to unnecessary expense or equipment that does not\nmeet the actual requirement. A generic equipment list from another business\nmay miss the risks in your own premises.\nThe Fire Safety Inspection Certificate is an important part of business-permit\ncompliance where required. The inspection checks more than whether a fire\nextinguisher is present; the layout and escape routes also matter.\nWritten requirements give you a basis for preparing correctly and questioning\nunexplained charges. They also help your fit-out contractor understand what\nmust remain clear and accessible.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nRequest the applicable BFP checklist and have the office explain the required\nextinguisher type, capacity, number, placement, and servicing or tagging\nrequirements. Follow the requirements for your establishment rather than a\nuniversal square-meter rule.\nCheck exit markings and arrange storage so it does not block escape routes.\nCoordinate these details before the fit-out makes changes difficult.\nBuy compliant equipment from a reputable supplier, such as a hardware store\nable to document the required specifications. Avoid a rushed purchase based\nonly on a vendor’s proximity to the BFP office; check quality and compliance\nbefore paying.\nConfirm inspection readiness and schedule the visit. For government charges,\nrequest the official assessment or Order of Payment and retain the receipt. If a\nrequested purchase or fee is unclear, ask for its basis before paying.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour equipment and premises match the applicable written requirements, the\ninspection is completed, and the required certificate and official payment\nrecords are filed.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nGet BFP requirements for your occupancy, hazards, floor area, and layout, plus\nthe official fee assessment.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 32","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02 • CHECKLIST 2.06\n\nComplete the formal zoning\nrequirement\nDO THIS\n\nUse the location check from Chapter 1 to secure whatever zoning clearance your\npermit application requires. Confirm that the approval covers the exact address\nand business activity, and keep the written result.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA pre-lease conversation and a formal registration clearance may be different\nsteps. Find out what your BPLO will accept so an unresolved zoning issue does\nnot hold up the permit at the final stage.\nA landlord may be unfamiliar with the property’s current classification or with\nthe requirements for your specific activity. A history of commercial tenants is\nuseful evidence to investigate, but it does not replace the LGU’s decision.\nIf you have not signed the lease yet, resolve the issue before making that\ncommitment. If you already have written confirmation, check whether it satisfies\nthe application or whether another document is needed.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nBring the exact address, a clear description of your planned activity, and the\nprospective or signed lease documents requested by the zoning office.\nAsk for written confirmation of the permitted use and the formal clearance\nrequired by the BPLO. Check current fees, documents, processing time, and any\nconditions.\nIf the office identifies a concern, ask whether a valid variance, exemption, or\nreclassification exists for the address. Request evidence and confirm that it\napplies to your proposed activity.\nIf no applicable approval exists, find out the process and implications before\npaying for fit-out. Keep the clearance, conditions, and related written advice in\nthe compliance folder so you can refer to them during inspections or later\nchanges.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe zoning document accepted by the BPLO is ready, matches the business use,\nand has no unresolved condition that would prevent lawful operation.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the zoning approval required by the BPLO and any conditions, fees, or\nadditional steps for the premises.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 33","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02 • CHECKLIST 2.07\n\nPlan around the local renewal\ncalendar\nDO THIS\n\nCheck your LGU’s business-permit renewal calendar before choosing a\nregistration and launch timetable. If you have flexibility, avoid the busiest local\nperiod and use available online or hybrid services.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nAnnual permit renewals can create heavier workloads at the BPLO. A new\napplication filed close to that period may take longer than the same application\nat a quieter time.\nCheck whether your LGU follows a January 1–20 renewal window and how that\naffects new applications. If your launch timing is flexible, consider\nFebruary–October after confirming the local workload and the lead time your\nbusiness needs.\nAlso ask how the permit’s validity and next renewal date apply to a business\nopening late in the year. Registration timing can affect how soon you need to\nbudget for the next renewal.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nAsk your specific BPLO for its current renewal deadline, any announced\nextension, and the process for new applicants during the busy season.\nIf you need to register in November, December, or early January, call or check\nthe official online service before making a trip. Ask whether new applications\nare being processed normally and whether inspections have a separate queue.\nConfirm the first permit’s expiry date and your next renewal obligation. Put that\ndate into the compliance calendar before opening.\nCheck for an online or hybrid application route. Prepare the same complete\nsupporting records you would need for an in-person application, and keep\nacknowledgments so you can follow up without restarting the process.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour launch schedule reflects the actual local workload, permit validity, renewal\ndeadline, and available application route.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nCheck the current local renewal deadline, permit validity, new-application\nworkload, and online options.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 34","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02 • CHECKLIST 2.08\n\nPrepare employer registrations before\nstaff start\nDO THIS\n\nConfirm and complete the applicable employer-registration and\nemployee-reporting steps for SSS, PhilHealth, and Pag-IBIG. Plan this work\nalongside local registration so staff coverage and remittances are ready when\nrequired.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nEmployer compliance should be part of opening preparation, not something left\nuntil the business feels settled. Registration, employee reporting, and\ncontribution payments each need attention.\nFailure to report employees or remit contributions can create arrears, penalties,\nand other employer liabilities. Do not assume that a registration number alone\ncompletes the ongoing obligation.\nSSS states that failure to report or remit does not by itself remove an employee’s\nbenefit entitlement; eligibility and verification rules still apply. Employer\narrears, penalties and possible liabilities remain.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nCheck each agency’s current requirements for your structure and staff. Start the\nsteps that can proceed while the BPLO application is being processed, and\nconfirm any prerequisites instead of assuming they are identical across\nagencies.\nCheck the current SSS R-1 employer-registration and R-1A\nemployment-reporting forms, PhilHealth’s Employer Data Record, and\nPag-IBIG’s employer-registration form. Confirm the latest versions and online\nroutes.\nUse official portals for available steps and complete any required follow-up. Save\nemployer numbers, registration confirmations, employee-reporting records, and\ncontribution instructions in your compliance folder.\nAssign responsibility for remittances and record the deadlines so the work\ncontinues after opening week.\nDONE WHEN\n\nApplicable employer registrations and employee reports are complete, the\nrecords are saved, and someone owns the recurring contribution schedule.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm current SSS, PhilHealth, and Pag-IBIG registration forms,\nemployee-reporting rules, contribution deadlines, and online routes.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 35","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02 • CHECKLIST 2.09\n\nComplete BIR registration before\ncommencing business\nDO THIS\n\nConfirm your BIR registration deadline and complete registration before\ncommencing business. Use the correct Revenue District Office or authorized\nonline route, and prepare the current checklist for your entity type.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nDo not interpret a 30-day reference as permission to sell first and register later.\nBIR’s guidance links commencement to the first sales transaction or the lapse of\n30 calendar days after the earliest relevant registration or local permit\ndocument.\nLate registration can lead to penalties and additional work. Make tax\nregistration part of the opening schedule, with enough time for the invoicing and\nbookkeeping arrangements that follow.\nThe ₱500 annual registration fee was abolished effective January 22, 2024. Do\nnot budget for it as an ongoing annual registration payment; check the current\nassessment for any other applicable charges.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nUse the official BIR service to identify the office or route responsible for your\nbusiness. Confirm the correct place of registration instead of choosing the\nnearest office for convenience.\nCheck the current document list for your application, including whether DTI or\nSEC registration, lease or business-address records, and a valid ID are required.\nConfirm BIR Form 1901 for sole proprietors or Form 1903 for corporations and\nany route-specific attachments.\nAfter registration, check your Certificate of Registration, BIR Form 2303, for\ncorrect details and registered tax obligations. Keep the original safe and comply\nwith current display and posting requirements.\nBefore leaving the registration process, confirm your next steps for invoices,\nbooks, filing access, and deadlines.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour BIR registration is complete on time, the COR details are correct, and you\nunderstand the next invoicing, bookkeeping, and filing requirements.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the exact BIR deadline, correct registration route, current documents,\nand COR posting requirements for your business.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 36","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02 • CHECKLIST 2.10\n\nChoose tax options with a CPA\nDO THIS\n\nBefore completing tax registration and the relevant election, ask a licensed CPA\nto compare the options available to your business. Use projected revenue, costs,\nand structure to guide the decision.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA tax option that is simple to administer may not produce the best result for\nevery business. Your eligibility, cost structure, other income, and expected\ngrowth can change the comparison.\nAsk about the 8% income-tax option for eligible non-VAT individuals, graduated\nincome-tax rates with the Optional Standard Deduction, and VAT registration\nwhere applicable. Eligibility and registration obligations differ, so have the CPA\nexplain which options actually apply.\nThe initial decision also affects recordkeeping and filing. Correcting an\nunsuitable or invalid election later can be difficult, so settle the question before\ntreating registration as complete.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nSchedule a focused consultation and bring your 12-month projections, expected\nsales, costs, business structure, and any other income the CPA needs to assess\neligibility.\nAsk whether the 8% option is available, how its tax base applies to your\ncircumstances, and how it interacts with percentage tax. Compare it with\ngraduated rates and the deduction methods for which you qualify.\nConfirm the current VAT threshold and what happens if growth takes you across\nit. Have the CPA explain when an election must be made, how it is documented,\nand which obligations remain.\nSet aside about an hour for the discussion, or longer if your situation needs it.\nRequest the professional’s current fee in advance and leave with the decision,\nelection deadline, and filing instructions written down.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou understand your chosen tax treatment, have confirmed eligibility and\nelection timing, and know which returns and records the business must\nmaintain.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nAsk your CPA to confirm tax eligibility, the current VAT threshold, election\ntiming, tax base, and required returns.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 37","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02 • CHECKLIST 2.11\n\nArrange compliant invoices before the\nfirst sale\nDO THIS\n\nConfirm how your business will issue compliant invoices and complete the\napplicable BIR requirements before opening. If you will use privately printed\ninvoice booklets, arrange the Authority to Print and printing early.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA Certificate of Registration does not, by itself, put usable sales documents in\nyour hands. Printing, layout checks, or system registration may still need time\nafter the initial registration step.\nUnder the updated invoicing rules, an invoice is the principal sales document.\nConfirm the current required details before commissioning a layout; another\nbusiness’s older Official Receipt booklet may no longer be an appropriate model\nto copy.\nYour route may involve BIR-printed invoices, an accredited printer, or an\napproved or registered system, depending on the applicable rules. Confirm the\nroute for your business rather than assuming every applicant must follow one\nidentical process.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nDiscuss invoicing during BIR registration so it does not become a last-minute\nopening problem. If an Authority to Print is required, verify the current use of\nBIR Form 1906 and the supporting documents.\nFor printed booklets, have the proposed invoice layout and COR details checked,\nthen work with a BIR-accredited printer. Confirm both the agency’s processing\ntime and the printer’s delivery date.\nAllow for both approval and production time. For example, a\nfive-to-ten-working-day ATP estimate plus printing could justify setting aside at\nleast three weeks. Confirm your actual route and lead times before relying on\nthat allowance.\nBuild a buffer into the launch plan. Check the delivered documents and train the\nperson making sales before the first customer transaction.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe correct invoices or compliant invoicing system are ready for use, required\nauthorizations are complete, and the sales team knows the process.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm current invoice details, ATP or system requirements, and actual\napproval and production lead times.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 38","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02 • CHECKLIST 2.12\n\nSet up and register the books of\naccounts\nDO THIS\n\nChoose a suitable bookkeeping method with your CPA and complete the\napplicable registration requirements on time. Prepare the records before\ntransactions begin so you can capture the business accurately from opening day.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nBuying a ledger or subscribing to software does not settle its tax-registration\nrequirements. Manual, loose-leaf, and computerized records can have different\nprocedures and deadlines.\nIf you discover a missing requirement months later, correcting the records may\ntake time and professional help. It is easier to establish the method,\nresponsibility, and registration evidence while the opening records are still\nmanageable.\nRegistration procedures and deadlines differ by taxpayer status and record type.\nConfirm the current BIR process, including ORUS where applicable, instead of\nassuming an in-person stamping visit is the only route or that every applicant\nhas the same deadline.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nDiscuss whether your records should include a Cash Receipts Journal, Cash\nDisbursements Journal, General Ledger, and General Journal. Have the CPA\ndetermine the complete and appropriate set for your activities and show you\nhow entries should be recorded.\nPrepare the COR and the books or information required by the current\nregistration route. If using manual ledgers, confirm the process for new\nregistrants rather than assuming it matches the procedure for replacement\nbooks.\nCoordinate this work with invoicing preparation to reduce separate visits or\nsubmissions. Save the registration confirmation with the books and compliance\nrecords.\nIf growth may take you toward the VAT threshold within the first two years,\ndiscuss whether loose-leaf or computerized accounting is more practical. Allow\nfor any additional registration, authority, or system requirements.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour bookkeeping method, required records, registration deadline, and\nresponsible person are clear, and the applicable registration evidence is saved.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the appropriate books, BIR or ORUS registration route, deadlines, and\nany additional system requirements.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 39","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02 • CHECKLIST 2.13\n\nCheck industry-specific permits\nDO THIS\n\nAsk for the requirements for your exact business activity before starting\nregistration. Add applicable industry licenses, testing, inspections, and product\napprovals to your opening plan rather than stopping at the standard local and\ntax registrations.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nThe requirements for a food manufacturer, water refilling station, salon, and\npharmacy are not identical. Even within one industry, a different activity or\nproduct can change which agency has authority.\nFinding a missing license during an inspection is more disruptive than\nidentifying it during planning. Some approvals may also depend on premises,\nqualified personnel, product details, or testing that takes time to arrange.\nUse a sector checklist as a starting point and confirm requirements directly with\nthe responsible agency. A broad business label is not always enough to\ndetermine the full set.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nVisit or contact the nearest DTI Negosyo Center and ask for guidance on the\nindustry-specific checklist for your proposed activity. Confirm the information\nwith the relevant agencies and LGU.\nFor food manufacturing, processing, or repacking, check whether FDA licensing\nand product authorization apply. For water refilling, check local health and\nsanitary requirements and the required water-quality testing.\nFor salons and barbershops, ask which local, health, training, and personnel\nrequirements apply to the actual services offered. Verify any claimed\nprofessional-licensing requirement with the responsible authority rather than\nassuming all personal-care services follow the same rules.\nFor pharmacies, verify FDA requirements and whether PDEA requirements\napply to the products or activities involved. Record the exact approval, issuing\nagency, documents, lead time, and renewal responsibility in your registration\nmap.\nDONE WHEN\n\nEach business activity has been checked for sector-specific requirements, and all\napplicable approvals and testing are included in the launch schedule.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm each activity’s licenses, testing, personnel requirements, and product\napprovals with the responsible agency and LGU.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 40","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02 • CHECKLIST 2.14\n\nPrepare one master document folder\nDO THIS\n\nAssemble a master registration folder before the first agency visit. Organize it\nby agency, keep originals protected, and use the current checklists to confirm\nthe exact copies and formats needed.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nThe same core documents may appear in several applications, but the accepted\nformat can vary: ordinary photocopy, certified copy, notarized copy, or an\noriginal shown for verification.\nArriving with the right document in the wrong format can still mean another\ntrip. Preparing the folder once reduces repeated searching, photocopying, and\nlast-minute notarization.\nThe folder should be a practical kit, not a reason to produce unnecessary copies\nor carry more cash than you need. Use current requirements to decide what to\nbring and which submissions can be handled digitally.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nA suggested preparation kit is ten front-and-back copies of a valid government\nID; five copies each of the DTI or SEC certificate, signed lease, and cedula; and\nfive 2 × 2 ID photos. Adjust these quantities to the actual agency checklists.\nKeep originals in a separate protective sleeve. Present them when required and\nclarify whether an office needs to retain an original, a certified copy, or an\nordinary copy. Keep a record of anything surrendered.\nLabel sections by agency and include the relevant forms and checklists. Prepare\ndigital copies in the format requested by online services.\nCheck current fees and accepted payment channels, then carry only the cash\nneeded for confirmed cash payments. Keep the remaining registration budget\navailable through the accepted channels and retain proof of each payment.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou can quickly find each required document, copy, form, and payment\ninstruction, with originals protected and agency-specific requirements checked.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nMatch copies, original-document handling, fees, and payment channels to the\ncurrent checklist for each agency.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 41","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02 • CHECKLIST 2.15\n\nMake compliance a condition of\nopening\nDO THIS\n\nSet the opening date around completion of the approvals and operating\ndocuments your business needs. Treat a soft opening that serves paying\ncustomers as part of that readiness decision.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nCalling the period a “soft opening” should not be treated as a general exemption\nfrom business, fire-safety, tax, or industry requirements. Check the obligations\nthat apply before serving customers.\nAn unfinished application can leave the business exposed to findings, penalties,\nor interruption while rent, staffing, and other costs continue. A delayed approval\nneeds an explicit plan, not a different label on the storefront.\nThe practical goal is to avoid launching with a known compliance gap. Your\nspecific agencies can confirm whether any documented provisional authorization\nexists and exactly what it permits.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nWork backward from your target opening date. Use the current agency\ntimelines, inspection needs, printing lead times, and correction allowances from\nyour registration map.\nFor every required approval, record the evidence of completion and any\nconditions still to satisfy. A submitted application is not automatically an\nauthorization to operate.\nCheck that the required certificates, invoices, books, and employer\narrangements are ready for the activities you will actually conduct. Make this a\nfinal readiness check before inviting paying customers.\nIf a requirement is delayed and you have no valid authorization covering\noperation, move the opening date. Compare the immediate revenue delay with\nthe possible disruption and extra costs of an enforcement problem during the\nfirst weeks of business.\nDONE WHEN\n\nAll applicable pre-operation requirements are completed or covered by a verified\nauthorization, and the business can document its readiness before its first sale.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm that every required pre-operation approval is complete and that any\nprovisional authorization covers the planned activity.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 42","text":"CHAPTER 02 / NAVIGATE BUSINESS REGISTRATION\n\nCHAPTER 02 • CHECKLIST 2.16\n\nKeep proof of every registration\npayment\nDO THIS\n\nCollect the official receipt or payment confirmation for every government fee.\nFile it immediately and maintain a payment log that your accountant can use to\nreconcile registration costs.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nSeveral small payments across different agencies are easy to lose track of.\nWithout the supporting record, it can be difficult to confirm what was paid,\nresolve a discrepancy, or explain an opening cost later.\nPayment evidence also helps your accountant determine the appropriate\ntreatment of each expense. A receipt does not automatically make every amount\nseparately deductible under every tax method, so let the tax treatment follow\nthe rules applicable to your business.\nRegistration costs depend on the LGU, declared capitalization, business type,\nand required approvals. Build the budget from current official assessments, then\ncompare actual payments with it. Investigate unexplained differences while the\npayment details are still easy to trace.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nBefore leaving a payment counter, ask for the official receipt or accepted\npayment confirmation and check that the details are correct. For online\npayments, save the confirmation and associated assessment or reference\nnumber.\nKeep the records in the master compliance folder. Log five details in a simple\nspreadsheet: agency, payment date, amount, purpose, and receipt or\nconfirmation number.\nIf a charge is unclear, request the official assessment or Order of Payment\nrather than relying on an unexplained verbal amount. Match the payment to the\nservice or requirement it covers.\nContinue this habit after opening. The log becomes a useful running record for\nrenewals, accounting, and questions about prior payments.\nDONE WHEN\n\nEvery payment is supported by an official record, matched to a clear purpose,\nand entered in the log for accounting review.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nCheck official assessments and payment records, then ask your accountant how\neach cost should be treated.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 43","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03\n\nBefore You Rent\n\nThe commercial space and fit-out checklist\n\nA space can feel right while its contract puts your business under pressure. A\nmaintenance fee, an electrical upgrade, or a fit-out running three weeks late can\nconsume money meant to carry your first three months of operations. Find these\ncosts before you commit.\nYour goal is a space you can use legally, safely, and within budget. Read the\nlease, price the full occupancy cost, check the building, and put negotiated\ncommitments in writing. You can ask about every term, although the landlord\nmay not agree to change it.\nCommercial leasing practices vary across Metro Manila, secondary cities, and\nprovincial municipalities. This is practical preparation, not legal advice. Have a\nPhilippine lawyer review your actual lease and its attachments before signing.\nThe rent start date, your obligations, and your exit options depend on the\nagreement and applicable law.\n\nStart here\n• Get the complete draft lease, all house rules, and a written schedule of charges.\n• Check the building documents and technical capacity before paying for a\ndesign.\n• Bring the full cost model and unresolved clauses to a lawyer before signing.\nBEFORE YOU MOVE ON\n\n• I know the total monthly occupancy cost and the rent for every lease year.\n• The intended use, fit-out, utilities, and signage have been checked with the\nrelevant professionals and offices.\n• The final lease records concessions, improvements, renewal, and exit\narrangements.\n• A lawyer has reviewed the final documents, and the possession-day condition\nreport is planned.","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 44","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03 / YOUR CHECKLIST\n\nMark your progress\nCheck an item only when the work is complete and the evidence is saved.\n☐ 3.01 Read the whole lease twice\n☐ 3.02 Get every monthly charge in writing\n☐ 3.03 Define the rent-free fit-out period\n☐ 3.04 Agree who keeps the improvements\n☐ 3.05 Require compliant rental documentation\n☐ 3.06 Understand utility billing before signing\n☐ 3.07 Match electrical capacity to your equipment\n☐ 3.08 Check the building’s occupancy approval\n☐ 3.09 Make rent increases predictable\n☐ 3.10 Price your exit before entering\n☐ 3.11 Record the space before work starts\n☐ 3.12 Turn promises into written terms\n☐ 3.13 Check the space before designing it\n☐ 3.14 Pay for fit-out in checked milestones\n☐ 3.15 Confirm permits before construction\n☐ 3.16 Confirm signage rules before design\n\nKeep in mind\nTreat the lease as part of your financial model. Base rent, shared charges,\nescalation, fit-out costs, and exit costs all compete for the same capital.\nYour strongest opportunity to clarify and negotiate is usually before you sign.\nAsk for the rent-free period, predictable charges, workable termination terms,\nand clear ownership of improvements. Keep every agreed concession in the final\ndocuments.\nRead every clause. Price every obligation. Record every commitment. Then let\nyour lawyer review the final version. The space should give your business room\nto operate without creating costs you never planned to carry.","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 45","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03 • CHECKLIST 3.01\n\nRead the whole lease twice\nDO THIS\n\nGet the complete lease before negotiating individual clauses. Print it or use a\ncopy you can mark up. Include schedules, house rules, and any document the\nlease says forms part of your agreement.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nExcitement can make an expensive obligation look like routine paperwork. The\ndefinitions, termination, and renewal sections can change what you pay or how\nlong you can stay. Understanding the whole agreement helps you see how one\nclause affects another.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nOn your first reading, mark every financial, operational, and conduct obligation.\nList anything unclear. Check whether the definition of gross sales affects\npercentage rent or other charges, and whether the landlord can end the lease\nfor conditions you might overlook.\nCheck renewal carefully. Is renewal an option you can exercise, something both\nparties must agree to, or entirely discretionary? What notice is required? How\nwill the next rent be determined? Do not assume that a successful first term\nguarantees another.\nOn your second reading, ask of each clause: “What is the worst reasonable\ninterpretation, and could the business absorb it?” Bring the marked copy and\nyour questions to a lawyer. Ask for unclear language to be rewritten before you\nsign.\nRead the final version again after negotiations. A discussed concession is useful\nonly if the agreed wording survives into the documents you actually sign. Do not\nlet a familiar-looking revised draft replace that last check.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou understand every obligation, have resolved material ambiguities in writing,\nand can afford the consequences of the final terms. Your lawyer has checked the\nclauses you flagged.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nHave a Philippine lawyer review unclear language and the final lease before\nsigning.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 46","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03 • CHECKLIST 3.02\n\nGet every monthly charge in writing\nDO THIS\n\nAsk the landlord for the complete monthly bill before signing. Specifically ask:\n“Aside from base rent, what other charges will appear on my billing statement?”\nGet the answer as a written breakdown.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nCUSA, often called common-use or common-area service charges, can cover\nshared security, hallway maintenance, common utilities, elevators, and upkeep.\nSimilar costs may appear as maintenance fees, association dues, building fees,\nor common-area contributions. The name matters less than what you must pay.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nFor each charge, identify what service it covers and how it is calculated. Is it a\nfixed monthly amount, a rate per square metre, or your share of actual building\nexpenses? Ask when it can change and how any increase will be communicated.\nBe especially careful with uncapped charges based on actual costs. You need a\nway to estimate your monthly exposure. Request a fixed amount or a defined\nmaximum per square metre, and check whether taxes or other components sit\noutside that cap.\nPut the agreed computation and any cap in the lease or a signed addendum\nbefore signing the main contract. Use the full monthly total in your budget, not\nthe attractive base-rent figure shown in the advertisement.\nIf a new charge appears later, compare it with the signed lease and attachments,\nrequest its written basis, and seek advice before accepting or disputing it. Do\nnot assume that an unfamiliar charge is automatically payable or automatically\ninvalid.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour lease file contains the full recurring-charge schedule, each computation\nmethod, and any negotiated cap. Your occupancy budget uses the all-in total.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nHave your lawyer check all charge provisions, including schedules, incorporated\nrules, and separate agreements.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 47","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03 • CHECKLIST 3.03\n\nDefine the rent-free fit-out period\nDO THIS\n\nRequest a rent-free fit-out period before agreeing to the space. Fit-out covers\nthe work between taking possession and opening: renovation, equipment\ninstallation, counters, and the other preparations needed for operations.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nYou may be paying rent while the business earns nothing. At Php 30,000 a\nmonth, a 30-day fit-out at full rent consumes Php 30,000 before your first\ncustomer. At Php 50,000 a month, the same period consumes Php 50,000.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nPrepare a realistic construction and installation schedule, then ask for 30 to 60\nrent-free days if that is what the work needs. A shorter 15-to-30-day period may\nbe a negotiation outcome, but neither range is a guaranteed entitlement or a\npromise of local market practice.\nTry a specific request: “Given the fit-out and installation work required, we\nwould like a 30-day rent-free construction period before rent begins.” Raise it\nearly, while you still have alternatives, and show how the requested period\nconnects to the work.\nWrite the exact start date, end date, possession date, and rent commencement\ndate into the agreement. Check whether “rent-free” means base rent only or\nalso includes maintenance and other shared charges. Budget utilities and other\ncosts that remain payable.\nBuild the concession into your opening cash plan. A free month does not remove\nthe need to manage contractor delays or permit lead times. If the schedule slips,\nknow when paid rent begins rather than assuming the landlord will extend the\nconcession.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe signed lease states the concession’s exact dates and coverage. Your fit-out\nschedule and cash budget use those dates, including costs payable before\nopening.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 48","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03 • CHECKLIST 3.04\n\nAgree who keeps the improvements\nDO THIS\n\nBefore spending on fit-out, agree which improvements you may remove, which\nwill stay, and what restoration you must complete when the lease ends. Record\nthose arrangements item by item.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA Php 200,000-to-Php 500,000 fit-out can include counters, floor tiles, partitions,\nwiring, and built-in fixtures. Paying for that work does not, by itself, settle what\nyou can recover or take away when you close, relocate, or reach lease expiry.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nMake an inventory of planned improvements and equipment before signing.\nSeparate freestanding equipment, shelves, display fixtures, and unattached\ncounters from items integrated into the building. The distinction should be\ndocumented rather than left to an argument on moving day.\nFor tiling, electrical-panel upgrades, built-in cabinetry, and ceiling work,\nnegotiate a specific treatment. One option is removal with an agreed restoration\nobligation. Another is leaving the improvement in place in exchange for a\nconcession, such as lower rent, a longer rent-free period, or a negotiated\nrenewal right.\nClarify what happens on ordinary expiry, agreed early termination, and other\ntermination scenarios. Ask your lawyer to check the wording against the actual\nwork and applicable rules. Do not assume every item has the same treatment\nmerely because the contract calls all of it “improvements.”\nInclude the likely unrecoverable portion of fit-out in your business decision. If\nyou must leave expensive work behind, your expected trading period needs to\njustify that cost. A useful concession should appear in the written agreement\nalongside the obligation it offsets.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe lease identifies removable and retained items, restoration duties, and any\nmatching concessions. You have budgeted what cannot reasonably be recovered\nat exit.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nHave a Philippine lawyer check the improvements clause, including applicable\nreimbursement, removal, and restoration rights.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 49","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03 • CHECKLIST 3.05\n\nRequire compliant rental\ndocumentation\nDO THIS\n\nBefore signing, ask what tax-compliant document the landlord will issue for\nevery rental payment. Have your CPA confirm the currently required document\nand the records your business needs to retain.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nRent is a real expense, but a handwritten acknowledgment or bank transfer\nalone may not meet every tax-documentation requirement. Missing documents\ncan make accounting, deduction support, and an audit more difficult. Deal with\nthis before it becomes a monthly problem.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nAsk directly: “Can you provide the BIR-compliant invoice or other document\ncurrently required for each rental payment?” If possible, let your accountant\nreview a sample. An unclear answer is a reason to investigate further; it is not\nproof of the landlord’s tax conduct.\nNegotiate a lease clause specifying the required documentation, when it will be\nissued, and the agreed remedy if it is not provided. Ask your lawyer whether that\nremedy is workable, and your CPA whether the document meets the relevant\nrequirements.\nKeep a clear payment trail. Where you pay by bank transfer, use a reference\nsuch as “Rent payment for [month], [address]” and retain the transfer\nconfirmation with the lease and billing documents. This supports the records but\nshould not be treated as an automatic substitute for required tax documentation.\nIf the landlord cannot provide compliant records, have your accountant explain\nthe effect before you commit. Your options may include securing a documented\ncompliance arrangement or choosing a landlord who already supplies the\ncorrect records. Include the documentation risk in the decision.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour CPA has confirmed the documentation requirements, the landlord’s written\ncommitment is in place, and you have a filing process for every rental bill and\npayment.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nAsk your CPA to confirm current BIR rental-invoicing and\nexpense-documentation requirements before you sign.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 50","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03 • CHECKLIST 3.06\n\nUnderstand utility billing before\nsigning\nDO THIS\n\nFind out whether electricity comes through your own utility account or the\nlandlord’s sub-meter. Get the quoted rate per kilowatt-hour, the full calculation,\nand any additional charges in writing before committing.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nIn a shared-meter building, the landlord may divide the main bill among tenants.\nA flat fee or unexplained rate can hide costs you have not included in your\nmodel. You need enough detail to compare the arrangement and check future\nbills.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nAsk: “Is electricity billed through a sub-meter or a direct utility connection, and\nwhat rate will I pay?” Request an explanation of meter readings, shared\nconsumption, adjustments, and any separately charged service or administration\ncomponent.\nCompare the proposed calculation with the current published information from\nthe local electric utility or cooperative. Compare equivalent charges rather than\nassuming one headline rate covers the whole bill. Ask the landlord to explain\ndifferences and show the basis for them.\nIf the arrangement remains unclear or too costly, explore a direct connection\nwith the landlord and utility before signing. Feasibility, approvals, installation\ncosts, and timing need checking. Do not assume a ground-floor unit\nautomatically qualifies for a separate connection.\nCheck water billing too, especially for food service, laundry, water refilling, and\nsalons. Record whether water is sub-metered, the rate or allocation method, and\nthe expected monthly cost. Put both electricity and water into your operating\nmodel before deciding the rent is affordable.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou can explain how both utility bills will be calculated, have checked the\napplicable rules and connection options, and have included realistic utility costs\nin your budget.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the applicable electricity-billing rules and direct-connection\nrequirements with the local utility and relevant regulator.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 51","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03 • CHECKLIST 3.07\n\nMatch electrical capacity to your\nequipment\nDO THIS\n\nList the equipment you expect to run together at peak demand and have a\nqualified electrical professional check whether the unit can safely support it. Do\nthis before signing the lease.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nPrior commercial use does not mean the space suits your operation. A milk tea\nshop may run blenders, a refrigerator, an ice machine, a water heater, and a\ndisplay cooler together. A unit previously used for light retail may need\nsubstantial electrical work.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nRecord each appliance’s nameplate information, including its amperage or\npower rating. Identify which equipment runs simultaneously. This gives the\nassessor a useful starting list; a simple owner-made total is not a substitute for a\nproper electrical assessment.\nAsk the landlord for the current panel capacity and any electrical plan for the\nunit. Have the professional assess the panel, breakers, wiring, and equipment\nrequirements together. Tripped breakers, blown fuses, and overheating are\nproblems to prevent, not discoveries to make after installation.\nIf an upgrade is needed, establish its scope, cost, required approvals, and\nrealistic completion date. Agree whether the landlord or tenant pays. Put the\nresponsibility and timing in writing before you commit to the space or a rent\nstart date.\nCarry the upgrade into your fit-out budget and opening schedule. A short initial\nassessment might take under an hour, but the actual time depends on the\nbuilding and available plans. Confirm the scope and allow further investigation\nwhere the assessor cannot verify capacity from a quick visit.\nDONE WHEN\n\nA qualified professional has confirmed a safe plan for your equipment. Any\nrequired upgrade has a written scope, payer, approvals pathway, budget, and\ncompletion date.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the assessor’s qualifications and the permits or approvals required for\nthe proposed electrical work.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 52","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03 • CHECKLIST 3.08\n\nCheck the building’s occupancy\napproval\nDO THIS\n\nRequest a copy of the building’s Certificate of Occupancy before signing.\nConfirm that the document and approved use cover the specific unit and the\ntype of business you intend to operate.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA building may exist, have other tenants, and still raise questions about its\napproved use or documentation. You need to understand those issues before\ninvesting in fit-out. Missing or unsuitable approvals can affect your opening\nplans and may also affect insurance.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nCompare the certificate with the address, building, and space you are\nconsidering. Describe your actual operation when asking about use: the answer\nfor a small shop may differ from the answer for a kitchen, laundry, or other\nactivity with different technical needs.\nIf the landlord cannot produce the document, ask the LGU’s Office of the\nBuilding Official how to verify the property’s status. Follow its access and\ndocument-request process. Ask what approval or correction would be required\nfor your intended use.\nGet qualified advice on unresolved issues before committing. An older building’s\nhistory of informal operation does not establish that your business can lawfully\nopen there. Do not treat a verbal assurance that “everyone operates this way” as\nclearance.\nAsk your insurer or insurance adviser how the documented building status\naffects the cover you need. Coverage and claim decisions depend on the policy\nand circumstances; avoid assuming either that all cover disappears or that the\nissue has no effect.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe relevant office has confirmed the approval position for your intended use,\nunresolved issues have a documented path to resolution, and you understand\nany insurance implications.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the property’s approved use with the Building Official and discuss any\nunresolved status issue with your lawyer and insurer.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 53","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03 • CHECKLIST 3.09\n\nMake rent increases predictable\nDO THIS\n\nNegotiate an explicit escalation rate or a clearly defined ceiling. Then model the\nrent for every year of the lease, rather than deciding affordability from the\nstarting rent alone.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nAt a 10% annual increase, Php 25,000 monthly rent becomes Php 27,500 in Year\n2, Php 30,250 in Year 3, and Php 33,275 in Year 4. A manageable first year can\nbecome difficult if sales or margins do not keep pace.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nCheck when escalation begins, how often it applies, and whether it compounds\non the previous rent. Separate increases within the lease term from the method\nused to set rent on renewal. Wording tied to market rates or landlord discretion\nneeds careful review.\nUse 5% or lower as a negotiation target if it suits your local market and business\nmodel, not as a legal entitlement. Ask for the agreed percentage to appear\nplainly in the contract, with the dates or anniversaries on which it applies.\nIf a market-based mechanism is proposed, ask for an objective reference and a\nmaximum increase. One negotiating option is the lower of an agreed percentage\nand a specified regional Consumer Price Index measure. Have the formula,\nreference period, and treatment of unusual outcomes checked before accepting\nit.\nEnter each year’s rent into your financial model. If Year 3 breaks the business’s\nbreak-even assumptions, revisit the price, term, or location before signing. Keep\nthe arithmetic visible so you can distinguish an annual rent increase from the\ntotal extra rent across several years.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe lease has a clear escalation mechanism, and your forecast shows the actual\nrent for each year and any separate renewal assumptions.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nCheck all rent calculations. In this example, Years 1–3 total Php 993,000, which\nis Php 93,000 above three years at the unchanged starting rent.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 54","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03 • CHECKLIST 3.10\n\nPrice your exit before entering\nDO THIS\n\nRead and negotiate early-termination terms before signing. Know the notice\nrequired, the penalty, the treatment of the deposit, and any other costs the\nbusiness would face if it had to leave early.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nMarkets, health, and personal circumstances can change. A contract may\ncombine lost deposits with several months of rent as an early-exit charge. For\nplanning, test what a two-to-six-month penalty would cost, then use the actual\nlease terms in your decision.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nAsk for a manageable notice-and-penalty arrangement. One negotiating proposal\nis 60 days’ written notice, forfeiture of one month’s security deposit as the only\npenalty, and no rent obligation beyond the notice period. The landlord may\npropose different terms; model the total cost of each.\nMake sure the clause applies to the intended situation. Early termination and\nnormal expiry should not be confused. Ask your lawyer to clarify triggers, notice\ndelivery, final rent, deposits, and any restoration duties that may remain.\nDiscuss disruption from government-mandated closures and other major events.\nThe closures of 2020 and 2021 are a useful reason to ask what happens to rent,\naccess, and termination if the business cannot legally operate. A force majeure\nlabel alone does not explain those consequences.\nGet the agreed protections into the written lease. Keep an exit-cost figure in\nyour financial planning so that closing an unviable operation does not create a\nsecond surprise. Clear terms are useful even when you fully expect the business\nto succeed.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou can calculate the cost of an early exit, distinguish it from normal expiry, and\nexplain how the contract handles major operating disruptions.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nHave a Philippine lawyer check the actual early-termination and disruption\nclauses, including rent obligations during a mandated closure.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 55","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03 • CHECKLIST 3.11\n\nRecord the space before work starts\nDO THIS\n\nInspect and document the unit on possession day before workers or materials\nenter. Arrange the walkthrough with the landlord or an authorized\nrepresentative so both sides can acknowledge its starting condition.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nAt the end of a lease, you may need evidence that a crack, stain, damaged\nfitting, or worn surface was already there. A shared condition record makes\nrestoration discussions clearer and can help protect your deposit from disputed\nrepair charges.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nPhotograph every wall surface, floor section, ceiling panel, existing electrical\noutlet and fixture, and plumbing point. Take wider views to show locations, then\nclose-ups of cracks, stains, and damage. Use dated or timestamped photographs\nand retain the original files.\nCompile the pictures into one dated condition report. Describe any defect that\nmay not be obvious from a photograph, and identify the relevant part of the unit.\nKeep the record factual rather than relying on a general statement that the\nspace is “in good condition.”\nPrepare two copies and have both parties sign and date each. Attach a signed\ncopy to each party’s lease set as an exhibit. If the representative cannot sign on\nthe day, resolve acknowledgment before work changes the condition you are\ntrying to record.\nAllow around two hours for the walkthrough and documentation as an initial\nplanning estimate; a larger or damaged unit may need longer. File the signed\nreport and original photos where you can retrieve them when negotiating\nrepairs or preparing to leave.\nDONE WHEN\n\nBoth parties hold a signed, dated condition report with clear photographs, and\nyou have retained the original image files before any fit-out work begins.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 56","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03 • CHECKLIST 3.12\n\nTurn promises into written terms\nDO THIS\n\nKeep a record of every commitment discussed with the landlord, then include\nthe agreed commitments in the lease or signed addendum before signing. Do not\nbuild your opening plan around an undocumented assurance.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA free month, a repaired ceiling, permission for a particular sign, or a promise\nabout a neighboring competitor can affect your decision to rent. If each side\nremembers the conversation differently, the business faces uncertainty at\nprecisely the wrong time.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nAfter each negotiation conversation, send a written summary by SMS or email\nwithin 24 hours: “Just to confirm what we agreed today…” List each point\nseparately, including dates, amounts, scope, and responsibility where relevant.\nAsk the landlord to confirm or correct the list.\nKeep the response with the lease records. This creates a useful record of what\nwas discussed, but do not stop there. Convert each agreed point into a specific\nclause or signed attachment before the final contract is executed.\nCheck the wording against the practical result you need. “Repair the ceiling”\nshould not leave the location, responsibility, or completion timing unclear. A\nsignage permission should match the sign you intend to install and the separate\napprovals it may need.\nIf the landlord will say something but will not record it, leave it out of your\nfinancial assumptions and reconsider how much your decision depends on it. Ask\nyour lawyer about the legal effect of existing communications rather than\nassuming all verbal promises are either enforceable or meaningless.\nDONE WHEN\n\nEvery material concession used in your decision appears in the final signed\ndocuments, and your supporting negotiation messages are saved together.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nHave your lawyer check that every material promise is properly reflected in the\nfinal documents.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 57","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03 • CHECKLIST 3.13\n\nCheck the space before designing it\nDO THIS\n\nArrange a technical assessment of the raw space before commissioning the\nlayout or ordering custom fixtures. Use appropriately qualified professionals for\nstructural, electrical, plumbing, and other specialist questions.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nYour ideal design must fit the building’s limits. Heavy equipment may exceed a\nfloor’s capacity. Low ceilings can restrict exhaust or air-conditioning work.\nMoving drains or altering load-bearing walls can add cost, approvals, and delay\nafter design money has already been spent.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nAsk for the floor’s load capacity and any restrictions on equipment placement.\nIdentify load-bearing walls and other elements that cannot simply be removed.\nHave structural questions assessed by a qualified structural professional rather\nthan accepting a contractor’s casual assurance.\nMeasure the actual ceiling height in every relevant section. Locate drains and\ncheck their capacity and condition. Review the electrical panel’s condition and\ncapacity alongside the equipment list from Item 7. Give the assessor the actual\nbusiness plan and intended equipment layout.\nFor initial budgeting, a one-to-two-hour visit at Php 1,500 to Php 3,000 is only an\nillustrative allowance. Obtain local quotations and a clear scope before relying\non it. Neither that budget nor a short visit guarantees that every technical issue\ncan be settled.\nGive the resulting constraints to your designer before the first draft. Resolve\nmajor feasibility questions before paying for custom cabinetry or committing to\nequipment positions. It is much easier to change a drawing than to rebuild\ninstalled work while rent is running.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour designer has a documented set of verified site constraints, and any major\nstructural or service changes have a feasible scope, cost allowance, and\napproval path.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the professional’s credentials, assessment scope, current fee, and any\nrequired engineering approvals.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 58","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03 • CHECKLIST 3.14\n\nPay for fit-out in checked milestones\nDO THIS\n\nAgree a written milestone-payment schedule with the contractor before work\nbegins. Tie progress payments to defined completed work and inspection, and\nretain a final amount until defects on the punch list are resolved.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA contractor may request 50% or more upfront. Large advance payments put\nmore of your money at risk before you have seen the work. A staged schedule\ngives both sides clearer expectations and keeps the remaining payments linked\nto actual progress.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nUse this illustrative structure as a starting point: 30% on contract signing and\nmobilization; 40% after completion and inspection of major structural, electrical,\nand tiling work; 20% at substantial completion of visible and functional work;\nand the final 10% after walkthrough, punch-list completion, and sign-off.\nDefine what each milestone includes. Broad labels such as “half finished” can\ncreate arguments. Specify the work you expect to see, the inspection or\nchecking process, and who confirms completion before the payment becomes\ndue.\nWrite the final-retention arrangement into the contract. A punch list is the list of\nincomplete or defective items to resolve. Agree how it will be recorded and\nchecked so the final walkthrough becomes a practical acceptance process rather\nthan a rushed request for the balance.\nCompare the payment schedule with your cash plan and rent commencement\ndate. Contractor payments, equipment purchases, and opening reserves must fit\ntogether. Staged payments do not guarantee timely or good work, so keep\nchecking progress and workmanship throughout the project.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe signed fit-out contract states the payment amounts, measurable milestones,\ninspection process, final punch-list requirement, and conditions for releasing the\nlast 10% or other agreed retention.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 59","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03 • CHECKLIST 3.15\n\nConfirm permits before construction\nDO THIS\n\nTake your proposed fit-out to the LGU’s Office of the Building Official and ask\nwhich permits and supporting plans it requires. Make this check before\nconstruction, not after a contractor has started work.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nCalling work a “minor renovation” does not settle its permit requirements.\nStructural changes, electrical work, plumbing, and changes affecting fire exits\ndeserve particular attention. Unresolved approvals can lead to failed\ninspections, remedial work, or a stop-work problem while rent continues.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nDescribe the actual scope and bring a sketch or proposed plan. If you are\nalready visiting the LGU to verify zoning or the building’s status, use that visit to\nask about the fit-out as well. Get the relevant office’s requirements rather than\nrelying only on the contractor’s view.\nFor work requiring permits, identify the plans, signatures, professional\ncredentials, and supporting documents needed. Ask which appropriately\nlicensed professionals must prepare or sign each plan. Do not assume any\ncontractor or draftsman can meet every submission requirement.\nApply and obtain the necessary approvals before the regulated work begins.\nTreat two to four weeks as a tentative planning allowance, then verify current\nlocal lead times and allow for incomplete submissions or revisions. Put that time\ninto the fit-out schedule and rent negotiation.\nKeep approved plans and permits with the project documents, and make sure\nthe contractor understands the authorized scope. If the design changes, check\nwhether the approval must change too. Treat compliance as part of the project\nrather than a task to squeeze in before opening.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe relevant offices have confirmed the requirements, necessary approvals are\nin place, and the contractor’s work schedule follows the approved scope and\ntiming.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm current fit-out permits, qualified plan signatories, fire-safety\ncoordination, and processing times with the relevant local offices.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 60","text":"CHAPTER 03 / BEFORE YOU RENT\n\nCHAPTER 03 • CHECKLIST 3.16\n\nConfirm signage rules before design\nDO THIS\n\nGet the landlord’s written signage rules and check the LGU’s permit\nrequirements before commissioning or installing your storefront sign. Give the\nconfirmed limits to the designer from the beginning.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nFascia boards, hanging signs, window graphics, and illuminated lettering can be\nimportant for walk-in customers. A sign that exceeds the allowed size, uses an\nunapproved location, or lacks a required permit may have to be altered or\nremoved at your expense.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nAsk the landlord for the maximum dimensions and permitted locations for\nexterior signage on your unit. Check restrictions on materials, illumination,\nmounting, and other design features that affect the sign you plan to use.\nConfirm whether the landlord requires design review before installation. Ask\nwhat must be submitted, who approves it, and how the approval is recorded.\nKeep the approved design with the written permission so the installer works\nfrom the same version.\nAgree who removes the sign and restores the facade at lease end. Include this\nresponsibility in the lease or signage permission rather than leaving it as an\nexit-day discussion. Build any expected removal cost into your plan.\nAsk the LGU’s Business Permits or Engineering Office which office handles your\nsign type and size. Confirm whether a separate permit is needed, the current\nfee, required documents, and processing time. Only then finalize the design and\ninstallation schedule; landlord approval and government approval may be\nseparate steps.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe final design fits the landlord’s written limits, all required approvals are\naccounted for, and installation, fees, removal, and facade restoration are\nincluded in the plan.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the responsible office, current signage rules, fees, and processing time\nfor your exact location and sign.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 61","text":"CHAPTER 04 / CAPITAL & LOGISTICS\n\nCHAPTER 04\n\nCapital & Logistics\n\nThe business bank account and supplier checklist\n\nBuild your money and supply systems before the first sale. A separate bank\naccount, clear cash reserves, reliable suppliers, and documented orders help\nyou see where your money is going and what the business can actually afford.\nThese are operating tools. They help you spot money already committed to tax,\nstock that ties up cash, and inputs that could stop trading if a supplier misses a\ndelivery. You do not need an elaborate system to begin. You need a simple one\nthat you use consistently.\nBank products, documentary requirements, maintaining balances, fees, and\nlending programs change. Verify current details with the specific institution and\nbranch. Ask your CPA to check tax reserves, payroll obligations, and\nrecordkeeping for your business. Any figures in this chapter are planning\nexamples unless clearly identified otherwise.\n\nStart here\n• Choose a suitable business account and confirm the branch’s document\nchecklist.\n• Separate operating cash, tax reserves, and emergency money before opening.\n• Validate your key suppliers, document their terms, and test a purchase-order\nand receiving process.\nBEFORE YOU MOVE ON\n\n• My business account is operational, and personal spending is separated.\n• Operating cash, tax reserves, and an emergency buffer have clear rules.\n• Key supplier terms, backups, purchase orders, and receiving responsibilities\nare documented.\n• Landed costs, petty cash, monthly obligations, and restocking logistics are\nincluded in my opening plan.","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 62","text":"CHAPTER 04 / CAPITAL & LOGISTICS\n\nCHAPTER 04 / YOUR CHECKLIST\n\nMark your progress\nCheck an item only when the work is complete and the evidence is saved.\n☐ 4.01 Separate business money from day one\n☐ 4.02 Choose a bank that fits your cash flow\n☐ 4.03 Prepare the bank’s exact document pack\n☐ 4.04 Get corporate account authority right\n☐ 4.05 Give cash three separate jobs\n☐ 4.06 Learn financing requirements early\n☐ 4.07 Check suppliers on four essentials\n☐ 4.08 Ask for terms after building trust\n☐ 4.09 Prepare backup sources for critical stock\n☐ 4.10 Put supplier terms on one clear page\n☐ 4.11 Use a purchase order for every order\n☐ 4.12 Price from the full cost of stock\n☐ 4.13 Control petty cash with a fixed float\n☐ 4.14 List the full monthly cash commitment\n☐ 4.15 Plan how stock reaches the shelf\n\nKeep in mind\nGive every peso a job. Know what is available for daily operations, what is\nreserved for obligations, and what protects you when something goes wrong. A\nhealthy-looking bank balance is only useful when you know how much is already\ncommitted.\nTreat suppliers as part of your operating system. Check their reliability, agree\nthe terms, record orders, and have alternatives for critical inputs. Consistent\nrecords make decisions easier as the business grows.\nStart this work before opening. Some steps, such as negotiating credit after 60\nto 90 days of reliable payments, continue once you are trading. Build the\nstructure early, then improve it using real transactions and actual demand.\nGood systems reduce how much the business depends on the owner\nremembering every detail. That is the practical goal behind PromdiPreneur’s\nsystems-first approach.","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 63","text":"CHAPTER 04 / CAPITAL & LOGISTICS\n\nCHAPTER 04 • CHECKLIST 4.01\n\nSeparate business money from day\none\nDO THIS\n\nOpen a dedicated business bank account before the first business transaction.\nPlan the application alongside registration so the account is ready when\ncustomers and suppliers begin moving money.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nWhen personal and business transactions share one account, it is harder to tell\nwhether the business is profitable or quietly using your savings. Reconstructing\nthose movements later creates extra bookkeeping and can make supporting your\nrecords during a tax review more difficult.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nAim to begin the account-opening process in the same week you complete BIR\nregistration, subject to the bank’s document requirements. Do not rely on a\npromise to separate the money “once things settle down.” Each extra mixed\ntransaction adds another item to untangle.\nUse the business account details consistently for customer payments and\nsupplier transactions. Keep personal purchases out of it. Where the owner\ncontributes money to the business, record what the transfer represents so it is\nnot confused with sales.\nSet a fixed, scheduled transfer to your personal account for owner pay or\ndrawings, using the treatment your accountant recommends for your business\nstructure. Avoid taking unrecorded cash whenever a personal expense comes\nup.\nCheck the account regularly against your records. Separation gives you a clean\nstarting point; you still need to record receipts, expenses, transfers, and\nbalances. If a mixed transaction happens, document and correct its treatment\npromptly rather than leaving it for year-end.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe account is operational before trading, customers and suppliers have the\ncorrect payment details, and owner transfers have a schedule and clear\naccounting treatment.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nAsk your accountant to confirm the account arrangement and treatment of\nowner contributions, compensation, and drawings for your business structure.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 64","text":"CHAPTER 04 / CAPITAL & LOGISTICS\n\nCHAPTER 04 • CHECKLIST 4.02\n\nChoose a bank that fits your cash flow\nDO THIS\n\nCompare accounts against your capital, expected balance, payment needs, and\naccess to a useful branch. A recognizable bank name is only one consideration;\nthe account must work for the size of your business.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA maintaining balance uses cash you cannot freely deploy without risking fees.\nFor illustration, a business taking in Php 80,000 monthly may find it difficult to\nkeep Php 50,000 parked while also paying rent, stock, and staff. Revenue is not\nthe same as available cash.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nTest maintaining balances of Php 25,000 to Php 100,000 and monthly\nbelow-balance fees of Php 300 to Php 500 in your comparison. These are\nillustrative figures, not current quotes from a named bank. Obtain the actual\nterms for the exact product.\nFor starting capital below Php 500,000 and irregular early cash flow, examine\nlower-entry options alongside BDO, BPI, and Metrobank. Compare transfer\ncharges, cash access, eligibility, and the required documents.\nAsk Maya Business about QR acceptance and transaction dashboards;\nUnionDigital about eligible business products; LANDBANK about relevant\nMSME financing; and BDO Network Bank about branch access in your province.\nVerify every offering. A merchant-payment service and a business deposit\naccount may serve different purposes.\nCheck maintaining balances and service access against your actual location and\nneeds. An existing account does not guarantee financing. Move to a more\ndemanding current account when your average daily balance and operational\nrequirements justify it, rather than paying for a business image you do not yet\nneed.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou have compared current written terms and selected an eligible account\nwhose costs, access, and services fit your forecast.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nCheck each institution’s current product availability, business eligibility, fees,\nminimum balances, and relevant financing requirements.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 65","text":"CHAPTER 04 / CAPITAL & LOGISTICS\n\nCHAPTER 04 • CHECKLIST 4.03\n\nPrepare the bank’s exact document\npack\nDO THIS\n\nCall the specific branch at least one business day before visiting. Ask its\nnew-accounts officer for the complete, current checklist for your business\nstructure and chosen account, including originals, copies, and certification\nrequirements.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA missing document can turn a bank visit into a wasted trip. This is especially\ncostly when you travel from another municipality. Preparing to the actual\nbranch’s checklist is more reliable than assuming a generic list will be accepted\neverywhere.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nUse this as a preparation list, subject to branch confirmation: DTI business-name\nregistration for a sole proprietor; or SEC registration and incorporation\ndocuments for a corporation or One Person Corporation. Ask what equivalent\ndocuments apply if your structure differs.\nCheck whether the bank needs the current Mayor’s or Business Permit, BIR\nCertificate of Registration (Form 2303), proof of business address such as a\nsigned lease or acceptable utility bill, and identification for every signatory.\nPrepare two government-issued photo-and-signature IDs as a starting checklist,\nthen confirm which IDs and how many the bank accepts.\nGet the bank’s account-opening forms ahead of time if available. For\ncorporations and OPCs, confirm the appropriate account-opening authority or\nresolution and authorized signatories. Item 4 explains the details to settle with\nthe branch.\nConfirm the initial deposit amount and accepted funding method. For a long trip,\nthree photocopies of each document can be a practical precaution, but ask\nwhich copies must be certified or notarized. Bring required originals and check\nwhether every signatory must attend.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe branch has confirmed its checklist, you have checked every required\noriginal and copy, and your signatories and initial funding are ready for the\nappointment.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the complete current document checklist with the specific branch and\naccount-opening officer before travelling.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 66","text":"CHAPTER 04 / CAPITAL & LOGISTICS\n\nCHAPTER 04 • CHECKLIST 4.04\n\nGet corporate account authority right\nDO THIS\n\nFor a corporation or One Person Corporation, ask the bank what formal\nauthority it requires to open the account and appoint signatories. Use the\nbranch’s own resolution, certificate, or other required format where available.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nThe bank needs to know that the entity authorized the account and who can\ntransact for it. Unclear names, missing details, or the wrong signing\narrangement can delay opening even when the rest of the document pack is\ncomplete.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nBefore drafting, request the bank’s current template and instructions. Ask\nwhether the requirements differ for an OPC. A generic template can help you\nprepare, but it should not override the institution’s required wording or the\napprovals your entity must properly make.\nCheck that the document identifies the bank and branch, the account type, and\neach authorized signatory’s full name and corporate role. State the signing\narrangement clearly: may one person sign alone, or must two or more act\njointly?\nHave the appropriate corporate officer or adviser confirm how the authority\nmust be approved, signed, certified, and, if required, notarized. Do not assume\nevery corporation and OPC uses the same directors, officers, or approval\nprocess.\nAsk the branch to review a draft if it offers that service. Then bring the required\noriginal and copies. An original notarized document plus two photocopies is a\nuseful preparation example only where those requirements apply. Keep a\ncompany copy with the account records.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe authority matches the bank’s format and your entity’s requirements, the\nsigning arrangement is unambiguous, and the required original and copies are\nready.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm your entity’s required approval, certification, signatories, and\nnotarization with the bank and an appropriate corporate adviser.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 67","text":"CHAPTER 04 / CAPITAL & LOGISTICS\n\nCHAPTER 04 • CHECKLIST 4.05\n\nGive cash three separate jobs\nDO THIS\n\nSet up three clearly separated cash pools from the start: operating money, a tax\nreserve, and an emergency buffer. Dedicated accounts can make the separation\neasier, provided the available products and fees suit your business.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA single balance can look healthy while much of it is already committed. Rent,\npayroll, supplier payments, and tax may fall due together. Separating reserves\nhelps you see what is genuinely available for everyday spending and what needs\nto remain untouched.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nOperating account: deposit business revenue here and pay ordinary operating\ncosts from it. Use the records to see your actual cash movement rather than\ntreating the balance alone as proof that the business is doing well.\nTax reserve account: transfer an agreed percentage of each week’s revenue into\nthis reserve. Ask your CPA to set and review the percentage using your tax type,\nprojected revenue, and other relevant obligations. Use the reserve for its\nintended tax payments when they become due.\nEmergency buffer account: aim to fund one month of fixed operating expenses\nbefore opening. Define what counts as a genuine operational emergency and\navoid using the buffer to cover routine overspending without addressing the\ncause.\nConfirm account eligibility, maintaining balances, and transfer charges before\nopening several accounts. Where you use another clear separation method,\nmake sure the reserved amounts remain visible and reconciled. This structure\nimproves control; it cannot eliminate every cash shortage or replace a realistic\nbudget.\nDONE WHEN\n\nEach cash pool has a purpose, opening target, transfer rule, and authorized use.\nYour weekly routine shows operating cash separately from tax and emergency\nreserves.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nHave your CPA confirm the tax-reserve method and percentage, and check\naccount fees before setting up the structure.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 68","text":"CHAPTER 04 / CAPITAL & LOGISTICS\n\nCHAPTER 04 • CHECKLIST 4.06\n\nLearn financing requirements early\nDO THIS\n\nDuring the first 60 days of operation, research at least one government MSME\nfinancing program that may fit your business. Learn its current eligibility rules\nand documents before a cash emergency forces a rushed decision.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nFinancing applications take preparation. Waiting until a supplier is overdue or\nequipment fails may leave too little time to gather records and evaluate the\nterms. Early research gives you a clearer view of possible options without\ncommitting you to borrowing.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nStart with the relevant programs of Small Business Corporation (SB Corp),\nLANDBANK, or the Development Bank of the Philippines (DBP). SB Corp and\nSmall Business Corporation are the same institution, not two separate lenders.\nCheck which current program fits your sector, business age, and capital needs.\nRequest the current eligibility and document list from the institution. Ask about\napplication steps, expected processing time, interest and fees, security\nrequirements, repayment schedule, and any other conditions that would affect\naffordability.\nAssemble useful records while your books and compliance documents are\ncurrent. You can prepare the file and understand the process even if you do not\nplan to apply immediately. Do not assume a preliminary conversation or an\nexisting bank account guarantees approval.\nCompare the total cost and repayment obligation with the business’s likely cash\nflow. Government-backed programs are not automatically cheaper or\nappropriate for every borrower. Treat financing as a decision to evaluate, not as\nmoney already available in your emergency plan.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou know the current requirements for at least one relevant program, have an\norganized preparation file, and understand that eligibility, approval, cost, and\ntiming remain subject to the lender’s assessment.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nVerify current program availability, eligibility, rates, fees, processing times, and\nother conditions directly with the lender.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 69","text":"CHAPTER 04 / CAPITAL & LOGISTICS\n\nCHAPTER 04 • CHECKLIST 4.07\n\nCheck suppliers on four essentials\nDO THIS\n\nEvaluate every prospective supplier before the first order using four criteria:\nreliability, price stability, minimum-order flexibility, and replacement policy. Get\nthe important answers in writing so you can compare suppliers consistently.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA nearby supplier, a personal connection, or a low introductory price may be\nuseful, but none proves dependable service. Late deliveries, unexpected price\nchanges, and unresolved damaged stock can interrupt sales and disappoint\ncustomers even when the quoted price looks attractive.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nReliability: ask whether the supplier can consistently deliver the agreed\nproducts, quantities, and dates. Request two existing customer references and\nask about routine service, not only the first order. A good sales conversation is\ndifferent from a dependable delivery record.\nPrice stability: ask how often prices change, how much notice is given, and how\nrevisions are communicated. Record the price basis so you can identify changes\nbetween the order and delivery.\nMinimum-order flexibility: compare the required quantity with your storage\nspace, cash cycle, and expected demand. A low unit price may still be a poor\ndeal if the minimum order ties up money or stock you cannot use quickly.\nReplacement policy: ask how damaged, defective, or short-delivered goods are\nreported and resolved, and how quickly replacements or other remedies are\nprovided. Record the contact person and the evidence they require. Keep all four\nanswers with the supplier’s quotation before committing to the order.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou have written answers to all four criteria, have checked available references,\nand can explain why the supplier fits your operation beyond price or personal\nfamiliarity.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 70","text":"CHAPTER 04 / CAPITAL & LOGISTICS\n\nCHAPTER 04 • CHECKLIST 4.08\n\nAsk for terms after building trust\nDO THIS\n\nAfter 60 to 90 days of consistent, on-time cash-on-delivery payments, ask key\nsuppliers whether they can offer suitable payment terms. Use your actual order\nand payment record to support the conversation.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nUnder cash on delivery (COD), you pay before you have sold the stock. With a\nseven-to-fourteen-day inventory turn, money stays tied up while you wait for\nsales. Appropriate terms can reduce that timing pressure, but they also create a\nfuture payment commitment.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nFrame the request around a dependable trading relationship: “We have been\nconsistent with payments and would like to discuss net-15 or net-30 terms to\nmanage ordering volume and grow the account.” These are negotiation options,\nnot terms every distributor will offer.\nAsk how the due date is calculated, whether there is a credit limit, and whether\nprices, discounts, or fees differ from COD. Confirm the consequences of late\npayment. A longer payment period is useful only if the full arrangement works\nfor the business.\nCheck the proposed due dates against your sales and collection cycle. Do not\nincrease orders merely because payment is delayed. Plan for the bill when it\nfalls due and preserve the reliable payment behavior that made the conversation\npossible.\nGet any agreement in a letter, email, or signed terms sheet. Keep it with the\nsupplier record so a replacement sales representative can see the same terms. If\ncredit is declined, continue with a workable COD plan rather than assuming it\nwill soon become available.\nDONE WHEN\n\nAny approved terms are documented, their due dates and costs are clear, and\nyour purchasing plan can meet the resulting obligations without relying on\nhoped-for sales.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 71","text":"CHAPTER 04 / CAPITAL & LOGISTICS\n\nCHAPTER 04 • CHECKLIST 4.09\n\nPrepare backup sources for critical\nstock\nDO THIS\n\nIdentify your three most critical inputs: the items whose absence could stop or\nseriously disrupt operations within 48 hours. Establish at least one workable\nalternative supplier for each before you need an emergency order.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA supplier can face shortages, vehicle problems, its own payment disputes, or\npressure to prioritize larger customers. If you depend on only one source, its\nproblem quickly becomes yours. A backup relationship gives you another option\nwhen the primary source cannot deliver.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nLook at the operation rather than just the biggest spend. For a milk tea shop, tea\nbase or fresh milk may be critical. For a minimart, the key dependency may be\nits fastest-moving products. For a water-refilling operation, identify the\ntreatment or other inputs your actual process requires.\nName the top three dependencies and record the time you could keep operating\nwithout each. Use the two-day threshold as a practical test, not a claim that\nevery business has identical risks. Check which shortages would affect your core\ncustomer promise.\nPlace a small test order with each backup where practical. This establishes the\ntrading relationship, checks the product against your standards, and tests\ndelivery and payment arrangements. A saved phone number alone does not\nconfirm that the supplier can serve you.\nKeep the backup’s current contact details, normal lead time, minimum order,\nand acceptable product specifications with the purchasing records. Know how to\nactivate the alternative quickly when needed. Redundancy is preparation, not an\naccusation that your main supplier will fail.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour three critical inputs each have a tested or otherwise verified backup, and\nthe person ordering stock knows when and how to use it.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 72","text":"CHAPTER 04 / CAPITAL & LOGISTICS\n\nCHAPTER 04 • CHECKLIST 4.10\n\nPut supplier terms on one clear page\nDO THIS\n\nFor your three highest-volume suppliers, request a simple written agreement\ncovering the terms that matter when something goes wrong. One or two clear\npages may be enough for a straightforward arrangement.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA handshake or scattered message thread can leave different expectations about\nprice changes, damaged goods, short deliveries, and payment dates. A written\nreference makes everyday ordering easier and gives both parties something\nspecific to consult during a disagreement.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nInclude agreed unit prices or the accepted price-list reference, along with the\nnotice required before a revision takes effect. State minimum quantities and\nnormal delivery lead times so you can plan storage and stock levels.\nRecord payment terms and what happens if payment is late. Include the\nreporting, return, replacement, or credit process for defective, damaged, and\nshort-delivered goods. The wording should match what the supplier can actually\nprovide.\nAgree how unresolved disputes will be handled and who each party should\ncontact first. Where the arrangement is more complex or carries significant risk,\nhave an appropriate adviser review it rather than relying on an informal\ntemplate.\nPresent the document as a practical way to keep both sides aligned. If a supplier\nwill not confirm any terms in writing, investigate why and include that\nuncertainty in your selection decision. It is a warning about the clarity of the\narrangement, not proof of how the supplier will behave in every future dispute.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour main supplier files contain mutually confirmed prices, delivery\nexpectations, payment terms, remedies, and a dispute process, with a clear\nrecord of any later changes.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 73","text":"CHAPTER 04 / CAPITAL & LOGISTICS\n\nCHAPTER 04 • CHECKLIST 4.11\n\nUse a purchase order for every order\nDO THIS\n\nCreate a simple purchase-order template before ordering opening stock. A\nspreadsheet or printed form is enough if it captures the agreed details and is\nused consistently for every supplier order.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nWithout an order record, it is difficult to tell whether the delivery matches what\nyou requested or whether the price changed. Missing records also make it\nharder to trace stock discrepancies, identify reorder patterns, and spot products\ntying up cash without enough sales.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nGive each purchase order, or PO, a sequential number. Record the order date,\nsupplier name, and expected delivery date. List each item separately with a\nclear description or specification, quantity, and agreed unit price, then show the\ntotal order value.\nIssue one PO per supplier per order. Keep the agreed version available to the\nperson receiving goods. If the order changes, make sure the receiving copy\nreflects the confirmed change rather than relying on an unexplained message or\nmemory.\nAssign a staff member to compare the delivery with the PO before signing the\nsupplier’s delivery receipt. Check actual quantities, item specifications, and\nprices. Record shortages, substitutions, damage, or other discrepancies on the\nreceipt before signing and follow the agreed resolution process.\nFile the PO with the delivery and payment records so you can trace the\ntransaction later. Set aside an initial 30 minutes to draft a basic template, then\nallow extra time to teach the checking routine. The consistent habit matters\nmore than the appearance of the form.\nDONE WHEN\n\nA numbered PO is used for every order, the receiver can access it, and\ndiscrepancies are documented before delivery acceptance and tracked to\nresolution.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 74","text":"CHAPTER 04 / CAPITAL & LOGISTICS\n\nCHAPTER 04 • CHECKLIST 4.12\n\nPrice from the full cost of stock\nDO THIS\n\nCalculate the per-unit cost of getting each product ready for sale before setting\nits selling price. Start with every product category, then examine individual\nitems where their costs or margins differ materially.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nThe supplier’s unit price is only part of the cost. Delivery, packaging, spoilage or\ndamage, and financing can reduce the margin you expected. A markup that\nlooks attractive on the quoted price may leave too little after those costs are\nincluded.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nUse a clear costing worksheet: supplier unit price, plus allocated delivery cost,\nplus required packaging, plus a spoilage or damage allowance where relevant,\nplus applicable financing cost. Express each component as a cost per saleable\nunit before adding it.\nFor perishables, document the spoilage assumption and the quantity you\nrealistically expect to sell. Do not add a percentage directly to a peso amount;\nconvert the assumption into a per-unit peso allowance using a consistent\nmethod. Review it against actual results once trading begins.\nIf stock is bought on credit or with borrowed funds, include the relevant cost in\nyour pricing analysis. Ask your accountant how these management-costing items\nshould be treated in the financial records; the pricing worksheet and formal\ninventory valuation may not be identical.\nCalculate your intended markup against the full cost, then compare the selling\nprice with what customers will reasonably pay. For a minimart or broad retail\nrange, this can expose low-margin categories before you tie up cash filling the\nshelves. Recheck when delivery costs, supplier prices, or wastage change.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour price list is supported by a documented full-cost worksheet, the\nassumptions are visible, and products with weak or unworkable margins have\nbeen reconsidered.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 75","text":"CHAPTER 04 / CAPITAL & LOGISTICS\n\nCHAPTER 04 • CHECKLIST 4.13\n\nControl petty cash with a fixed float\nDO THIS\n\nSet up a petty cash fund before opening, appoint one custodian, and document\nevery use. Choose a fixed amount suited to small operating needs rather than\ntaking unrecorded cash from the sales drawer.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nEmergency parts, cleaning supplies, same-day delivery charges, and small office\npurchases are easy to forget. Individually they look minor; together they create\nunexplained cash differences. A fixed fund gives you a simple way to record,\ncheck, and replenish them.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nUse Php 1,000 to Php 3,000 as a starting planning range for a small retail or\nfood establishment, then adjust to the operation. Record the approved float and\nwho is responsible for the cash. Keep petty cash separate from personal money\nand sales collections.\nSupport each payment with the supplier’s receipt or other appropriate\ndocument. If that is unavailable, use an internal petty cash voucher identifying\nthe purpose and recipient, signed by the recipient and countersigned by a\nsupervisor. Ask your accountant what additional tax support is required.\nWhen cash falls to around 30% of the approved float, reconcile the spending and\nreplenish the verified amount through one documented transfer from the\nbusiness account. File the vouchers and external documents with the\nreplenishment record.\nAt month-end, and before replenishment, check that remaining cash plus\nunreplenished supported spending equals the approved float. Investigate\ndifferences promptly. Do not count receipts already reimbursed a second time or\nsimply accept a shortage because the individual payments were small.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe fund has a named custodian, every payment is supported, replenishments\nare documented, and the cash-plus-unreplenished-spending check reconciles to\nthe fixed float.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nAsk your accountant which supporting documents are required for petty-cash\nexpenses and how to record exceptions.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 76","text":"CHAPTER 04 / CAPITAL & LOGISTICS\n\nCHAPTER 04 • CHECKLIST 4.14\n\nList the full monthly cash commitment\nDO THIS\n\nBefore opening, list every fixed and semi-fixed obligation, including reserves for\nbills that fall due quarterly or annually. Use the total to understand the cash the\nbusiness must cover before discretionary owner withdrawals or growth\nspending.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nRent and utilities are easy to remember. Loan repayments, employer\ncontributions, taxes, permit renewals, equipment maintenance, and 13th-month\npay are easier to overlook. Predictable bills feel like emergencies when they\nwere never included in the budget.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nInclude rent, expected utilities, loan repayments where applicable, and other\nrecurring commitments. Add applicable employer contributions to SSS,\nPhilHealth, and Pag-IBIG using current requirements and actual staffing\nassumptions.\nBudget a monthly reserve for projected tax payments and divide applicable\nannual business-permit renewal costs by twelve. Include preventive\nmaintenance for equipment. Verify current obligations with your accountant; do\nnot carry forward an old fee just because it appears in a previous checklist.\nFor eligible employees on a steady monthly basic salary, one-twelfth of that\nsalary each month can be a starting reserve for 13th-month pay. Confirm the\nactual entitlement, calculation, coverage, and treatment of partial-year service\nwith your payroll adviser.\nTotal the commitments and compare them with the assumptions in Chapter 1’s\nbreak-even and cash-flow model. They need to reconcile, but they are not\nautomatically the same number: stock costs, margins, loan principal, timing, and\nowner-pay assumptions may differ. Explain each difference and correct missing\ncosts before the first billing cycle.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour monthly budget includes recurring bills and reserves, uses current\nrequirements, and reconciles with the business’s break-even and cash-flow\nassumptions.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nCheck current taxes, employer contributions, and payroll rules with your\naccountant. The annual BIR registration fee was discontinued effective January\n22, 2024.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 77","text":"CHAPTER 04 / CAPITAL & LOGISTICS\n\nCHAPTER 04 • CHECKLIST 4.15\n\nPlan how stock reaches the shelf\nDO THIS\n\nMap the full restocking process before placing the first inventory order. Decide\nwho receives goods, where they wait before shelving, when deliveries may\narrive, and what happens if the usual delivery fails.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA delivery is not complete just because the vehicle arrives. Someone must be\navailable to check quantities, condition, and the purchase order. Without a plan,\nstock can arrive when the owner is absent, storage is not ready, or no trained\nreceiver is on shift.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nName a responsible receiver rather than leaving the task to “whoever is\navailable.” Give that person the authority and training to compare deliveries\nwith POs and record discrepancies on delivery receipts before signing. Arrange\ncoverage when the designated receiver is unavailable.\nSet aside a specific staging area for incoming stock before shelving. Check that\nit can hold your largest expected delivery and that the receiving process suits\nthe products. Do not discover on arrival that overstock has nowhere workable to\ngo.\nConfirm delivery windows with each supplier and schedule the receiver\naccordingly. Agree what staff should do if a vehicle arrives early or late,\nparticularly when no authorized person is available to accept and check the\ngoods.\nIn secondary cities and municipalities, verify the local transport arrangements\nrather than assuming the options available in Metro Manila exist nearby.\nIdentify at least one reliable transport-for-hire vehicle and driver for urgent\nrestocking runs. Save the contact and understand the service before your first\nstock-out emergency.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe receiving and staging process is ready, delivery windows match staff\ncoverage, and a verified local transport option is available when normal\nrestocking falls short.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 78","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05\n\nBefore You Hire\n\nBuild your employment and payroll system\n\nYour first hire brings responsibilities from the start. Before the person begins\nwork, prepare the contract, pay calculation, government-registration steps, time\nrecords, and payroll schedule. Give the employment relationship the same care\nyou gave your business registration.\nThis chapter helps you build that foundation. Clear standards, accurate records,\ntimely contributions, and fair treatment reduce avoidable disputes. They cannot\nguarantee that a complaint will never happen, but they give both you and your\nemployees a clearer basis for working together.\nPhilippine labor requirements can change, and coverage depends on the facts of\nthe business and the worker. Verify current wage orders, contribution rules,\nbenefits, and termination procedures with the relevant agency and a qualified\nlabor professional. Budget for a pre-hire consultation, particularly when building\na team of more than five people. That team-size marker is a planning suggestion,\nnot a legal threshold.\n\nStart here\n• Price the full cost of each role.\n• Confirm employer registration, worker classification, and benefit coverage.\n• Prepare role-specific contracts, payroll records, and calendar reminders.\nBEFORE YOU MOVE ON\n\n• Employer-registration and worker-reporting steps checked\n• Worker classification and wage category confirmed\n• Full role cost and hiring capacity tested\n• Role-specific contracts and review dates ready\n• Payroll, leave, attendance, and remittance routines assigned\n• Required notices and compliance folder prepared","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 79","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05 / YOUR CHECKLIST\n\nMark your progress\nCheck an item only when the work is complete and the evidence is saved.\n☐ 5.01 Set up all three employer registrations\n☐ 5.02 Identify the wage order that applies to you\n☐ 5.03 Budget the full cost of each employee\n☐ 5.04 Set clear probationary standards and review dates\n☐ 5.05 Use the right due-process procedure\n☐ 5.06 Classify the working relationship honestly\n☐ 5.07 Build a monthly contribution-remittance routine\n☐ 5.08 Give an itemized payslip every pay period\n☐ 5.09 Fund 13th-month pay throughout the year\n☐ 5.10 Track service incentive leave and its cash value\n☐ 5.11 Put correctly classified holidays into payroll\n☐ 5.12 Customize and review each role’s contract\n☐ 5.13 Post the notices your workplace requires\n☐ 5.14 Record working time from the first day\n☐ 5.15 Keep a ready-to-review employment folder\n☐ 5.16 Hire when the numbers support the role\n\nKeep in mind\nGood employment practices are part of good operations. Put the role in writing,\nexplain the standards, pay accurately, record working time, remit contributions,\nand handle concerns fairly. Keep doing these things after the first week, when\nopening-day attention has moved elsewhere.\nBuild the folder before the first working day and keep it current. Your records\nshould show that you treated the people helping you build the business with care\nand consistency. Systems support your team; the owner still remains responsible\nfor maintaining them.","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 80","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05 • CHECKLIST 5.01\n\nSet up all three employer\nregistrations\nDO THIS\n\nPlan and complete your SSS, PhilHealth, and Pag-IBIG employer-registration\nsteps before the first employee starts. Confirm the actual registration and\nemployee-reporting deadlines with each agency.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nYou introduced these registrations in Chapter 2. Now turn them into a working\npayroll system. Deferring the paperwork can create contribution arrears,\npenalties, and liability when an employee needs benefits. Do not assume a\nprobationary worker can be left out while you decide whether to keep them. For\nSSS, an employer’s failure to report or remit does not automatically extinguish\nan otherwise eligible employee’s benefit entitlement; the employer can still face\nserious consequences.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nBlock one dedicated week for the setup. A practical sequence is SSS, then\nPhilHealth, then Pag-IBIG. Check the current application route before visiting an\noffice so you know which steps can be completed online and which documents\nyour business type needs.\nCheck the current application requirements, including SSS Form R-1 for\nemployer registration and R-1A for the initial employment report, PhilHealth’s\nEmployer Data Record, and Pag-IBIG’s employer-registration form. Prepare your\nDTI or SEC certificate and valid government ID, plus any additional documents\nrequired for your business type.\nSave each employer number and the registration confirmation in your\ncompliance folder. Set up authorized online access immediately, then test that\nyou can view employee records, contribution tables, and remittance instructions.\nAssign one person to prepare the records and another to check them where\nstaffing allows.\nDONE WHEN\n\nEach registration is confirmed, employee-reporting steps are scheduled, and the\nperson handling payroll can access the official remittance channels.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm current employer-registration and employee-reporting steps with SSS,\nPhilHealth, and Pag-IBIG.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 81","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05 • CHECKLIST 5.02\n\nIdentify the wage order that applies\nto you\nDO THIS\n\nFind the current minimum-wage order for your region, industry, and\nestablishment category before offering a salary. Save the document used for\nyour calculation.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nThere is no single minimum daily wage for every Philippine business. Regional\nwage orders may distinguish between agriculture, non-agriculture, and\nparticular retail or service establishments. Using a rate from a friend’s business,\nan old screenshot, or a different region can leave your payroll model wrong.\nUnderpayment can become a claim for the difference. Paying above the\nminimum is a business choice; make that choice using the correct legal floor.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nGo to the National Wages and Productivity Commission or the relevant Regional\nTripartite Wages and Productivity Board. Download the actual order and any\nlater amendments or scheduled tranches. Check both the date of issue and the\neffectivity date.\nIdentify your region, sector, worker category, and establishment-size\nclassification. Look for any retail or service category linked to the number of\nworkers, including a fewer-than-ten category if the order provides one.\nEstablishment size does not create a universal exemption or automatic\npermission to pay less. Ask the board when the category is unclear.\nWrite the applicable daily rate and effective date on your payroll worksheet.\nPrint or save the order in the DOLE compliance folder, and set a reminder to\nreview it when a new order takes effect. Use the official document when\nexplaining the calculation to employees or an inspector.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour salary budget names the applicable wage order, worker category, rate, and\neffective date, with any exemption separately verified.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm your current wage order, category, and any exemption with the regional\nwage board.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 82","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05 • CHECKLIST 5.03\n\nBudget the full cost of each employee\nDO THIS\n\nCalculate the total monthly employer cost for every planned role before you hire.\nPut that full amount into the Chapter 1 financial model.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nThe basic wage is only part of the commitment. Your budget also needs the\nemployer shares of SSS, PhilHealth, and Pag-IBIG; a reserve for 13th-month pay;\napplicable leave costs; and required uniforms, health certificates, or protective\nequipment. If you budget only the employee’s take-home pay, the gap can\nappear later as a payroll shortage or an unfunded December payment. The\nemployee’s statutory deductions are also different from the business’s own\ncontribution expense.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nStart with the applicable wage and realistic paid work schedule. Add each\nemployer contribution using the current agency table. Add a monthly\n13th-month-pay provision based on one-twelfth of the relevant basic salary\nearned, subject to the correct computation rules. Include service incentive leave\nwhere the employee and establishment are covered.\nUse 120–130% of basic monthly wages as a first-pass planning estimate if you\nneed a quick sensitivity check. It is not a promised total or a legal formula.\nReplace it with your actual itemized calculation before making an offer.\nOvertime, holiday work, staffing patterns, and benefits can change the result.\nTest the combined cost of the whole team against expected revenue and\nbreak-even. If the plan no longer works, revise the staffing arrangement,\nsupport a higher revenue target with evidence, or reduce another cost before\ncommitting to recurring payroll.\nDONE WHEN\n\nEvery role has an itemized employer-cost worksheet, and the complete staffing\nbudget fits a realistic operating and cash-flow plan.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nCheck current contribution tables, benefit coverage, and payroll assumptions\nwith your payroll adviser.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 83","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05 • CHECKLIST 5.04\n\nSet clear probationary standards and\nreview dates\nDO THIS\n\nUse a role-specific probationary contract with reasonable performance\nstandards communicated at engagement. Have a qualified labor professional\nconfirm the period and procedure before you use it.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nProbation is a structured assessment period, not permission to dismiss someone\nwhenever you feel dissatisfied. Problems arise when standards were never\nexplained, evaluation happens too late, the worker continues beyond the lawful\nperiod, or the owner assumes that no process is required. Vague phrases such as\n“satisfactory performance” do little to tell an employee what success looks like\nor help you document a fair decision.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nWrite measurable standards connected to the job. A cashier, kitchen crew\nmember, and delivery rider need different duties and assessment criteria.\nDiscuss the standards, give the employee a copy, and retain a signed\nacknowledgment. Document feedback during the period so the final decision is\nsupported by more than memory.\nSchedule a formal review before the 150th calendar day as an internal\nearly-warning target. This leaves time to evaluate, document, and communicate\nthe outcome before the lawful probationary period ends. Ask your adviser to\ncalculate the actual end date; six calendar months should not automatically be\ntreated as exactly 180 days.\nSet the reminders on the first day. Decide in time whether to regularize or take\nanother lawful step, then follow the procedure that applies to the actual ground.\nRegular status does not depend solely on whether the employer issues a letter.\nCustomize any contract template and have it reviewed before use.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe employee has acknowledged the role standards, the lawful end date is\nchecked, and an earlier review date is on the calendar.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nHave a labor professional verify the probationary period, standards, end date,\nand notice procedure.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 84","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05 • CHECKLIST 5.05\n\nUse the right due-process procedure\nDO THIS\n\nBefore discipline or dismissal, identify the legal ground and the procedure that\napplies. Get labor advice for a termination decision rather than applying one\ntemplate to every situation.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA verbal instruction not to return, a termination text, or quietly removing\nsomeone from the schedule can create a serious dispute. Even when you believe\nthe facts are obvious, the employee must receive the process applicable to the\ncase. Keep the grounds for the decision separate from the steps used to reach it:\na concern about performance or conduct still needs clear evidence and fair\nhandling.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nFor a just-cause dismissal, issue a written Notice to Explain that identifies the\nalleged act or omission factually and specifically. Give a reasonable response\nperiod of at least five calendar days under the applicable rules. Check the\nrequirements with your adviser before the process starts, and avoid wording\nthat prejudges the outcome.\nGive a genuine opportunity to respond. Depending on the facts and legal\nrequirements, this may include a written explanation and a clarificatory\nmeeting. Review the response with an open mind. Then issue the appropriate\nwritten decision stating the findings, basis, and action.\nRetain the notices, proof of delivery, response, and decision records securely.\nFailure to meet previously communicated probationary standards has a different\nnotice rule from just-cause dismissal. Authorized-cause termination also has its\nown requirements. Review the actual ground before issuing any notice, and\nfollow a lawful records-retention policy.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe applicable process is checked, the evidence and employee response are\nrecorded, and any decision is communicated through the correct procedure.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the lawful ground and case-specific procedure with a qualified labor\nprofessional before acting.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 85","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05 • CHECKLIST 5.06\n\nClassify the working relationship\nhonestly\nDO THIS\n\nDecide whether each worker is an employee or a genuine independent\ncontractor using the actual arrangement, not the heading on the contract.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nCalling someone a freelancer does not by itself remove employment obligations.\nPhilippine decisions examine factors such as selection and engagement,\npayment, the power of dismissal, and control over how the work is done. Control\nover the means and manner of work is particularly important. A person directed\nwhen to arrive, what to wear, where to work, and how to perform daily tasks\nmay have an employment relationship despite a service-contract label.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nWrite down who sets the schedule, supplies the instructions, supervises the\nwork, and decides how results are produced. Note whether the person serves\nother clients and operates an independent business. Review all the facts\ntogether rather than treating any one fact as a shortcut.\nExamples worth examining include a project-based graphic designer with their\nown schedule and multiple clients, a monthly bookkeeper with an independent\nprofessional practice, and a remote IT contractor working to a defined\nmaintenance scope. These examples are not automatic exemptions; the actual\nrelationship still matters.\nGive closer attention to someone working exclusively on your premises, within\nhours you set, on core daily operations, and under your supervision. Ask a labor\nprofessional to review doubtful cases before work begins. Misclassification can\nresult in back contributions, penalties, and benefit claims, so compare the lawful\narrangement’s real cost rather than choosing the cheapest contract label.\nDONE WHEN\n\nEvery worker has a documented classification supported by the actual working\narrangement, with uncertain cases reviewed before engagement.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nHave uncertain employee–contractor classifications reviewed against the actual\nworking arrangement.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 86","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05 • CHECKLIST 5.07\n\nBuild a monthly\ncontribution-remittance routine\nDO THIS\n\nSet up SSS, PhilHealth, and Pag-IBIG contribution preparation, payment, and\nconfirmation before the first payroll cycle. Give each deadline a named owner.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nGovernment contributions need their own routine. A calendar reminder without\na calculation, available funds, and a working payment route is not enough. Late\nor missing remittances can create penalties and employee-benefit problems. SSS\ncurrently identifies a 2% monthly late-payment penalty on unpaid contributions.\nConfirm current rules separately with PhilHealth and Pag-IBIG rather than\nassuming all three use the same penalty or deadline.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nDuring the first week after registration, confirm the current employer payment\nchannels and test authorized portal access. Some digital-wallet channels may\nsupport particular government payments, but verify whether the exact employer\ncontribution type is supported before relying on GCash, Maya, or another\nprovider.\nRecord each agency’s actual deadline in a recurring calendar and set a\npreparation reminder ten days earlier. For regular business employers, SSS\ncurrently sets the deadline at the last day of the following month, with the stated\nnext-working-day adjustment for weekends or holidays. Confirm the separate\nPhilHealth and Pag-IBIG schedules before building the calendar.\nDownload official contribution tables and check them at least quarterly, as well\nas whenever an agency announces a change. After paying, keep the confirmation\nand check that the remittance is reflected correctly in the relevant records.\nReconcile payroll deductions, employer shares, and actual remittances so an\naccepted payment does not hide a missing employee or incorrect amount.\nDONE WHEN\n\nCurrent tables and deadlines are recorded, the payment route works, and each\npayroll deduction can be matched to a confirmed remittance.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nRecheck contribution rates, deadlines, penalties, and employer-payment\nchannels with each agency.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 87","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05 • CHECKLIST 5.08\n\nGive an itemized payslip every pay\nperiod\nDO THIS\n\nCreate a consistent payslip and payroll-record process before the first payday.\nGive each employee a clear breakdown and retain evidence of payment.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nCash payments and verbal confirmations leave too much room for disagreement.\nA worker should be able to see what was earned, what was deducted, and what\nwas received. If a wage claim arises, you will need documentary support for your\ncalculation and payment. An employee’s silence during an informal payday is not\na reliable substitute for records. A signed receipt is useful evidence, but it does\nnot make an incorrect computation lawful.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nYour template should show the pay period, basic pay, relevant allowances,\novertime or other premiums, mandatory contribution deductions, withholding\ntax where applicable, other lawful deductions, and net pay. Use a spreadsheet or\npayroll tool that your adviser has checked against the applicable requirements.\nGive the employee a copy each period. For cash payments, retain a dated\nacknowledgment of the amount received. For bank or electronic payments, keep\nthe appropriate payment confirmation alongside the payroll computation, and\nuse a reliable acknowledgment process for the payslip. Resolve questions while\nthe time records are still easy to check.\nLink the payslip to attendance, approved adjustments, contribution\ncomputations, and payment evidence. A simple template may take less than five\nminutes per employee once the calculations are ready, but check accuracy\nbefore speed. Establish secure retention with your adviser; the three-year period\nrelevant to many money claims does not override longer requirements or a live\ndispute.\nDONE WHEN\n\nEvery pay period has an itemized employee copy, a matching retained record,\nand evidence that the correct net pay was received.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm payroll documentation, acknowledgment, lawful deductions, and\nretention requirements with your adviser.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 88","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05 • CHECKLIST 5.09\n\nFund 13th-month pay throughout the\nyear\nDO THIS\n\nCalculate the applicable 13th-month-pay obligation correctly and set aside\nmoney monthly, starting with the first covered employee’s first month.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nFor covered employees, 13th-month pay is a statutory obligation rather than a\ndiscretionary Christmas bonus. The general calculation is total basic salary\nearned during the calendar year divided by twelve. Using the wrong salary base,\nmishandling a mid-year hire, or waiting until December to find the cash can all\ncreate problems. Check coverage and the treatment of particular compensation\nitems instead of assuming every allowance is always excluded.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nBuild a monthly worksheet for each employee. Record the basic salary actually\nearned, update the year-to-date total, and calculate the developing obligation.\nCheck coverage for rank-and-file employees who worked for at least one month\nduring the calendar year, including probationary workers, and confirm how the\nrules apply to the actual workforce.\nReserve one-twelfth of the relevant monthly basic salary as you go. You may use\nthe separate reserve account from Chapter 4, but identify the payroll-benefit\nallocation separately so it is not accidentally spent on taxes. Reconcile the\namount reserved with the payroll worksheet each month.\nConfirm the December 24 payment deadline and any requirements for\nemployees who leave during the year. Use the current DOLE computation guide\nand have your payroll adviser review examples for mid-year starts, changes in\nsalary, and other relevant circumstances. By the payment date, the money and\nsupporting computation should already be ready.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe computation is checked, the monthly reserve is funded, and the payment\ndeadline and employee-by-employee amounts are scheduled.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nCheck current DOLE guidance on coverage, basic-salary computation, and\npayment timing.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 89","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05 • CHECKLIST 5.10\n\nTrack service incentive leave and its\ncash value\nDO THIS\n\nConfirm whether your establishment and workers are covered by service\nincentive leave, then maintain individual leave records and handle unused leave\nunder the applicable rules.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nArticle 95 provides five days of service incentive leave after at least one year of\nservice for covered employees. Exceptions include establishments regularly\nemploying fewer than ten employees, among other exclusions. Confirm coverage\nbefore budgeting or writing the policy. Where the benefit applies, an employee’s\nfailure to ask does not automatically remove the obligation. Unrecorded leave\nand unpaid conversion can become a liability when someone leaves or raises a\nclaim.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nStart an employment and leave record on the first day. Record the service date,\neligibility date, leave taken, leave type, and balance. Explain the policy in plain\nlanguage so staff understand how to request leave and how the balance is\nchecked. Keep approvals and payroll entries with the leave record.\nAt the relevant year-end or other required settlement point, calculate unused\ncovered leave using the correct daily rate and rules. Have your adviser confirm\nwhen cash conversion is required and whether any proposed carry-forward\narrangement is lawful. Do not assume a company policy can replace a statutory\nentitlement.\nConsider the recordkeeping problem created by three years of unresolved leave\nfor five employees. Regular reconciliation prevents that pile-up. Record each\ncomputation and payment in the personnel file, then confirm that the employee\nreceives the benefit that applies. A small spreadsheet maintained throughout the\nyear is easier to manage than reconstructing several years later.\nDONE WHEN\n\nCoverage is confirmed, leave balances reconcile to records, and required\nunused-leave payments are calculated and documented on time.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm SIL coverage, exceptions, accrual, conversion, and separation\ntreatment with DOLE or your adviser.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 90","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05 • CHECKLIST 5.11\n\nPut correctly classified holidays into\npayroll\nDO THIS\n\nBuild the payroll holiday calendar from the official proclamation for the relevant\nyear, then check current DOLE pay advisories and any later national or local\nproclamations.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nRegular holidays and special non-working days have different pay treatment.\nUsing an old holiday list or treating all holidays alike can produce incorrect pay.\nCoverage, attendance conditions, rest-day overlap, and overtime can also\nchange the calculation. A holiday’s familiar name is not enough to establish its\nclassification for the year. Confirm the official declaration before applying a\nrate.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nAt the start of the year, download the proclamation from the Official Gazette or\nanother official government source. Label each date by its actual category.\nCheck familiar dates such as New Year’s Day, Araw ng Kagitingan, Holy Week,\nLabor Day, Independence Day, National Heroes Day, Bonifacio Day, Christmas\nDay, and Rizal Day against the complete current list.\nBasic reference rates include 100% for a covered unworked regular holiday and\n200% for covered work on it, versus a usual no-work-no-pay approach and 130%\nfor work on a special non-working day. Verify coverage, attendance conditions,\nrest-day overlap, and additional premiums before using these starting rates in\npayroll.\nCheck for later proclamations throughout the year. A weekly search alert can\nhelp you notice changes, but confirm each result at an official source. Be\nespecially careful with movable holidays, locally declared days, and dates such\nas the EDSA anniversary whose classification must not be assumed from an old\nlist.\nDONE WHEN\n\nEach relevant holiday has an official classification and checked pay treatment in\nthe payroll calendar, including later changes.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nCheck the year’s official national and local proclamations and applicable DOLE\npay advisories.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 91","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05 • CHECKLIST 5.12\n\nCustomize and review each role’s\ncontract\nDO THIS\n\nUse a reviewed contract that describes the actual job, pay, schedule, standards,\nand workplace process. Customize it before asking the employee to sign.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA downloaded template can be a useful starting point, but it may contain\noutdated clauses or omit the details that matter in your business. A contract for\na cashier will not fully describe a kitchen worker’s duties or a rider’s schedule.\nClear role-specific terms help the employee understand the job and help you\nmanage it consistently. A professional review reduces avoidable gaps; it does not\nguarantee the outcome of every future dispute.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nCheck that the contract identifies the specific duties, compensation\narrangement, work schedule, appropriate probationary standards, and relevant\nconfidentiality obligations. Include a clear route for workplace concerns without\nsuggesting employees give up their legal rights. Make sure the written terms\nmatch how the work will actually operate.\nUse a suitable sample contract as a drafting aid. Remove anything that does not\nfit the role and have a qualified Philippine labor lawyer or suitably qualified HR\nprofessional review the customized version before the first hire. A reassuring\ntemplate label cannot replace checking the actual clauses and working\narrangement.\nA reviewed base for the same role may save time on later hires, but check it\nagain whenever the role, schedule, compensation, law, or business arrangement\nchanges. Explain the finished contract to the employee, allow questions, provide\na copy, and keep the signed contract and acknowledgments in the personnel file.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe contract matches the real role, has been professionally reviewed, and is\nsigned, understood, and filed with the employee’s copy provided.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nHave the customized contract reviewed before use and whenever the role or\nrelevant rules change.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 92","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05 • CHECKLIST 5.13\n\nPost the notices your workplace\nrequires\nDO THIS\n\nAsk the relevant agencies which notices and permits your establishment must\ndisplay, then put current copies where the intended audience can actually see\nthem.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nPosting requirements are easy to miss because they feel smaller than payroll or\npermits. Yet employees need access to required labor information, and\napplicable agencies may require certain business documents to be displayed. A\ndocument filed neatly in a drawer may not meet a display obligation.\nRequirements differ by industry and location, so avoid treating a generic online\nlist as a complete compliance checklist.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nCheck your DOLE regional office for the current posting requirements and\nprint-ready materials. Confirm whether the applicable wage order and particular\nlabor-standards notices must be posted. For food, personal-care, or other\nregulated activities, ask the relevant current health or sector regulator about\nadditional notices.\nPrint legibly, protect the pages if the location is exposed to moisture or heavy\nhandling, and mount employee-facing notices near the break area, back office,\nor time-record station. Staff should not need permission to enter the owner’s\noffice just to read them. Keep a digital master so an outdated or damaged copy\ncan be replaced quickly.\nAt the same time, check the separate display requirements for the BIR\nCertificate of Registration and the current Mayor’s Permit. Photograph the\nfinished locations for your own records and add a monthly check to the\ncompliance-folder routine. One planned printing and mounting session can close\nseveral gaps, but later wage orders and renewed permits still need updating.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe required notices are confirmed, current, legible, and displayed in the proper\nlocations, with a replacement check assigned.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the exact notices and permit displays required by DOLE, BIR, your\nLGU, and any sector regulator.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 93","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05 • CHECKLIST 5.14\n\nRecord working time from the first\nday\nDO THIS\n\nChoose a practical time-and-attendance method and use it consistently before\nthe first payroll is calculated. Record actual time worked and retain the\nsupporting records securely.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nOvertime, undertime, night differential, rest-day work, and holiday premiums\ndepend on accurate time information. Memory and supervisor impressions are\nweak foundations for payroll. If a dispute arises, records help both sides check\nwhat happened. The absence of records does not automatically decide every\ncase, but it makes an accurate reconstruction much harder and leaves the\nbusiness with less support for its calculation.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nFor a small team of one to five people, consider an appropriate time-recording\ndevice or a consistently maintained daily time record. An illustrative\nbiometric-device budget is ₱2,000–₱4,000; obtain current quotes and check\nsuitability before buying. A device price or product label does not establish legal\ncompliance.\nWith a paper system, record clock-in and clock-out, have employees\nacknowledge their entries, and have the supervisor review the shift record.\nEstablish a transparent correction process so mistakes are corrected with an\naudit trail rather than silently altered. Check actual hours against schedules\nbefore producing payslips.\nSet a retention policy with your adviser. Three years is relevant to many labor\nmoney claims, but keep records longer where another rule or an unresolved\nmatter requires it. If using biometrics or a digital platform, review privacy,\naccess, security, and retention obligations before collecting employee data.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe team can record and review actual work time, payroll uses those records,\nand corrections and retention follow a documented process.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nCheck attendance-record retention and privacy requirements, especially before\nusing biometrics.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 94","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05 • CHECKLIST 5.15\n\nKeep a ready-to-review employment\nfolder\nDO THIS\n\nCreate a DOLE compliance folder before the first hire, then update it at the end\nof every month. Keep employee information accessible only to authorized\npeople.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nLabor inspections may require you to produce contracts, payroll, attendance,\ncontribution records, benefit-payment evidence, and wage references. Having\norganized records helps an inspector understand what you have done and helps\nyou identify missing steps before a visit. The folder supports compliance;\npresentation alone cannot cure an unpaid benefit or incorrect record. Be\nprepared to produce the full period and documents lawfully requested.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nUse seven sections: employer registrations; employment records; payroll; time\nand attendance; government remittances; leave records; and compliance\nreferences. Keep signed current contracts and acknowledgments in the\nemployment section. Add payslips and payment evidence to payroll, and retain\nthe matching attendance records.\nKeep SSS, PhilHealth, and Pag-IBIG payment confirmations, leave records, the\napplicable wage order, and required posting materials. Include proof of\n13th-month payment once due. Place the most recent three months of payroll,\nattendance, and remittance records in a handy front section. Retain the full\nrequired history behind it or in a clearly indexed archive.\nAt month-end, check each section against a short index. Replace superseded\nreference copies while preserving records needed for earlier periods. Store\nphysical originals safely and keep usable digital backups where appropriate.\nWhen an authorized officer requests records, verify the request and provide the\nrelevant documents promptly without exposing unrelated employee information.\nDONE WHEN\n\nAll seven sections are current, recent records are easy to retrieve, and required\nhistorical records remain securely available.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm current inspection requirements, full retention periods, and authorized\naccess to employee records.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 95","text":"CHAPTER 05 / BEFORE YOU HIRE\n\nCHAPTER 05 • CHECKLIST 5.16\n\nHire when the numbers support the\nrole\nDO THIS\n\nBefore posting a job, test whether the business can sustain the full cost of the\nrole through an ordinary month and a below-average revenue month.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nOwner overwhelm is a real operational warning, but it does not tell you how\nmuch payroll the business can afford. A hire may reduce your workload\nimmediately while adding a recurring commitment that revenue cannot yet\ncover. Make the financial decision before the employment commitment. If the\ntiming is not right, define the revenue milestone and operational changes\nneeded to make it work rather than hoping the new employee will somehow fund\nthe role.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nTotal the basic monthly wage, employer SSS share, employer PhilHealth share,\nemployer Pag-IBIG share, applicable 13th-month provision, and applicable leave\nprovision. Use the correct wage category and contribution tables. Add other\nrole-specific costs identified in Item 3 rather than relying only on take-home pay.\nCompare that total with current average monthly net cash flow after existing\nfixed obligations. Is there enough recurring surplus to cover the new role with a\nmargin for a weaker month? For a pre-launch business, use a conservative\nprojection and funded runway, clearly distinguishing estimates from an\nestablished trading record.\nIf the answer is yes, confirm the rest of this chapter’s setup before the person\nstarts. If the answer is no, write down the specific revenue or cash-flow\nmilestone that will trigger the hire and review progress against it. Adjust work\nallocation or simplify the operation within lawful staffing arrangements while\nbuilding that capacity.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe hire is supported by a realistic cost and cash-flow test, or a clear future\nhiring trigger is recorded in the financial plan.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 96","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06\n\nBefore You Sell\n\nPrepare invoices, books, tax filings, and sales systems\n\nBefore the first customer pays, make sure the transaction can be documented\ncorrectly. You need the right registered books, compliant sales documents, a\nsuitable sales-recording method, and a filing calendar someone can actually\nexecute.\nTax administration becomes easier when you turn it into a routine: record\ntransactions daily, prepare returns early, fund payments, and retain the\nevidence. A Certificate of Registration is one part of that setup. It does not\nreplace invoice requirements, machine registration, bookkeeping, or the returns\nthat apply to your business.\nPhilippine tax rules and filing systems change. This chapter gives you the\noperating steps; a licensed CPA and your Revenue District Office (RDO) should\nconfirm your exact forms, coverage, elections, deadlines, and retention\nrequirements. Under EOPT invoicing rules, invoices are the primary sales\ndocuments for both goods and services. Official Receipts serve as supplementary\nevidence of payment or collection. Check current requirements before using an\nolder document template.\n\nStart here\n• Have a CPA confirm your registration and tax obligations.\n• Prepare compliant invoices and any required sales-system registration.\n• Set up daily recording, a 12-month calendar, and payment reserves.\nBEFORE YOU MOVE ON\n\n• Tax types, elections, and filing calendar checked\n• Compliant invoices ready and tested\n• Sales-system registration completed where required\n• Daily bookkeeping and reconciliation assigned\n• Withholding, reserves, and payment routes working\n• BIR folder and retention schedule established","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 97","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06 / YOUR CHECKLIST\n\nMark your progress\nCheck an item only when the work is complete and the evidence is saved.\n☐ 6.01 Put every tax deadline on one calendar\n☐ 6.02 Choose the tax treatment using your numbers\n☐ 6.03 Record transactions while the evidence is fresh\n☐ 6.04 Have compliant invoices ready before opening\n☐ 6.05 Use the right invoice for each transaction\n☐ 6.06 File the applicable VAT or percentage-tax return\n☐ 6.07 Compute quarterly income tax cumulatively\n☐ 6.08 Set up withholding on the payments that require it\n☐ 6.09 Prepare for a BIR compliance visit\n☐ 6.10 Choose a sales system that fits and complies\n☐ 6.11 Complete registration for the actual POS installation\n☐ 6.12 Train staff to invoice and handle voids consistently\n☐ 6.13 Understand compliance penalties before they arise\n☐ 6.14 Track growth before it changes your VAT obligations\n☐ 6.15 Arrange accounting help before the first deadline\n☐ 6.16 Keep a complete BIR records file\n\nKeep in mind\nTax compliance works best as a repeatable operating habit. Use the correct\ninvoices, keep records current, file the returns that apply, confirm payment, and\nkeep the evidence. Ask your CPA to review the setup whenever your activity,\nsize, or rules change.\nOne calendar, one organized records system, a daily bookkeeping habit, and\nclearly assigned professional support can make the work manageable. Build\nthose routines before the first sale so the team can concentrate on serving\ncustomers without leaving a trail of missing paperwork.","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 98","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06 • CHECKLIST 6.01\n\nPut every tax deadline on one\ncalendar\nDO THIS\n\nDuring the first week after BIR registration, build a 12-month calendar of the\nreturns, reports, payments, and supporting documents that apply to your\nbusiness.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nBusiness tax compliance is not just an annual April task. Different obligations\ncan follow monthly, quarterly, or annual schedules, and withholding has its own\nrequirements. A period with no sales may still require a return. Filing and\npaying are separate steps; completing one does not prove the other was done.\nMissed deadlines can create costs before you have earned enough to absorb\nthem.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nSit with your CPA and list each applicable form, filing frequency, deadline,\nrequired attachment, submission method, and payment channel. Use your\nregistration details and actual business activities to build the list rather than\ncopying another owner’s calendar. Identify who prepares, checks, submits, and\npays each item.\nPrint the calendar and put it where the responsible person sees it regularly. Set\nreminders two weeks before each due date so missing records and calculation\nquestions can be resolved while there is still time. Include a completion check\nthat saves both the filing acknowledgment and, when tax is due, the payment\nconfirmation.\nReview the calendar when rules or registration details change, and at the start\nof each tax year. Include every required nil return. If a deadline has already\nbeen missed, ask the CPA to calculate the actual exposure for your taxpayer\nclassification and advise on the appropriate correction. Do not assume a generic\nonline penalty range describes your case.\nDONE WHEN\n\nEvery obligation has an owner, due date, early reminder, submission route, and\na place to save proof of completion.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm your taxpayer-specific forms, filing dates, payment deadlines, and any\nannounced extensions with your CPA.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 99","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06 • CHECKLIST 6.02\n\nChoose the tax treatment using your\nnumbers\nDO THIS\n\nBefore your first filing, ask a licensed CPA to confirm your registered tax types\nand compare the options for which your business is actually eligible.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA tax election made quickly during registration may not suit your expected\nrevenue and costs. Common situations for sole proprietors include the eligible\nindividual’s 8% income-tax option, graduated income tax with applicable\npercentage tax, and VAT registration where required or appropriately chosen.\nThese are not interchangeable choices for every business structure. The result\ndepends on eligibility, deductions, revenue, and whether you also have\nemployment income.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nBring the Chapter 1 projections, expected expenses, business structure, and\nCertificate of Registration to the consultation. Ask for a side-by-side computation\nrather than a general statement that one option is always cheaper. Include any\nother income that affects how the rules apply to you.\nArrange a one-hour consultation within the first 30 days of registration, but\ncheck the election deadline immediately. The 8% election is annual, and a new\nbusiness’s initial-quarter rules differ from those for an existing taxpayer. Ask\nwhat filing or registration action makes the election valid and how it affects the\nyear’s later returns.\nAn illustrative consultation budget is ₱500–₱1,500; obtain a current quote and\nconfirm the scope. For the 8% option, ask about the ₱250,000 reduction\navailable under the rules for purely business/professional income and its\ndifferent treatment for mixed-income earners. Keep the explanation and filing\ninstructions with your registration records.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour eligibility, election deadline, and comparative tax calculation are checked,\nand your registration and first-filing instructions match the chosen treatment.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm tax-option eligibility, valid election steps, deadlines, and the treatment\nof any employment income.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 100","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06 • CHECKLIST 6.03\n\nRecord transactions while the\nevidence is fresh\nDO THIS\n\nBuild a daily bookkeeping routine, with regular professional review, so your\nregistered books and supporting records stay aligned with the business’s actual\nactivity.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA month-end backlog turns recording into reconstruction. Receipts go missing,\ncash differences become harder to explain, and the details behind supplier\npayments fade. Timely entries make it easier to detect mistakes while they can\nstill be checked. They also give you information for managing the business\nbefore a tax deadline arrives. The aim is a clear trail from the sale or expense to\nthe record and supporting document.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nReserve 15–20 minutes at close of business as a starting routine. Bring together\nthe day’s invoices, cash-drawer reconciliation, supplier documents, delivery\nrecords, and payment evidence. Record transactions using the approved method\nfor your books, and note anything needing clarification rather than guessing.\nIf a part-time bookkeeper maintains the books, agree on weekly visits or a\ndependable weekly handover instead of allowing a monthly pile-up. Someone\nstill needs to collect and organize source documents daily. Set a cutoff for\nresolving missing information before the filing preparer starts a return.\nAs transaction volumes grow, discuss whether a computerized accounting\nsystem will help. Start that conversation before the end of the first operating\nyear if the manual workload is becoming unmanageable. Confirm the BIR\nregistration, notification, or authorization requirements for your chosen system\nbefore changing methods; do not assume every spreadsheet or accounting\nsubscription is a registered replacement for your books.\nDONE WHEN\n\nDaily documents are organized, entries are current, discrepancies have an\nowner, and the bookkeeper’s review schedule is working.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nCheck requirements for your registered books and any planned computerized\naccounting system.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 101","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06 • CHECKLIST 6.04\n\nHave compliant invoices ready before\nopening\nDO THIS\n\nConfirm your invoicing setup with your CPA or RDO and complete any required\nAuthority to Print (ATP) and printing steps before the first sale.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA Certificate of Registration and the authority or registration for your sales\ndocuments are separate parts of compliance. Do not open on the assumption\nthat invoices can be arranged after a few days of trading. A printing delay can\nleave staff without the correct documents when you start serving customers.\nCheck that your documents meet current invoicing rules rather than copying the\nformat another business used years ago.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nStart the invoice discussion as soon as your registration details are available. If\nATP applies, confirm the current application route, including whether BIR Form\n1906 is appropriate, and choose an accredited printer without delay. Check your\nbusiness name, TIN, address, tax status, and required invoice information before\napproving a proof.\nAn illustrative schedule allows five to ten working days for ATP processing plus\nprinter production time, with at least three weeks between registration and\nopening. These are planning allowances, not service guarantees. Ask your RDO\nand printer for current lead times, then place the opening date after confirmed\navailability.\nFor a manual-invoice setup, consider at least two booklets: one active and one\nprotected backup. When they arrive, check serial-number ranges and particulars\nagainst the authorization, record the ranges in the BIR folder, and place the\nactive booklet at the cash point. Train staff before the first customer transaction.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe correct invoices are physically or electronically ready, the required\nauthority is confirmed, and staff know how to issue and safeguard them.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm current ATP, invoice-content, printer, serial-control, and\nelectronic-invoicing requirements.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 102","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06 • CHECKLIST 6.05\n\nUse the right invoice for each\ntransaction\nDO THIS\n\nHave your CPA confirm the correct invoice format for your sales of goods and\nservices, and distinguish the sales invoice from any supplementary payment\ndocument.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nUnder EOPT invoicing rules, invoices are the primary documents for sales of\nboth goods and services. An Official Receipt generally acknowledges payment or\ncollection as a supplementary document. VAT status, required particulars, and\nany applicable electronic-invoicing rules affect the format you need. Familiar\nlabels are not enough; make sure the document actually meets the rules for your\nbusiness and transaction.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nList the transactions the business will actually make. A retail store sells\nproducts; a salon supplies services and may also sell products; a café may have\nseveral sales categories. Show this list to the CPA or RDO before applying for\nprinted documents or configuring a sales system.\nAsk which primary invoice to issue, what details must appear, and when a\nseparate payment acknowledgment is useful. VAT taxpayers generally issue an\ninvoice for every sale. For non-VAT taxpayers, check the ₱500 threshold,\nbuyer-request rule regardless of amount, and daily consolidation requirements\nfor below-threshold sales. Issuing an invoice for every sale is a simple internal\ncontrol.\nCheck sample transactions before opening. Confirm that staff can identify the\ncorrect document, enter the required details, and retain the business copy. If\nyou already have older unused documents, ask about the current rules\ngoverning their use or replacement rather than relabeling them on your own.\nDONE WHEN\n\nEvery expected sales category has an approved invoice workflow, and\nsupplementary payment documents cannot be mistaken for the required sales\ninvoice.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nCheck invoice particulars, issuance rules, transitional stock rules, and any\nelectronic-invoicing coverage.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 103","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06 • CHECKLIST 6.06\n\nFile the applicable VAT or\npercentage-tax return\nDO THIS\n\nConfirm whether VAT or percentage tax applies, enroll in the required filing\nsystem, and submit every required return on time, including a nil return when\nrequired.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA quiet trading period does not automatically suspend filing obligations. Your\nregistration and applicable rules determine what is due, even when there is little\nor no tax to pay. Under the rules effective from January 2023, the standard VAT\nreturn is quarterly. Optional monthly VAT payment procedures do not create a\ngeneral requirement to file a monthly return; follow the process that applies to\nyour taxpayer category.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nAsk your CPA to confirm your VAT or percentage-tax coverage, including the\neffect of a valid 8% election if relevant. For quarterly VAT, confirm Form 2550Q\nand the deadline of 25 days after the close of the taxable quarter, subject to\napplicable rules or announced extensions. Confirm the current percentage-tax\nform and deadline separately.\nSet up the appropriate eBIRForms or eFPS access before the first filing is due.\nCheck whether your taxpayer category is required to use a particular system.\nTest the process using the correct software version and approved account\naccess; do not wait until the deadline to discover an enrollment problem.\nPair filing with an accepted payment channel where tax is due. Save the\nsubmission acknowledgment and payment evidence, and reconcile them to the\nreturn. A straightforward nil or low-activity filing may take under 30 minutes\nonce prepared, but allow time for checks, connection problems, and corrections\ninstead of leaving it to the final half-hour.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe correct return, frequency, system, and payment route are confirmed, with\nevidence saved for every required period.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm your VAT or percentage-tax obligations, required filing system, and\ncurrent deadlines.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 104","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06 • CHECKLIST 6.07\n\nCompute quarterly income tax\ncumulatively\nDO THIS\n\nUse a year-to-date computation for each required quarterly individual\nincome-tax return, then reconcile it to the annual return with your CPA.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nTreating every quarter as an isolated three-month business can produce the\nwrong tax result. Use the income and applicable deductions from the beginning\nof the year through the relevant quarter, with the proper credits and prior\npayments considered. Keep earlier returns and payment confirmations available\nso the next computation starts from reconciled year-to-date figures rather than\nan empty worksheet.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nFor the relevant individual taxpayer, confirm Form 1701Q for the first three\nquarters and the appropriate annual Form 1701 or 1701A. April 15 of the\nfollowing year is the usual annual deadline. Have your CPA confirm the forms,\ndates, and any current exceptions or extensions that apply to you.\nAt the first quarterly filing, ask the CPA to show you how the cumulative\ncalculation works for your chosen tax treatment. Check the year-to-date sales,\nexpenses where relevant, credits, and prior tax payments against the books.\nExplain unusual changes rather than carrying a mistaken figure into every later\nquarter.\nUse the reserve account from Chapter 4 to fund the obligation throughout the\nyear. Ask the CPA to recommend a practical weekly reservation percentage\nbased on projected revenue and tax treatment, then revisit it as actual\nperformance changes. A revenue percentage is a cash-planning tool, not a\nsubstitute for the legal computation. Keep the annual balance within the same\nfunding plan.\nDONE WHEN\n\nEach return reconciles to year-to-date records and prior payments, and the tax\nreserve is being adjusted toward the expected annual obligation.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the current income-tax forms, cumulative computation, credits, and\nfiling deadlines.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 105","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06 • CHECKLIST 6.08\n\nSet up withholding on the payments\nthat require it\nDO THIS\n\nBefore paying rent, professional fees, commissions, or another potentially\ncovered expense, ask your CPA whether expanded withholding tax applies and\nhow to document it.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nWithholding on payments to others is separate from your own income tax. Where\nrequired, the business must withhold the correct amount, pay the net amount to\nthe supplier, and remit and report the tax properly. Paying the gross amount\nbecause the supplier prefers it does not automatically remove your obligation.\nThe exact treatment depends on the payment, payee, payer status, and current\nrules.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nList recurring payments and ask the CPA to identify the rate and supporting\ninformation for each. Common rates to check include 5% on certain rentals and\n5% or 10% for certain individual professional fees. These are not blanket rates:\nthresholds, declarations, tax status, payer coverage, and exceptions must be\nreviewed.\nAgree the practical payment process with the payee before the first due date.\nShow the gross amount, any applicable withholding, and the resulting net\npayment. Prepare BIR Form 2307 when required so the payee has evidence for\nthe credit, using the correct details and issuance timing.\nPut the remittance and reporting deadlines into the calendar. Where applicable,\nForm 0619-E covers the first two months of the quarter and Form 1601-EQ\ncovers the quarterly return. Confirm eFPS or other filing-specific deadlines and\nrequired information reports. Reconcile withheld amounts, Form 2307\ncertificates, remittances, and the quarterly return before submission.\nDONE WHEN\n\nCovered payments have a checked rate and workflow, payees receive the\nrequired certificates, and every remittance and report is scheduled.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nCheck payer coverage, withholding rates, certificates, monthly remittances,\nquarterly returns, and reporting deadlines.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 106","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06 • CHECKLIST 6.09\n\nPrepare for a BIR compliance visit\nDO THIS\n\nCheck the basic controls a BIR field visit may examine: registration display,\ncompliant invoices, actual issuance, and accessible, current books and records.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nTax Mapping and closure-related enforcement are different processes with their\nown legal requirements. Do not treat an inspection as a guarantee of a fine or\nassume every minor issue leads directly to padlocking. What you can control is\nwhether ordinary transactions are documented and the supporting records are\navailable. Routine compliance is easier to maintain than an urgent cleanup after\na problem is identified.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nCheck the required display of your Certificate of Registration. Keep the active\nmanual invoice booklet at the cash point, or ensure the authorized electronic or\nmachine-based system is working. Keep a compliant backup arrangement for a\nsystem outage where needed. Staff should know where the documents are\nwithout waiting for the owner.\nUse a business rule of issuing the appropriate invoice for every sale. This is a\npractical control even where statutory thresholds or exceptions need separate\nchecking. Do not leave issuance to whether the customer remembers to ask.\nReconcile the day’s invoices or sales-system records to cash and other\npayments.\nKeep books current through the daily routine and a weekly completeness review.\nKnow where the legally required records are maintained and how to produce\nthem. If an officer arrives, verify identification and the authority or purpose of\nthe visit, involve the responsible owner or adviser, and respond using organized\nrecords. These practices reduce avoidable gaps; they do not guarantee an\ninspection outcome.\nDONE WHEN\n\nRegistration, invoicing, sales reconciliation, and record-access controls work\nduring an ordinary shift without improvised preparation.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm current display, invoice, record-access, and inspection requirements\nwith your CPA or RDO.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 107","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06 • CHECKLIST 6.10\n\nChoose a sales system that fits and\ncomplies\nDO THIS\n\nChoose a manual, cash-register, or POS arrangement that matches your\ntransaction volume, then verify the BIR requirements before buying or\nsubscribing.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA capable sales app is not automatically a compliant invoicing system. At the\nsame time, not every internal spreadsheet or management report has identical\nregistration requirements. Ask how the proposed system will be used: will it\nissue invoices, record official sales, maintain registered books, or simply support\ninternal analysis? That distinction matters when deciding which accreditation,\nregistration, or authorization steps apply.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nOption 1 is an approved manual-invoice setup with daily totals recorded in the\nbooks. For example, fewer than 20 transactions per day may be manageable\nmanually. This is a workflow guide, not a BIR threshold or exemption. Check\nwhether a manual setup is suitable for your actual obligations.\nOption 2 is an appropriate cash register machine from an authorized supplier,\nwith the required registration for your particular unit. Option 3 is a suitable POS\nsoftware installation with the applicable vendor and taxpayer documentation.\nAsk the vendor for evidence of the relevant accreditation or registration and a\nwritten explanation of what you must still complete.\nBefore committing, show the proposed arrangement to your CPA or RDO.\nConfirm invoice output, sales reports, serial controls, void handling, backups,\nand any e-invoicing coverage. Compare the total setup and recurring cost\nagainst the manual workload. A vendor’s assurance that “we are BIR-ready” is a\nstarting point for checking documents, not the final proof.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe chosen system fits the operation, and vendor evidence and taxpayer-specific\ncompliance steps are checked before purchase and first use.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nVerify the vendor’s documents and the requirements for your particular system,\nbranch, and taxpayer category.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 108","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06 • CHECKLIST 6.11\n\nComplete registration for the actual\nPOS installation\nDO THIS\n\nBefore the sales system processes a live transaction, complete the taxpayer- and\ninstallation-specific BIR steps that apply to your unit, software, and location.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nBuying from an accredited vendor does not automatically finish your own\ncompliance work. The unit or installation may need to be associated with your\nTIN, branch, business address, and other particulars under the current process.\nA relocation, replacement, or material system change may also require action.\nFind out before opening, while you still have time to correct the details or use a\nlawful alternative.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nAsk the vendor and CPA for the current registration checklist. Prepare the\nmachine’s serial number and specifications, your Certificate of Registration, the\nvendor’s documentation, and the correct application or online submission.\nConfirm whether Form 1907 and a Permit to Use apply to your installation;\ndifferent systems may follow different current procedures.\nAllow one to two weeks as an initial scheduling example, then replace it with a\ncurrent processing estimate. Put the application date and expected readiness\ndate into the opening schedule. Build in room for corrections to the application.\nDo not run customer transactions merely because the hardware has arrived.\nCheck the resulting permit, acknowledgment, or required registration evidence\nagainst the actual installation and location. Display or retain it as required.\nRecord the steps needed if the machine moves, is replaced, or fails. Have a\ncompliant backup transaction method ready so a pending application or\nequipment fault does not pressure staff into undocumented sales.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe actual installation has the required valid registration evidence, its\nparticulars are correct, and staff know the approved backup procedure.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the current installation-registration procedure, display rules, and\nrequirements for changes of location.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 109","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06 • CHECKLIST 6.12\n\nTrain staff to invoice and handle voids\nconsistently\nDO THIS\n\nMake correct invoice issuance part of every transaction-handling role. Train\nstaff on the complete workflow, including errors, voids, and system downtime.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA well-chosen sales system still depends on the person using it. Skipping a\ndocument because the sale is small, accepting unrecorded cash, or discarding an\nincorrect invoice breaks the trail between customer payments and business\nrecords. The owner needs controls that work during busy shifts and staff\nchanges. Training should explain the task and its reason without relying on\nthreats or unsupported claims about automatic closure.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nUse a clear operating instruction: “Record every sale and issue the proper\ninvoice before the customer leaves. If you make a mistake, follow the correction\nprocedure and ask the supervisor when you are unsure.” Apply that consistent\ninternal standard without confusing it with a statement that every statutory\nissuance rule has no threshold or exception.\nFor manual invoices, have the CPA or printer confirm the correct void process. A\npractical control is to mark all copies VOID, draw a diagonal line where\nappropriate, keep the copies intact, and retain the serial-number trail. Never\ndiscard a copy merely to make the booklet look tidy.\nFor POS transactions, use the authorized void or correction function and\npreserve its audit trail. Define who can approve a void and how it is reconciled\nat close. During training, test an ordinary sale, a small sale, a mistaken entry, a\ncustomer question, and a system failure. Check that each staff member can\ncomplete the process accurately.\nDONE WHEN\n\nEvery transaction handler has demonstrated correct invoicing, error handling,\nand reconciliation using the documented procedure.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nHave the manual and electronic invoice-correction procedures checked before\ntraining staff.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 110","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06 • CHECKLIST 6.13\n\nUnderstand compliance penalties\nbefore they arise\nDO THIS\n\nAsk your CPA to explain the current penalties relevant to your taxpayer category\nand build a simple prevention check around the obligations you actually have.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nRegistration failures, missing books, failure to issue required invoices, missing\nregistration displays, and late returns are preventable compliance risks. Tax\nreforms have changed important penalty rules, including relief for qualifying\nmicro and small taxpayers. Compromise amounts, surcharges, interest, and the\nunderlying tax are distinct matters. The cost in a particular case depends on the\nfacts and applicable law, so a generic fine list is not a reliable calculation.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nReview the current BIR issuances with your CPA. For each risk, record the\npreventive action and who checks it: registered operation, appropriate books,\ncompliant invoices, required displays, and timely returns and payments. Focus\nfirst on fixing the underlying gap rather than trying to guess a single maximum\nfine from a general guide.\nIf an obligation has already been missed, obtain a written computation\nexplaining the unpaid tax, surcharge, interest, and any proposed compromise\namount separately. Confirm your current taxpayer classification, including\nwhether the micro/small provisions of BIR RR 6-2024 apply. Ask about the\nproper correction and any lawful remedy rather than paying an unexplained\nfigure.\nUse the result to prioritize your weekly and monthly compliance review. A\ncurrent folder, daily recording, correct invoicing, and a reliable deadline\ncalendar reduce many avoidable exposures. They cannot eliminate every audit\nrisk or guarantee that a return contains no error, so keep professional review in\nthe routine.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYou know the current rules that apply to your classification, and each relevant\ncompliance risk has a practical prevention check.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nCheck the current penalty rules and taxpayer classification with your CPA before\nrelying on any amount.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 111","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06 • CHECKLIST 6.14\n\nTrack growth before it changes your\nVAT obligations\nDO THIS\n\nMonitor sales against the current VAT-registration tests and discuss\napproaching triggers with your CPA before a registration change becomes\nurgent.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nGrowth can change your tax obligations during the year. Waiting for the annual\nreturn may leave you responding late to a threshold issue. The applicable test\nmay include the relevant 12-month period and expected sales, so a simple\nJanuary-to-December total may not be enough. Confirm the current threshold,\nexclusions, timing, and registration steps for your business before relying on a\nsingle number.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nBuild a running sales tracker into your monthly bookkeeping from Day 1. For\nexample, ₱200,000 average monthly revenue annualizes to ₱2.4 million. The\nEOPT guidance states a ₱3 million baseline VAT threshold with periodic inflation\nadjustment provisions. Check the current operative threshold and relevant\nexclusions before using it in your tracker.\nTrack the period required by the rules, including a rolling 12-month view where\napplicable, and update the forward projection. Set 80% of the applicable\nthreshold as an early-warning point for CPA review. That signal gives you\nplanning time; it does not automatically mean voluntary VAT registration is the\nright decision.\nDiscuss the consequences of a move to VAT: pricing, customer profile, invoice\nconfiguration, input-tax records, filings, and cash flow. Voluntary registration\ncan have costs and restrictions as well as possible supply-chain advantages. Ask\nthe CPA whether it fits your situation and when action is legally required.\nDocument the advice and revise the sales system and financial model in time for\nthe effective change.\nDONE WHEN\n\nSales and projections are monitored against the correct tests, with an\nearly-review trigger and a documented plan for any required change.\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the operative VAT threshold, relevant sales tests, registration timing,\nand voluntary-registration consequences.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 112","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06 • CHECKLIST 6.15\n\nArrange accounting help before the\nfirst deadline\nDO THIS\n\nEngage the right combination of bookkeeping and CPA support before the first\nreturn is due, with a clear division of responsibilities.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA micro-business may not need a full-time accountant, but it still needs accurate\nrecords and timely, checked filings. Reconstructing several missed periods is\nusually more disruptive than maintaining records from the start. Do not assume\nthat hiring a quarterly tax preparer means someone is also handling daily\nrecords or monthly obligations. Confirm the coverage in writing so tasks do not\nfall between the owner, software, and adviser.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nFor a simple sole proprietorship, consider a CPA engagement for quarterly\nincome-tax returns, the annual return, and periodic tax planning. Use\n₱3,000–₱8,000 per quarter only as an illustrative budget. Obtain current quotes\nthat state which returns, consultations, corrections, and year-end work are\nincluded.\nPair that review with a part-time bookkeeper who checks records weekly, or a\nsuitable accounting platform managed by a trained owner or staff member.\nJuanTax is one product to evaluate for Philippine tax workflows. Confirm current\nfunctionality, pricing, data handling, and fit; a software subscription does not\nguarantee compliance.\nAgree the handover dates for invoices, expenses, bank statements, payroll, and\nwithholding information. Identify who prepares returns, who reviews them, who\nsubmits them, and who funds and confirms payments. Keep access appropriately\ncontrolled and retain copies of the final filings yourself. Review the arrangement\nwhen transaction volume or tax obligations change.\nDONE WHEN\n\nYour accountant, bookkeeper, and internal owner have an agreed scope,\nhandover schedule, deadline calendar, and evidence-retention process.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nCheck current fees, engagement scope, and software functionality before\nchoosing your accounting support.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 113","text":"CHAPTER 06 / BEFORE YOU SELL\n\nCHAPTER 06 • CHECKLIST 6.16\n\nKeep a complete BIR records file\nDO THIS\n\nCreate a structured BIR compliance file from registration onward and maintain\nit monthly, with secure backups and a checked retention schedule.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nGood records make it easier to explain the business’s actual compliance during a\nvisit or examination. They also help your CPA prepare accurate returns and find\nmistakes before they spread into later periods. A neat folder is useful because of\nwhat it contains and how well those records reconcile. It is not a substitute for\nthe underlying registration, proper invoice issuance, or payment of the correct\ntax.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nUse five sections. Registration: COR copy, ATP where applicable, and relevant\nPOS/CRM or system registration evidence, while displaying documents as\nrequired. Invoices: active and completed booklets or electronic records,\nincluding intact void records and serial controls. Books: current registered\nbooks and prior completed records. Filed returns: each submitted form and\nacknowledgment. Payments: receipts and online confirmations linked to the\nreturn or liability.\nKeep the active invoice available where transactions occur rather than locking it\naway for the sake of the folder. Update books daily and check completeness\nweekly. At month-end, add the latest filings and payments, reconcile missing\nitems, and confirm that someone other than the owner can locate the required\nrecords if authorized.\nApply the current statutory retention rule. EOPT provides a five-year baseline\nreckoned from the relevant filing deadline or actual filing date, as applicable.\nHave the CPA confirm the exact starting date and any longer preservation\nneeded for an unresolved examination, claim, or other requirement. Do not\ndiscard historical records solely because the folder is getting full.\nDONE WHEN\n\nAll five sections are current, required records can be retrieved promptly, and\nretention and backups follow a verified schedule.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm the five-year retention reckoning and any longer preservation needed\nfor unresolved matters.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 114","text":"CHAPTER 07 / BEFORE YOU SPEND YOUR FIRST PESO\n\nCHAPTER 07\n\nBefore You Spend\nYour First Peso\nProtect cash and rehearse the launch\n\nUse this chapter before final launch spending, and revisit it before opening. Your\npermits, lease, banking, hiring, and sales-document systems create the\nfoundation. The next decision is how much cash to put into the launch and how\nmuch to protect for the weeks that follow.\nVisible spending is tempting: finishing touches, tarpaulins, boosted posts, and an\nopening event. Working capital, training, and written procedures are less\nvisible, but they keep the business running when sales are quieter than hoped.\nGive them room in the budget first.\nThe figures in this chapter are planning examples and stress-test assumptions,\nnot promises about how every business will perform. Adapt them to supplier\nlead times, seasonality, product life, and actual costs. One principle holds: the\ngrand opening lasts a day. Your operating system needs to work every day after\nit.\n\nStart here\n• Separate and protect the operating reserve.\n• Map the first month of cash and fund the first two payrolls.\n• Run three rehearsal days and fix the gaps.\n• Set daily measures and book the 90-day review.\nBEFORE YOU MOVE ON\n\n• Operating reserve protected and setup budget funded\n• First-month cash gaps and first two payrolls covered\n• Three-day dry run completed and critical gaps fixed\n• Opening spend and offers within sustainable limits\n• Shift procedures and inventory triggers ready\n• Daily measures defined and 90-day review booked","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 115","text":"CHAPTER 07 / BEFORE YOU SPEND YOUR FIRST PESO\n\nCHAPTER 07 / YOUR CHECKLIST\n\nMark your progress\nCheck an item only when the work is complete and the evidence is saved.\n☐ 7.01 Protect working capital before launch spending\n☐ 7.02 Separate setup costs from ongoing obligations\n☐ 7.03 Run three rehearsal days before opening\n☐ 7.04 Set a firm opening-event ceiling\n☐ 7.05 Map the first 30 days of cash by week\n☐ 7.06 Start with a controlled inventory order\n☐ 7.07 Start with the local community you can reach\n☐ 7.08 Open at a price the business can sustain\n☐ 7.09 Write one checklist for each shift\n☐ 7.10 Set reorder points for the products that move\n☐ 7.11 Fund the first two payroll cycles in advance\n☐ 7.12 Measure three numbers from the first day\n☐ 7.13 Book the 90-day review before launch\n☐ 7.14 Make referrals easy and trackable\n☐ 7.15 Write the core operating system before you open\n\nKeep in mind\nYou have worked through demand, registration, premises, money, hiring, tax\ncompliance, and launch readiness. Each checklist turns an assumption into a\ndecision, a document, a test, or a repeatable routine. That work matters most\nafter opening, when the day becomes busy and the original excitement settles.\nProtect the reserve. Follow the daily checks. Watch the numbers. Review what\nthe business is showing you and act on it. Opening is a milestone; building a\ndependable business is the continuing work.\nFrom diskarte to systems. One checklist at a time. — PromdiPreneur\nBusiness first. Systems second. Technology third. Start with a process people\nunderstand and can follow; use technology only when it helps make the process\nmore consistent.","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 116","text":"CHAPTER 07 / BEFORE YOU SPEND YOUR FIRST PESO\n\nCHAPTER 07 • CHECKLIST 7.01\n\nProtect working capital before launch\nspending\nDO THIS\n\nSet aside the cash needed to keep the business operating before you budget\nfit-out upgrades, opening decorations, or other discretionary launch costs.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nWorking capital covers the timing gap between money going out and customer\npayments coming in. Suppliers may require cash on delivery, payroll arrives on\nschedule, and a small equipment failure may need immediate attention. If the\nreserve is simply whatever remains after setup, you may reach opening day with\nattractive premises and too little cash to run them. Protecting the reserve first\nmakes the limits of the rest of the budget visible.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nAs a starting planning target, calculate three full months of fixed operating\nexpenses. Consider aiming for six months where competition is strong, the\nlocation is untested, or the business is highly seasonal. These are planning\nbuffers, not universal minimums; test whether stock purchases, payment terms,\nand other cash needs require more.\nKeep the allocation separate and label its purpose clearly. Budget fit-out,\ninventory, and optional launch elements from the capital left after the reserve is\nfunded. Do not treat it as available cash for a nicer POS system or temporary\nborrowing that depends on next month’s hoped-for sales.\nWrite the conditions for using it: covering planned operating shortfalls and\nnecessary disruptions while the business moves toward sustainable cash\ngeneration. Pair every planned draw with the weekly cash-flow map in Item 5.\nReview the balance frequently so you can see how much runway remains and\nrespond before it is exhausted.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe reserve is separately funded, its permitted uses are clear, and the remaining\nlaunch budget can be met without quietly borrowing from it.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 117","text":"CHAPTER 07 / BEFORE YOU SPEND YOUR FIRST PESO\n\nCHAPTER 07 • CHECKLIST 7.02\n\nSeparate setup costs from ongoing\nobligations\nDO THIS\n\nPrepare a two-part pre-launch budget: one-time setup spending and the cash\nneeded for the first three months of operations. Fund both before committing to\noptional extras.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nPang-puhunan can become one large mental bucket that hides very different\ncommitments. Equipment and signage may be bought once, while rent, utilities,\npayroll, supplier payments, and contributions keep returning. Some costs also\ncontain both elements: an initial inventory purchase is a launch outlay, but\nreplenishment continues after opening. Separating the timing helps you see\nwhether the business is fully funded rather than merely ready to look open.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nColumn A: list the one-time or initial pre-opening outlays, including fit-out,\nequipment, signage, initial inventory, registration expenses, and essential\nopening costs. Identify anything that also has a later renewal, replacement, or\nsubscription cost so it is not mistaken for a permanent one-off.\nColumn B: list the first three months of recurring obligations. For steady\nmonthly costs, multiply by three; for variable purchases and unusual payment\ndates, use the expected cash schedule instead. Reconcile this amount to your\nworking-capital reserve so you do not double-count funding or omit a real\npayment.\nOnly the capital remaining after both columns are funded is available for\ncontingencies and optional launch additions. If there is no discretionary balance,\nremove the extras. If essential setup or the operating runway is still unfunded,\nreduce the launch scope or move the date. Do not solve a fit-out overrun by\nsilently cutting the reserve.\nDONE WHEN\n\nEvery launch peso belongs to a clear category, both essential setup and\noperating runway are funded, and discretionary spending has a real limit.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 118","text":"CHAPTER 07 / BEFORE YOU SPEND YOUR FIRST PESO\n\nCHAPTER 07 • CHECKLIST 7.03\n\nRun three rehearsal days before\nopening\nDO THIS\n\nPlan at least three consecutive dry-run days using real products, real\nprocedures, and the staff who will operate the business. Fix each day’s findings\nbefore the next test.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nA single relaxed afternoon with supportive family members may miss the\npressure points you need to discover. Problems often appear when several\ncustomers arrive together, an unusual question interrupts the queue, or opening\nand closing tasks have to happen on time. Rehearsals still consume stock, labor,\nand time, so budget for them. Their value is finding correctable failures before a\npublic opening makes those failures part of a customer’s first impression.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nDay 1: staff only. Run the full day from opening checks to transaction\nprocessing, cash reconciliation, cleaning, and closing. Watch for missing\ninstructions, awkward task order, and responsibilities nobody owns.\nDay 2: invite people from your personal and professional network to behave like\nactual customers. Use real products and realistic transactions at regular prices\nor a clearly defined nominal test price. Ask for candid feedback about waiting,\nclarity, service, and the buying experience. If money changes hands, use the\ncompliant transaction process.\nDay 3: stress-test the projected peak-hour volume, with everyone in their actual\noperating position. Check queue length, equipment, kitchen or service\nbottlenecks, and staffing capacity. After each day, hold a 30-minute structured\ndebrief. Record each gap, assign an owner, agree the fix, and confirm it has\nworked before moving on.\nDONE WHEN\n\nAll three scenarios have been tested, critical gaps are fixed and retested, and\nstaff can complete the full operating cycle.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 119","text":"CHAPTER 07 / BEFORE YOU SPEND YOUR FIRST PESO\n\nCHAPTER 07 • CHECKLIST 7.04\n\nSet a firm opening-event ceiling\nDO THIS\n\nChoose a maximum peso budget for the grand opening before planning starts.\nTell everyone involved what it includes and protect the operating reserve from\nadditions.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nOpening budgets often grow one reasonable suggestion at a time: a balloon arch\nbecomes a photo booth, a tarpaulin becomes a stage, or a small gathering\nbecomes a catered event. Each addition may look manageable on its own.\nTogether, they can consume cash needed for the next 90 days. A busy opening\nevent can also create an unrealistic expectation for ordinary trading, so judge it\nas a marketing activity with a limited budget.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nA possible discussion range for the opening event is 5–10% of total startup\ncapital. It is not an amount you must spend. Choose a lower ceiling when the\nnumbers require it. The event budget must leave the working-capital reserve\nintact and fit what the business can afford to risk on customer acquisition.\nList what the event is meant to achieve: first-time local customers, a clear\nintroduction to the offer, and a good first impression. Price the essential\nelements first. Give the same written ceiling to staff, family helpers, suppliers,\nand anyone handling invitations so guest-list changes do not quietly become\nspending approval.\nTrack committed costs against the ceiling as quotes are accepted. If someone\nproposes an addition, identify what will be removed or reduced to pay for it.\nCheck that it helps the customer objective rather than simply making the event\nlook bigger. Record actual spending afterward and compare it with the\nmeasurable customer response.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe event has a fixed funded ceiling, all commitments fit inside it, and no\nopening expense reduces the protected operating reserve.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 120","text":"CHAPTER 07 / BEFORE YOU SPEND YOUR FIRST PESO\n\nCHAPTER 07 • CHECKLIST 7.05\n\nMap the first 30 days of cash by week\nDO THIS\n\nBefore opening, prepare a weekly cash-flow map for the first month. Identify\nwhen cash leaves, when it is expected to arrive, and which gaps the reserve\nmust cover.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nOpening-week interest may not continue at the same level. A quieter second or\nthird week does not automatically mean the business has failed, but it still\naffects the ability to pay bills. Planning a lower-sales scenario gives you time to\ndecide how to respond. It also reduces the temptation to slash prices or\noverreact to a few days of data before you understand what changed.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nBuild a base case and a conservative case. One stress test puts Week 1 at the\nestimated baseline, then models Weeks 2 and 3 at 40–60% of Week 1. Use those\nfigures as one scenario, not a prediction for every shop. Adjust for your market,\nopening offer, and expected seasonality.\nList each known cash outflow in the week it will be paid: supplier\ncash-on-delivery purchases, the first payroll, outstanding utility deposits, rent or\nother fixed charges, and any remaining pre-opening bills. Keep timing separate\nfrom the accounting month; a bill can be profitable on paper and still create a\ncash gap on its due date.\nCalculate the weekly opening balance, inflows, outflows, and closing cash. Mark\nplanned reserve draws and update the map with actual results each week.\nInvestigate changes before making a major price or inventory decision.\nDONE WHEN\n\nEvery first-month payment has a funding source, expected shortfall weeks are\nidentified, and the reserve can cover the tested scenario.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 121","text":"CHAPTER 07 / BEFORE YOU SPEND YOUR FIRST PESO\n\nCHAPTER 07 • CHECKLIST 7.06\n\nStart with a controlled inventory\norder\nDO THIS\n\nUse a cautious opening order to preserve cash and learn what customers\nactually buy, while protecting essential products against predictable stock-outs.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nFull shelves and a bigger bulk discount can feel reassuring before opening. They\ncan also tie up cash in products that move slowly at your specific location.\nSupplier minimum order quantities may be designed for larger buyers, and mas\nmalaking order, mas malaking diskwento does not automatically mean a better\ncash decision. Compare the discount with the cost of holding stock, the risk of\nspoilage, and the operating cash you give up.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nTest an opening order at 60–70% of the quantity you initially think you need.\nTreat this as a conservative scenario for suitable products, not a blanket rule.\nCheck supplier lead times, minimum orders, shelf life, essential assortment, and\nthe cost of a stock-out before reducing any line.\nGive the opening order three jobs: preserve cash for the first 30 days, reveal\nwhich products sell at the location, and establish the habit of reordering from\nactual sales. Avoid purchasing maximum variety solely to fill display space. A\nsmaller, considered opening range can be easier to monitor and replenish.\nReview sales and stock movement frequently in the first month. Separate\ngenuinely slow-moving products from items that simply were unavailable when\ncustomers wanted them. Use the reorder points in Item 10 to replace what sells\nbefore it runs out. Keep a record of why you changed quantities so the next\norder reflects evidence rather than opening-week excitement.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe opening order preserves operating cash, covers essential availability, and\nhas a practical replenishment plan based on actual early sales.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 122","text":"CHAPTER 07 / BEFORE YOU SPEND YOUR FIRST PESO\n\nCHAPTER 07 • CHECKLIST 7.07\n\nStart with the local community you\ncan reach\nDO THIS\n\nBuild pre-launch awareness in the immediate community, then use paid\nadvertising where it can amplify a message that is already attracting the right\npeople.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nOnline reach and actual store visits are different measures. Consider an\nillustrative campaign: ₱5,000 spent, 15,000 impressions, 300 interactions, and\n20 opening-day visitors. That means ₱250 per recorded visitor if those visits are\ngenuinely attributable to the ad. These figures are an example, not a\nperformance benchmark. Measure the outcome that matters to the shop rather\nthan treating impressions as sales.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nUse four practical channels. Put a readable “Opening Soon” tarpaulin on the\npremises where nearby customers pass. Send personal introductions to real\ncommunity contacts; 50 individual messages can be a manageable starting\neffort. Compare the actual response with paid reach, rather than assuming\n10,000 ad impressions are automatically more valuable.\nPost in relevant local Facebook groups where business announcements are\nallowed. Choose a clear opening hook people can share: a first-50-customers\noffer, a genuine community element, or an agreed local-personality visit. Check\nthe cost and terms of any offer against your margin.\nWhere appropriate, ask for introductions through local chambers of commerce,\nFilipino-Chinese chambers, alumni groups, or professional associations.\nApproach them as a new community business rather than assuming membership\nor endorsement. Track where visitors heard about you, then use paid advertising\nto test or expand the channels that produce relevant interest.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe nearby audience knows what is opening, where, and when, and each\nmarketing channel has a way to track real customer response.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 123","text":"CHAPTER 07 / BEFORE YOU SPEND YOUR FIRST PESO\n\nCHAPTER 07 • CHECKLIST 7.08\n\nOpen at a price the business can\nsustain\nDO THIS\n\nCalculate the regular selling price before launch and make it the clear baseline\nfrom the first transaction. Cost any opening offer before announcing it.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nHeavy introductory discounts can attract customers whose expectations are\nbuilt around a price you cannot maintain. Raising the price later may then feel\nlike a loss to them, even if the original offer never covered your costs. That does\nnot mean every promotion is wrong. It means the price, duration, economics,\nand transition need to be deliberate instead of relying on mura muna para\nsumikat and hoping the margins will improve later.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nUse the costing work from earlier chapters to include landed cost, a realistic\nshare of overhead, and the target margin. Check that your expected transaction\nmix and volume can support the resulting price. Show the regular price clearly\nso customers understand the normal offer.\nIf you want opening excitement, consider a costed value-add: a free item with a\nqualifying first purchase, bonus starting points on a loyalty card, or a convenient\nbundle sold at the regular individual prices. These options still have a cost;\ninclude it in the opening budget and check that the offer is accurately described.\nIf you choose a discount, define the end date and terms before it begins,\ncommunicate the regular price, and know the cash and margin limit. Track what\ncustomers buy and whether they return at normal prices. Avoid making a\npermanent pricing change in response to one quiet week without reviewing\nvolume, customer feedback, and contribution margin together.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe regular price supports the financial model, and every opening incentive has\nclear terms, a funded cost, and a defined limit.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 124","text":"CHAPTER 07 / BEFORE YOU SPEND YOUR FIRST PESO\n\nCHAPTER 07 • CHECKLIST 7.09\n\nWrite one checklist for each shift\nDO THIS\n\nPrepare separate opening, mid-day, and closing checklists before Day 1. Make\nevery instruction specific enough that someone can complete and verify it\nwithout interpreting your intentions.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nVerbal training is easily remembered differently by different people. When the\nowner is away or the shop is busy, vague instructions leave gaps in cleaning,\nstock checks, cash handling, and closing. A short checklist makes the expected\nresult visible. It also gives a supervisor a fair way to check the work and spot\nwhere the procedure itself needs improvement.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nKeep each shift’s checklist to a usable page. Replace “clean the store” with\nconcrete tasks: wipe counter surfaces with the appropriate sanitizing solution,\nsweep and mop the floor, empty and reline trash bins, and refill the condiment\nand packaging station to marked levels. Follow the product and workplace\nsafety instructions for the actual cleaning materials.\nReplace “check inventory” with a specific count of the top ten fastest-moving\nitems. Record quantities against the reorder sheet and flag any item at or below\nits reorder point. Include cash-reconciliation and security steps in the closing\nlist, with clear instructions on who handles exceptions.\nHave the staff member sign or acknowledge completion, then have the shift\nsupervisor review it. Test the checklist during the dry run and remove ambiguity\nwhen someone gets stuck. Keep it accessible at the point of work and update the\ncontrolled copy when the process changes. A signed box is useful only when it\nreflects work that was actually checked.\nDONE WHEN\n\nEach shift has a tested checklist with specific tasks, responsible staff, a\nverification step, and a clear route for reporting exceptions.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 125","text":"CHAPTER 07 / BEFORE YOU SPEND YOUR FIRST PESO\n\nCHAPTER 07 • CHECKLIST 7.10\n\nSet reorder points for the products\nthat move\nDO THIS\n\nBefore the first delivery arrives, calculate a starting reorder point for your\nhigh-volume products and build the check into the daily closing routine.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nOrdering only when a shelf looks nearly empty ignores the time a supplier needs\nto deliver. A supplier lead time of three to seven days, for example, can leave\nyou out of stock after a late reorder. Early stock-outs disappoint customers while\nyou are establishing reliability. A quantity-based trigger gives you a visible\naction point before the shelf is empty.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nStart with this formula: average daily sales multiplied by supplier lead time in\ndays equals expected demand during the lead time. If a product sells 20 units\nper day and delivery takes five days, that demand is 100 units. In this simple\nexample, reaching 100 units triggers the purchase order.\nRecognize the assumption: the calculation has no safety allowance for demand\nspikes, late deliveries, missed order cutoffs, or stock discrepancies. Add a\nreasoned buffer where needed, balanced against shelf life and cash limits.\nAccount for stock already ordered so the same trigger does not create duplicate\npurchases.\nSet initial figures for the top ten products, then revise them using actual\nfirst-month sales and delivery performance. Put the closing count and reorder\nflag into the shift checklist. Identify who approves and places the order, when\nthe supplier must receive it, and how the expected delivery is recorded and\nchecked against the purchase order.\nDONE WHEN\n\nTop products have documented reorder triggers, an order owner, and daily\nchecks that allow replenishment before the tested stock-out point.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 126","text":"CHAPTER 07 / BEFORE YOU SPEND YOUR FIRST PESO\n\nCHAPTER 07 • CHECKLIST 7.11\n\nFund the first two payroll cycles in\nadvance\nDO THIS\n\nBefore the first employee starts, choose a lawful, fixed payroll schedule and\nprotect the money for the first two full pay cycles within the operating reserve.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nStaff should not have to guess when wages will arrive based on that week’s\nsales. Reliable payment lets people plan their own expenses and supports trust\nduring the unsettled early months. Wage-payment frequency and timing are also\nlegal obligations, so the payroll calendar needs to be checked rather than built\naround convenience alone. An opening cash shortage does not make an informal\ndelay a sound staffing practice.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nThe 15th and the last day of the month are common payroll dates. Have your\npayroll adviser confirm the lawful intervals and arrangements for your\nworkforce, including what happens when a scheduled payday falls on a\nnon-working day. Explain the agreed schedule before employment begins and\nrecord the actual dates for the first two cycles.\nCalculate Payroll 1 and Payroll 2 using the expected hours, applicable benefits\nor premiums, lawful deductions, and the full employer cost. Allocate the cash\nbefore assuming any opening revenue will be available. Link related employer\ncontributions and benefit reserves to their own due dates so funding net wages\ndoes not leave the associated obligations uncovered.\nSet a preparation cutoff: attendance reviewed, adjustments checked, payslips\nproduced, payments authorized, and confirmation saved. Test the payment route\nbefore the first payday. If an operational problem appears, address it promptly\nthrough the lawful process and communicate clearly rather than making staff\nrepeatedly chase the owner for payment.\nDONE WHEN\n\nTwo complete payroll cycles are funded, the legal schedule is confirmed, and the\nteam responsible can prepare, pay, and document the first payday.\n\nVERIFY BEFORE USE • Official sources\n\nConfirm lawful wage-payment intervals and payday arrangements for\nnon-working days with your payroll adviser.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 127","text":"CHAPTER 07 / BEFORE YOU SPEND YOUR FIRST PESO\n\nCHAPTER 07 • CHECKLIST 7.12\n\nMeasure three numbers from the first\nday\nDO THIS\n\nSet daily targets for revenue, average transaction value, and transaction count\nbefore opening. Record the actual figures at close and review the pattern each\nweek.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\n“Okay naman” and “may tao” can describe a mood without showing whether the\nbusiness is meeting the assumptions that justified opening. The same sales may\nfeel exciting in Week 1 and disappointing in Week 4. Consistent measures give\nyou a steadier comparison and help you identify whether a gap comes from\ncustomer volume, spending per transaction, or both. They work best alongside\ncash and operational observations.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nDaily revenue: compare recorded sales with the target from the Chapter 1\nbreak-even model. Use a consistent definition and reconcile the number with the\nbooks or POS. Average transaction value: divide the relevant sales total by the\nnumber of transactions to see whether the buying pattern matches the model.\nDaily transaction count: record completed sales and compare the pattern with\nthe pre-opening foot-traffic or shadow-count assumptions. Transactions are only\na proxy for customer numbers: one person may make multiple purchases, and\none invoice may cover a group. Track actual visitors separately if you need a\nconversion rate.\nSet an early-review rule: a miss of more than 20% on any target for three\nconsecutive weeks deserves investigation. This is a management trigger, not a\nuniversal failure test. Check seasonality, opening effects, availability, service,\nand pricing, then choose a specific change and measure its result.\nDONE WHEN\n\nTargets and definitions are written, daily figures are recorded consistently, and\nweekly reviews turn material gaps into specific questions and actions.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 128","text":"CHAPTER 07 / BEFORE YOU SPEND YOUR FIRST PESO\n\nCHAPTER 07 • CHECKLIST 7.13\n\nBook the 90-day review before launch\nDO THIS\n\nReserve a half-day 90 days after the planned opening date and prepare a\none-page review template now. Use the meeting to decide what the evidence\ncalls for next.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nAt roughly three months, you should have more useful operating evidence than\nyou had before launch. That is a good point to compare the original assumptions\nwith actual performance, although seasonality and the business model may\nmean the full picture takes longer. The purpose is to distinguish a market\nassumption that needs revising from an operational process that is failing to\ndeliver. Either finding can lead to a practical change.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nBlock the calendar as “90-Day Business Review.” Prepare six sections: monthly\nactual versus projected revenue for each of the three months; actual daily\ncustomer or transaction count against the shadow-count assumptions, with\nconsistent definitions; actual average transaction value versus the model; the\nthree main operational problems and how each was addressed; the three things\nthat worked better than expected; and an assessment of the overall trajectory.\nBring the completed template, underlying records, and the Business Concept\nSummary from Chapter 1. Highlight where evidence supports the plan and\nwhere the plan depended on an assumption that did not hold. Separate a\ntemporary disruption from a repeated pattern before committing to a major\nchange.\nFinish with decisions, owners, and dates. Strengthen what is working, repair\nweak processes, and reconsider structural assumptions where needed. Update\nthe cash forecast and operating instructions to match those decisions. Schedule\nthe next review so the document becomes part of how you run the business\nrather than a file you never reopen.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe review is booked, the six-section template is ready, and responsibility for\ngathering the supporting figures is assigned.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 129","text":"CHAPTER 07 / BEFORE YOU SPEND YOUR FIRST PESO\n\nCHAPTER 07 • CHECKLIST 7.14\n\nMake referrals easy and trackable\nDO THIS\n\nChoose one simple referral mechanism before opening and include it in the\nnormal customer interaction, with a clear offer and a way to measure use.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nHappy customers may recommend you, but an easy prompt can help them know\nwhat to share and what the referred person should do. In a local business, a\nrecommendation from someone nearby can be valuable because it carries\npersonal experience. Do not assume it always outperforms paid advertising or\nneeds no management. The offer still needs to be affordable, understandable,\nand consistently honored.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nA practical starting point is a physical referral card with the business name,\nlocation or contact details, a clear reward, and a unique tracking code. Define\nwho receives the reward and what event qualifies: for example, a referred\ncustomer visiting and presenting the card under the stated terms. Cost the\nreward before printing.\nTrain staff to offer the card as a standard closing step after a completed\ntransaction. Explain the benefit briefly, without pressuring customers. Think\nabout your first 50 customers: nearby early buyers may introduce the business\nto their networks, but referrals still depend on a good experience and an offer\nworth sharing.\nKeep a small redemption log and reconcile rewards so the same card is not\ncounted incorrectly. Review which referrals lead to real customers and whether\nthe margin supports the offer. Other options include a bring-a-friend offer or a\ngenuinely shareable community benefit, but start with one mechanism the team\ncan execute reliably before adding complexity.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe referral offer is costed, printed or ready to share, included in staff\ninstructions, and supported by a simple redemption record.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 130","text":"CHAPTER 07 / BEFORE YOU SPEND YOUR FIRST PESO\n\nCHAPTER 07 • CHECKLIST 7.15\n\nWrite the core operating system\nbefore you open\nDO THIS\n\nDocument the recurring work in plain language, test it with someone who will\nuse it, and correct the gaps before opening the doors.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n\nDiskarte is useful when an unexpected problem needs a practical response.\nDaily operations also need repeatable instructions so every decision does not\nwait for the owner. A promising idea, adequate capital, a favorable location, and\ncommunity support still need dependable execution. Written procedures help\nthe team carry out the plan and give you a place to capture improvements as\nreal trading reveals what needs to change.\nMAKE IT PRACTICAL\n\nDocument the full customer transaction: arrival, order or product selection,\npayment, invoice issuance, and cash reconciliation at close. Record how stock is\ncounted, how reorder points trigger action, how purchase orders are approved,\nand how deliveries are received and checked against the order.\nWrite the payroll preparation and payment steps, customer-complaint process,\nand daily cleaning and maintenance standards. Give each task an owner, the\nneeded inputs, the expected result, and a route for exceptions. Link the short\nshift checklists to fuller instructions where a task needs more detail.\nAsk a new or unfamiliar staff member to follow the instructions during a\nrehearsal. Watch where they hesitate, guess, or need to call you. Clarify those\npoints and test again. The goal is reliable routine work and sensible escalation,\nnot a claim that nobody will ever have a question. Keep the first version usable,\nthen revise it with evidence from daily operations and the 90-day review.\nDONE WHEN\n\nThe core routines are written, tested by their users, and clear enough to run\nconsistently with defined exceptions and escalation.\n← Chapter checklist","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 131","text":"APPENDIX\n\nAPPENDIX\n\nTemplates &\npractical tools\nThree starting frameworks for important business conversations: opening a\ncorporate bank account, negotiating a lease and engaging an employee on\nprobation.\n\n01 Board resolution for a corporate bank account\n02 Letter of intent to lease commercial space\n03 Probationary employment contract framework\nREVIEW BEFORE USE\n\nThese are editable frameworks, not ready-to-sign documents. Complete every\nfield and obtain professional review before sending, signing or submitting.\n\nPrepare each document in five steps\n• Replace every [BRACKETED FIELD] with accurate information. Delete\nalternatives that do not apply.\n• Confirm the current legal requirements and the recipient’s preferred format.\n• Read every clause, including the powers, payment terms and obligations it\ncreates.\n• Have the completed document reviewed by the appropriate Philippine\nprofessional. For bank documents, confirm the bank’s requirements too.\n• Remove preparer notes from the final document, obtain the required approvals\nand signatures, and give each party the appropriate copy.\n\nA signed template can create real obligations. Examples of deposit amounts,\nrent increases, performance standards and review dates are planning or\nnegotiation choices, not legal safe harbors.","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 132","text":"A QUICK REFERENCE\n\nA QUICK REFERENCE\n\nKnow the names\nBIR / RDO\nBureau of Internal Revenue / Revenue District Office. Your tax registration and\nadministration contacts.\n\nDTI / SEC\nDepartment of Trade and Industry / Securities and Exchange Commission. The\nregistration route depends on your business structure.\n\nLGU / BPLO\nLocal government unit / Business Permits and Licensing Office. Check the\nrequirements of the city or municipality where the business operates.\n\nBFP\nBureau of Fire Protection. Confirm the fire-safety requirements for the premises\nand activity.\n\nDOLE / NWPC\nDepartment of Labor and Employment / National Wages and Productivity\nCommission. Check labor requirements and the applicable regional wage order.\n\nSSS / PhilHealth / Pag-IBIG\nSocial Security System / Philippine Health Insurance Corporation / Home\nDevelopment Mutual Fund. Confirm employer and employee coverage,\nregistration and current contribution rules.\n\nOPC\nOne Person Corporation. A corporate structure with one stockholder, subject to\nthe rules that apply to OPCs.\n\nSOP / POS\nStandard operating procedure / point-of-sale system. One documents how the\nwork is done; the other records or processes sales.\n\nLOI / CUSA\nLetter of intent / common-use or common-area service charges. Define the\nactual scope and charges in your lease documents.","weight":1},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 133","text":"TEMPLATE 1\n\nTEMPLATE 1\n\nCorporate bank account\nboard resolution\nUse this framework to discuss corporate account-opening authority with your\nbank. Ask the branch for its current resolution or Secretary’s Certificate form\nand documentary checklist. Ordinary corporations and One Person Corporations\nuse different governance procedures; use the wording appropriate to the entity.\nBefore submitting, have corporate counsel or the Corporate Secretary confirm\nvalid authorization, quorum and voting rules, certification, signatories and any\nnotarial requirements. Confirm the bank’s copy and identification requirements;\ndo not assume all directors must sign.\n\nBOARD RESOLUTION\n[FULL REGISTERED CORPORATE NAME] SEC Registration No.: [SEC\nREGISTRATION NUMBER] Principal Office: [COMPLETE REGISTERED\nBUSINESS ADDRESS]\n\nRESOLUTION OF THE BOARD OF DIRECTORS\n[FOR AN OPC: WRITTEN RESOLUTION OF THE SINGLE STOCKHOLDER\nAND SOLE DIRECTOR]\n[ORDINARY CORPORATION: At a duly convened meeting of the Board of\nDirectors of [CORPORATE NAME] held on [DATE] at [PLACE], with the required\nquorum present, the following resolutions were adopted in accordance with\napplicable law and the corporation’s governing documents.]\n[OPC: I, [NAME], being the single stockholder and sole director of [OPC NAME],\nadopt the following written resolutions on [DATE]. These resolutions shall be\nsigned, dated and recorded in the corporation’s minutes book as required by\nlaw.]\n[SELECT AND COMPLETE THE APPLICABLE VERSION; DELETE THE OTHER.]\nRESOLVED, that the Corporation be, and it is hereby, authorized to open and\nmaintain a [TYPE OF ACCOUNT - e.g., Savings Account / Checking Account /\nCurrent Account] with [FULL LEGAL NAME OF BANK], [BRANCH NAME],\nlocated at [COMPLETE BRANCH ADDRESS] (hereinafter referred to as the\n\"Bank\");","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 134","text":"TEMPLATE 1 / CONTINUED\n\nRESOLVED FURTHER, that the following named officer/s and/or director/s of\nthe Corporation be, and they are hereby, authorized to sign, endorse, execute,\nand deliver, for and on behalf of the Corporation, any and all checks, drafts,\nwithdrawal slips, orders for payment of money, deposit slips, and such other\nbanking instruments and documents as may be necessary in connection with the\noperation and maintenance of the said bank account:\n\nName\n\nPosition/Title\n\nSpecimen Signature\n\n[FULL NAME OF\nSIGNATORY 1]\n\n[POSITION]\n\n_________________\n\n[FULL NAME OF\nSIGNATORY 2]\n\n[POSITION]\n\n_________________\n\n[FULL NAME OF\nSIGNATORY 3, IF\nAPPLICABLE]\n\n[POSITION]\n\n_________________\n\nPREPARER NOTE\nList all individuals you intend to authorize as account signatories. Include only\nindividuals whose names and signatures you are comfortable presenting to the\nbank as authorized representatives of the corporation.\nRESOLVED FURTHER, that the signing arrangement for all transactions on the\nsaid bank account shall be as follows:\n[SPECIFY ONE OF THE FOLLOWING SIGNING ARRANGEMENTS - DELETE\nTHE ONES THAT DO NOT APPLY AND CUSTOMIZE THE REMAINING ONE:]\nOption A - Single Signatory: \"Any ONE (1) of the above-named authorized\nsignatories, acting alone, shall be authorized to sign on behalf of the\nCorporation for all transactions on the said account.\"\nOption B - Joint Signatories for All Transactions: \"Any TWO (2) of the\nabove-named authorized signatories, acting jointly, shall be required to sign on\nbehalf of the Corporation for all transactions on the said account.\"\nOption C - Tiered Signing Authority: \"Any ONE (1) of the above-named\nauthorized signatories, acting alone, shall be authorized to sign on behalf of the\nCorporation for transactions not exceeding Philippine Peso [AMOUNT IN\nFIGURES] (PhP [AMOUNT IN WORDS]). For transactions exceeding that\namount, any TWO (2) of the above-named authorized signatories must sign\njointly.\"\nPREPARER NOTE","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 135","text":"TEMPLATE 1 / CONTINUED\n\nChoose the signing arrangement that reflects your actual internal control\nrequirements. A sole signatory arrangement is operationally simpler for\nmicro-businesses. A joint signatory arrangement provides stronger internal\ncontrols for businesses where separation of duties is important. Confirm with\nyour specific bank branch which arrangements they accept before finalizing this\nclause.\nRESOLVED FURTHER, that the Corporate Secretary of the Corporation be, and\nis hereby, authorized and directed to certify to the Bank the foregoing\nresolutions, to furnish such specimen signatures of the authorized signatories as\nmay be required, and to provide such other documents and information as may\nbe required by the Bank in connection with the opening and maintenance of the\nsaid account;\n[OPTIONAL; INCLUDE ONLY AFTER BANK AND COUNSEL REVIEW] [SET\nOUT ANY BANK-REQUESTED RELIANCE OR AUTHORITY CLAUSE, WITH THE\nSCOPE DELIBERATELY APPROVED BY THE CORPORATION.]\n[OPTIONAL; INCLUDE ONLY AFTER REVIEW] RESOLVED FINALLY, that the\nfollowing specifically identified prior acts relating to opening this account are\nratified and approved: [LIST THE ACTS AND DOCUMENTS, OR DELETE THIS\nCLAUSE].\nIN WITNESS WHEREOF, I/We have hereunto set our hands this [DATE] at\n[CITY/MUNICIPALITY], Philippines.\n\nBOARD OF DIRECTORS / SOLE INCORPORATOR:\nSignature over Printed Name: _________________________________ Name: [FULL\nNAME] Position: [POSITION/TITLE] Date: ___________________________\nSignature over Printed Name: _________________________________ Name: [FULL\nNAME] Position: [POSITION/TITLE] Date: ___________________________\nPREPARER NOTE\nUse the approval, certification and signature format required by law, the\ncorporation’s governing documents and the receiving bank. Board approval does\nnot universally require every director’s signature. An OPC records its single\nstockholder’s written resolution under the rules applicable to OPCs.\n\nCERTIFICATION BY THE CORPORATE SECRETARY\nI, [FULL NAME OF CORPORATE SECRETARY], Corporate Secretary of [FULL\nREGISTERED CORPORATE NAME], hereby certify that:\nThe foregoing is a true, complete and correct copy of the resolutions duly\nadopted on [DATE] in accordance with applicable law and the corporation’s\ngoverning documents;","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 136","text":"TEMPLATE 1 / CONTINUED\n\n[ORDINARY CORPORATION: The required quorum was present throughout the\nmeeting.] [OPC: The single stockholder and sole director signed and dated the\nwritten resolution, which was recorded in the minutes book on [DATE].]\nSaid resolutions have not been amended, modified, revoked, or rescinded as of\nthe date of this Certification and remain in full force and effect;\nThe persons named above as authorized signatories are duly elected or\nappointed officers and/or directors of the Corporation as of the date of this\nCertification.\nSignature over Printed Name: _________________________________ Name: [FULL\nNAME OF CORPORATE SECRETARY] Corporate Secretary [FULL REGISTERED\nCORPORATE NAME] Date: ___________________________\n\nACKNOWLEDGMENT (To be completed by a duly commissioned\nNotary Public)\nREPUBLIC OF THE PHILIPPINES [PROVINCE/CITY] ) S.S.\nBEFORE ME, a Notary Public for and in [CITY/MUNICIPALITY/PROVINCE],\npersonally appeared the following individuals with their competent proof of\nidentity:\n\nName\n\nGovernment\nID Type\n\nID Number\n\nIssued By\n\nDate Issued\n\n[FULL NAME]\n\n_______________\n_\n\n_______________\n_\n\n_______________\n_\n\n_______________\n_\n\n[FULL NAME]\n\n_______________\n_\n\n_______________\n_\n\n_______________\n_\n\n_______________\n_\n\nknown to me and known to be the same persons who executed the foregoing\nBoard Resolution and Certification, and each of them acknowledged to me that\nthe same was their free and voluntary act and deed, and the free and voluntary\nact and deed of the corporation they represent.\nWITNESS MY HAND AND NOTARIAL SEAL on [DATE] at [PLACE OF\nNOTARIZATION], Philippines.\n\nNOTARY PUBLIC\nName: _________________________________ Notarial Commission valid until:\nDecember 31, [YEAR] PTR No.: ___________ / Date: ___________ / Place: ___________\nIBP Lifetime Membership No. / IBP No.: ___________ Roll of Attorneys No.:\n___________ MCLE Compliance No.: ___________\nDoc. No.: _______ Page No.: _______ Book No.: _______ Series of _______","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 137","text":"TEMPLATE 2\n\nTEMPLATE 2\n\nLetter of intent\nto lease\nUse this Letter of Intent before signing a formal lease to record your proposed\nterms and open negotiations. Its legal effect depends on its wording and the\nparties’ conduct. Have counsel confirm that it reflects the intended non-binding\nnegotiation stage.\nHow to use it most effectively: Present this LOI in person - bring a printed copy\nto a meeting with the lessor rather than sending it by message alone. The\nconversation that follows the presentation is where the actual negotiation\nhappens. The LOI documents what was agreed in that conversation and prevents\nthe \"I thought we agreed\" disputes that arise when terms discussed verbally do\nnot appear in the formal lease.\nPay particular attention to the fit-out period, all recurring charges, escalation,\nexit terms, utility billing and tax-compliant invoices. Record accepted changes in\nthe formal lease. A proposal does not create an automatic right to the requested\nterms.\nBefore sending or acknowledging an LOI, seek legal advice where the terms,\ndeposits, exclusivity or other commitments may be consequential. There is no\ngeneral lease-value threshold below which this template is guaranteed\nsufficient. Have the formal lease reviewed before signing.\n[YOUR FULL NAME OR REGISTERED BUSINESS NAME] [Your Complete\nAddress] [Your Contact Number] [Your Email Address]\n\n[DATE]\n[FULL NAME OF LESSOR] [Lessor's Complete Address]\nRE: Letter of Intent to Lease Commercial Space at [COMPLETE ADDRESS OF\nCOMMERCIAL SPACE]\nDear [MR./MS./ATTY./DR. - LESSOR'S LAST NAME],\nI write on behalf of [YOUR NAME / YOUR BUSINESS NAME] to formally express\nour intent to lease the commercial space located at [COMPLETE ADDRESS OF\nCOMMERCIAL SPACE], with an approximate floor area of [FLOOR AREA IN\nSQUARE METERS] square meters (the \"Premises\"), for the purpose of operating\na [SPECIFIC TYPE OF BUSINESS - e.g., milk tea shop / retail clothing store /\nwater refilling station].\nWe propose the following terms as the basis for our formal Lease Agreement:","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 138","text":"TEMPLATE 2 / CONTINUED\n\nSECTION 1 - LEASE TERM\nA fixed lease term of [PROPOSED LEASE TERM - e.g., two (2) years / three (3)\nyears], commencing on [PROPOSED LEASE COMMENCEMENT DATE - this is\nthe date rent begins running, after the rent-free fit-out period] and expiring on\n[COMPUTED EXPIRY DATE].\nThe Lessee shall have the option to renew this lease for an additional period of\n[RENEWAL TERM - e.g., one (1) year / two (2) years] under mutually agreed\nterms, provided the Lessee provides written notice of its intention to renew not\nlater than [NUMBER - e.g., sixty (60)] days before the expiration of the original\nlease term.\nPREPARER NOTE\nClarify whether this is an enforceable renewal option with settled terms or an\nagreement to negotiate future terms. “Under mutually agreed terms” may leave\nmaterial terms open. Have counsel align the clause with the continuation rights\nyou intend to obtain.\n\nSECTION 2 - MONTHLY BASE RENTAL\nA monthly base rental of Philippine Peso [MONTHLY RENT IN FIGURES] (PhP\n[AMOUNT IN WORDS]), payable on or before the [DATE - e.g., 5th] day of each\ncalendar month.\n\nSECTION 3 - RENT-FREE FIT-OUT PERIOD\nA rent-free fit-out period of [NUMBER - e.g., thirty (30) / forty-five (45) / sixty\n(60)] calendar days immediately preceding the Lease Commencement Date,\nduring which the Lessee shall be entitled to possession of the Premises for the\npurpose of conducting fit-out, renovation, installation, and preparation works,\nwithout obligation to pay base rent or any maintenance charges.\nThe rent-free fit-out period shall commence on [FIT-OUT START DATE] and\nconclude on [FIT-OUT END DATE - the day before the Lease Commencement\nDate in Section 1]. The Lease Commencement Date in Section 1 above is the\ndate immediately following the conclusion of this rent-free period.\nThe Lessee shall be responsible for all utility costs - electricity and water consumed during the fit-out period.\nPREPARER NOTE","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 139","text":"TEMPLATE 2 / CONTINUED\n\nThis is your most important negotiating point in the entire LOI. A 15- to 30-day\nfit-out request is a negotiation example to discuss early — before you have\nindicated that you have already decided on the space regardless of terms. Frame\nit as standard practice: \"Given the fit-out and installation works required before\nwe can open, we are requesting a 30-day rent-free fit-out period before rent\ncommences — this would allow us to open a properly operating business rather\nthan rushing our setup while rent is already running.\" If the lessor counters with\na shorter period, assess whether the shorter period still makes the budget\nworkable — even 15 days of rent-free fit-out at Php 30,000 per month saves Php\n15,000 of working capital.\n\nSECTION 4 - SECURITY DEPOSIT\nA security deposit equivalent to [NUMBER - e.g., two (2)] months' base rent Philippine Peso [COMPUTED AMOUNT IN FIGURES] (PhP [AMOUNT IN\nWORDS]) - payable upon signing of the formal Lease Agreement.\nThe security deposit shall be held by the Lessor as security for the faithful\nperformance of the Lessee's obligations under the Lease Agreement and shall be\nrefunded to the Lessee in full within [NUMBER - e.g., thirty (30)] calendar days\nafter the termination or expiration of the lease and the Lessee's delivery of the\nPremises in the agreed condition, less only amounts legitimately deducted for\ndamages beyond normal wear and tear as documented in a written end-of-lease\ncondition report.\nPREPARER NOTE\nTwo months is a negotiation example, not a statutory commercial-lease limit.\nCompare the requested deposit with your available working capital. Three\nmonths of rent locked in a deposit before you have generated a peso of revenue\nis a significant drain on working capital that serves the lessor's interest, not\nyours.\n\nSECTION 5 - ADVANCE RENT\nAdvance rent equivalent to [NUMBER - e.g., one (1)] month's base rent Philippine Peso [MONTHLY RENT IN FIGURES] (PhP [AMOUNT IN WORDS]) payable upon signing of the formal Lease Agreement, to be applied to the [first /\nlast] month of the lease term.\n\nSECTION 6 - COMMON USE SERVICE AREA AND MAINTENANCE\nCHARGES\nAny Common Use Service Area charges, building maintenance fees, association\ndues, or equivalent recurring charges applicable to the Premises shall not\nexceed Philippine Peso [MAXIMUM MONTHLY CUSA AMOUNT IN FIGURES]\n(PhP [AMOUNT IN WORDS]) per month and shall be itemized separately from\nthe base rent in all monthly billings issued to the Lessee.","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 140","text":"TEMPLATE 2 / CONTINUED\n\nPREPARER NOTE\nIf the lessor's property has no formal CUSA structure, include this clause\nanyway using a reasonable monthly cap amount — it prevents informal\n\"maintenance fees\" or \"building charges\" from appearing on your billing\nstatements at the lessor's discretion after the lease is signed. If you cannot\ndetermine the appropriate cap amount before negotiating, ask the lessor for a\nbreakdown of all charges that will appear on your monthly billing before you\nagree to any amount. Get the breakdown in writing.\n\nSECTION 7 - ANNUAL RENT ESCALATION\nThe monthly base rental shall be subject to an annual increase not exceeding\n[PERCENTAGE - e.g., five percent (5%)] of the prevailing monthly base rental,\neffective on each anniversary of the Lease Commencement Date set forth in\nSection 1.\nPREPARER NOTE\nPush for 5 percent or lower. Before agreeing to any escalation rate, compute the\nescalated monthly rent for every year of your proposed lease term and verify\nthat the Year 2 and Year 3 rent at the escalated rate remains within your\nfinancial model's break-even capacity. If the Year 3 escalated rent breaks your\nmodel, negotiate the rate down before signing — not after Year 3 arrives.\n\nSECTION 8 - PERMITTED USE\nThe Premises shall be used by the Lessee exclusively for the operation of a\n[TYPE OF BUSINESS] and directly related business activities. Any material\nchange in the nature of the business use shall require prior written consent of\nthe Lessor, which consent shall not be unreasonably withheld.\n\nSECTION 9 - UTILITY CONNECTIONS AND SUB-METERING\nThe Lessee shall have the right to apply for direct utility connections - electricity\nand water - in the Lessee's name, subject to the applicable utility provider's\nrequirements and the physical configuration of the Premises.\nWhere the Premises are served by sub-metering, the electricity charge shall use\nthe following transparent, agreed formula: [METERED CONSUMPTION ×\nAGREED RATE BASIS, PLUS ANY SPECIFICALLY AGREED LAWFUL\nCHARGES]. The Lessor shall provide the relevant meter readings and\nsupporting utility bills. Any mark-up, administration fee or other charge must be\nexpressly stated and comply with applicable utility and regulatory rules.\nPREPARER NOTE\nObtain the meter readings, supplier bills and agreed billing formula before\naccepting the arrangement. Confirm every component, administration fee and\nallocation method in writing, and have any disputed charge checked against the\ncontract and applicable utility rules.","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 141","text":"TEMPLATE 2 / CONTINUED\n\nSECTION 10 - BIR-COMPLIANT INVOICES AND PAYMENT\nDOCUMENTATION\nThe Lessor agrees to issue the BIR-compliant invoice required for rental\ntransactions and any applicable supplementary acknowledgment of payment, in\naccordance with current invoicing rules. The parties shall coordinate any legally\nrequired withholding documentation.\nPREPARER NOTE\nConfirm the lessor’s invoicing arrangements before signing. Keep payment\nevidence and ask your CPA about invoices, withholding and expense\nsubstantiation. A bank transfer alone is not a substitute for every required tax\ndocument; refusal to provide a document does not by itself establish the lessor’s\ntax status.\n\nSECTION 11 - PRE-TERMINATION\nThe Lessee may pre-terminate this lease prior to its natural expiration by\nproviding the Lessor with [NUMBER - e.g., sixty (60)] calendar days' advance\nwritten notice. Upon pre-termination in accordance with this Section, the\nLessee's financial obligation shall be limited to: (a) payment of rent and charges\nthrough the end of the notice period; and (b) forfeiture of [NUMBER - e.g., one\n(1)] month's security deposit. No additional financial penalties beyond those\nspecified in this Section shall apply to a pre-termination made in accordance\nwith this Section's notice requirement.\nPREPARER NOTE\nThis clause defines your exit options. A pre-termination penalty of one month's\nforfeited security deposit with 60 days' notice is a proposed negotiating position\nto evaluate with counsel. If the lessor insists on a larger penalty, ensure the\ntriggering conditions are defined with precision — vague pre-termination\npenalty language can be argued by a lessor to apply even to situations you did\nnot intend to cover, including failure to renew at expiration.\n\nSECTION 12 - FORCE MAJEURE AND GOVERNMENT-MANDATED\nCLOSURES\nIn the event that the Premises become unusable or access to the Premises is\nlegally prohibited due to causes beyond the reasonable control of both parties including but not limited to natural disasters, fires, floods,\ngovernment-mandated closures, health emergency quarantine orders, or other\nforce majeure events - the Lessee's obligation to pay base rent shall be\nsuspended for the duration of the period during which the Premises cannot be\nlawfully used for their intended purpose.\nPREPARER NOTE","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 142","text":"TEMPLATE 2 / CONTINUED\n\nThis proposes an express allocation of closure risk. Negotiate the trigger, relief,\nduration and notice procedure with counsel. Do not assume a government\nclosure automatically suspends rent without considering the law, facts and lease\nterms.\n\nSECTION 13 - FIT-OUT IMPROVEMENTS AND OWNERSHIP\nAll removable improvements, fixtures, and equipment installed by the Lessee including but not limited to freestanding shelving, display counters, movable\nequipment, and appliances - shall remain the property of the Lessee and may be\nremoved by the Lessee upon the termination or expiration of the lease, provided\nthe Lessee restores any damage caused by their removal.\nFixed improvements that become structurally integrated into the Premises including tiling, built-in cabinetry, electrical panel upgrades, and ceiling works shall be discussed and their ownership and disposition agreed upon in writing in\nthe formal Lease Agreement prior to execution.\nPREPARER NOTE\nThis clause establishes the baseline principle that removable items are yours.\nThe formal lease should include a more specific list of which improvements fall\ninto each category — removable and fixed — particularly for high-value fit-out\ninvestments. Have a lawyer review the improvements clause in the formal lease,\nas the distinction between removable and structural improvement is the most\ncommon source of end-of-lease disputes between Philippine commercial tenants\nand their landlords.\n\nSECTION 14 - CONDITION REPORT AT POSSESSION\nPrior to the commencement of any fit-out works, both parties shall conduct a\njoint physical inspection of the Premises and prepare a written Condition Report\ndocumenting the existing state of the Premises - including any pre-existing\ndamage, defects, or non-functional fixtures - supported by dated photographic\ndocumentation. The Condition Report shall be signed by both parties and\nattached to the formal Lease Agreement as a binding exhibit.\nPREPARER NOTE\nThis clause protects your security deposit at end of lease. Without a signed\ncondition report at possession, any pre-existing damage to the Premises\nbecomes a dispute between your word and the lessor's — and you do not want to\nbe in that dispute.\nThis Letter of Intent is presented in good faith as a basis for lease negotiations\nand is not intended to be legally binding on either party. The binding agreement\nbetween the parties will be the formal Lease Agreement, to be prepared by\n[LESSOR / LESSEE - specify who will prepare the draft] and reviewed by both\nparties before execution.","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 143","text":"TEMPLATE 2 / CONTINUED\n\nI am available at your convenience to discuss these proposed terms further and\nlook forward to reaching a mutually beneficial arrangement.\nRespectfully,\n[YOUR FULL NAME] [BUSINESS NAME, if applicable] [Contact Number] [Email\nAddress] [Date]\nAcknowledged and agreed in principle:\n[LESSOR'S FULL NAME] [Date]\nPREPARER NOTE\nAcknowledge only the intended status of the discussions. A signed LOI can have\nlegal consequences depending on its substance. Counsel should confirm the\nnon-binding language and any exceptions before either party signs.","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 144","text":"TEMPLATE 3\n\nTEMPLATE 3\n\nProbationary employment\ncontract framework\nFor retail, food service and comparable small businesses\nUse this framework for a role that can lawfully be filled on a probationary basis.\nIt emphasizes reasonable, role-specific standards communicated at engagement,\ntimely evaluation and the correct process for the actual ground of any\nseparation.\nHave a Philippine labor lawyer review the completed contract before use.\nCustomize it for the role, work arrangement, wage order and employee\ncoverage. Recheck it when the role, facts or law change; one review does not\nguarantee suitability for every future hire.\nProbation is generally limited to six months under Article 296, subject to lawful\nexceptions. Six calendar months should not be reduced to a universal 180-day\nformula. Have counsel verify the exact start and end dates. Set an internal\nreview well before the end date; continued work beyond the lawful probationary\nperiod can establish regular status.\n\nPROBATIONARY EMPLOYMENT CONTRACT\nThis Probationary Employment Contract (the \"Contract\") is entered into on\n[DATE OF CONTRACT SIGNING] at [CITY/MUNICIPALITY], Philippines, by and\nbetween:\n\nTHE EMPLOYER:\n[FULL REGISTERED BUSINESS NAME], a business duly registered with the\n[DTI / SEC] under Registration No. [REGISTRATION NUMBER], with principal\nplace of business at [COMPLETE BUSINESS ADDRESS], represented in this\nContract by [NAME OF AUTHORIZED SIGNATORY] in the capacity of\n[POSITION/TITLE]\n(hereinafter referred to as the \"Employer\");\n\n— AND —\nTHE EMPLOYEE:\n[EMPLOYEE'S FULL LEGAL NAME], of legal age, Filipino, residing at\n[EMPLOYEE'S COMPLETE HOME ADDRESS], holder of [GOVERNMENT ID\nTYPE - e.g., Philippine National ID / Passport / Driver's License] No. [ID\nNUMBER]","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 145","text":"TEMPLATE 3 / CONTINUED\n\n(hereinafter referred to as the \"Employee\").\n\nRECITALS\nWHEREAS, the Employer is engaged in the business of [BRIEF DESCRIPTION\nOF BUSINESS - e.g., retail food and beverage operations / retail clothing /\npersonal care services] at the above-stated address;\nWHEREAS, the Employer desires to engage the services of the Employee on a\nprobationary basis for the position described in this Contract, subject to the\nEmployee meeting the specific reasonable standards set forth in Article III;\nWHEREAS, the Employee accepts engagement under the terms and conditions\nof this Contract;\nNOW, THEREFORE, in consideration of the foregoing premises and the mutual\ncovenants herein contained, the parties agree as follows:\n\nARTICLE I - POSITION AND DUTIES\n1.1 The Employer engages the Employee as [JOB TITLE - e.g., Sales Associate /\nFood Service Crew / Cashier / Kitchen Staff / Service Technician], assigned to\n[BRANCH NAME OR LOCATION].\n1.2 The Employee's primary duties and responsibilities shall include, but are not\nlimited to, the following:\nPREPARER NOTE\nList specific, role-relevant duties — not generic descriptions. The duties listed\nhere form the basis for the performance standards in Article III. If the duties are\nvague, the standards will necessarily be vague, which weakens your legal\nposition in a non-regularization dispute. Be specific. Examples: \"Processing\ncustomer sales transactions accurately using the POS system and issuing the\nrequired BIR-compliant invoices for sales.\" \"Maintaining the cleanliness and\norganization of the assigned work area in accordance with the posted daily\ncleaning checklist.\" \"Performing accurate daily inventory counts of assigned\nproduct categories and recording results in the inventory log.\" \"Preparing food\nand beverage products in accordance with posted recipe standards and portion\nguides.\"\na. [SPECIFIC DUTY 1] b. [SPECIFIC DUTY 2] c. [SPECIFIC DUTY 3] d.\n[SPECIFIC DUTY 4] e. [SPECIFIC DUTY 5] f. Such other duties as may be\nreasonably assigned by the Employer from time to time, consistent with the\nEmployee's position and capabilities.\n\nARTICLE II - PROBATIONARY PERIOD AND EVALUATION TIMELINE","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 146","text":"TEMPLATE 3 / CONTINUED\n\n2.1 The Employee is engaged on a probationary basis from [START DATE]\nthrough [VERIFIED END DATE], for a period not exceeding the lawful limit\nunder Article 296 of the Labor Code, unless a lawful exception applies. The\nexact dates shall be verified before engagement.\nPREPARER NOTE\nCalculate the agreed lawful probationary period using the applicable legal rule\nand calendar dates. Do not use a fixed 180-day assumption or extend probation\ninformally.\n2.2 The Employer shall conduct and document a formal evaluation against\nArticle III standards on or before [EVALUATION DATE], set sufficiently before\nthe verified end date to allow assessment, communication and any required\nprocess.\n2.3 The Employer shall communicate the evaluation outcome and any action in\nwriting on or before [REVIEWED NOTICE DATE], observing the correct\nprocedure for the ground relied upon and the lawful probationary end date.\n2.4 Regular status shall be determined in accordance with applicable law. The\nEmployer shall document the Employee’s status and communicate it in writing; a\nwritten notice is not the exclusive means by which regular status can arise.\n2.5 If the Employee does not meet one or more of the performance standards set\nforth in Article III, the Employer may decline to regularize the Employee, subject\nto compliance with the due process requirements set forth in Article VII of this\nContract.\n2.6 Nothing in this Contract limits statutory security of tenure. An employee\nallowed to work after the probationary period shall be considered a regular\nemployee in accordance with Article 296.\nPREPARER NOTE\nTrack the verified dates and act lawfully in time. A contract cannot remove\nregular status that arises by operation of law.\n\nARTICLE III - PROBATIONARY PERFORMANCE STANDARDS\nPREPARER NOTE\nUse reasonable, role-specific standards and communicate them at engagement\nin a language the Employee understands. Keep signed evidence. Confirm the\nlegal adequacy of the standards with counsel. A generic label such as\n“satisfactory performance” may be insufficient.\nThe Employee is hereby informed, at the time of engagement, that the following\nspecific and reasonable standards must be met during the Probationary Period\nand demonstrated consistently by the Evaluation Date in Article II to qualify for\nconsideration for regular employment:","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 147","text":"TEMPLATE 3 / CONTINUED\n\n3.1 Attendance and Punctuality Standard\nThe Employee shall maintain an attendance record of not less than\n[PERCENTAGE - e.g., 95%] of scheduled working days during the Probationary\nPeriod and shall report to work on time as defined by the posted shift schedule.\nTardiness is defined as arrival more than [NUMBER - e.g., five (5)] minutes after\nthe scheduled shift start time. Accumulation of more than [NUMBER - e.g., three\n(3)] instances of tardiness in any calendar month, or more than [NUMBER - e.g.,\ntwo (2)] absences without prior approval in any calendar month, shall trigger a\ndocumented assessment against this standard, taking relevant circumstances\nand applicable law into account.\n\n3.2 Work Quality and Accuracy Standard\nPREPARER NOTE\nCustomize this standard specifically for the role. For a cashier or POS operator:\n\"The Employee shall maintain a cash variance rate of not more than Philippine\nPeso [AMOUNT] per shift as verified by the daily cash reconciliation record. Two\nor more cash variance incidents exceeding this threshold in any calendar month\nshall trigger a documented assessment against this standard, taking relevant\ncircumstances and applicable law into account.\" For food service crew: \"The\nEmployee shall prepare and serve products in accordance with the posted recipe\nstandards, portion guides, and food safety protocols. A customer complaint rate\nattributable to the Employee's food preparation or service exceeding [NUMBER]\ndocumented complaints per month shall trigger a documented assessment\nagainst this standard, taking relevant circumstances and applicable law into\naccount.\" For a sales associate: \"The Employee shall achieve a minimum average\ndaily sales assistance count of [NUMBER] completed customer transactions as\nrecorded in the POS system. Failure to meet this minimum for two consecutive\nweeks shall trigger a documented assessment against this standard, taking\nrelevant circumstances and applicable law into account.\"","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 147","text":"[SPECIFIC, MEASURABLE QUALITY STANDARD APPROPRIATE FOR\nTHIS ROLE]\n3.3 Customer Service Standard\nThe Employee shall consistently demonstrate courteous, professional, and\nresponsive customer service behavior in all customer interactions, as assessed\nthrough [ASSESSMENT METHOD - e.g., direct supervisor observation /\ncustomer feedback forms / mystery customer evaluations]. [NUMBER - e.g., Two\n(2)] or more documented customer complaints directly attributable to the\nEmployee's conduct or attitude toward customers within the Probationary\nPeriod shall trigger a documented assessment against this standard, taking\nrelevant circumstances and applicable law into account.","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 148","text":"TEMPLATE 3 / CONTINUED\n\n3.4 Policy and Procedure Compliance Standard\nThe Employee shall comply with all Employer policies, standard operating\nprocedures, safety protocols, hygiene standards, and workplace rules as\ncommunicated through orientation, training sessions, posted notices, and the\nEmployee Handbook if applicable. Issuance of [NUMBER - e.g., two (2)] or more\nwritten warnings for policy violations during the Probationary Period shall\ntrigger a documented assessment against this standard, taking relevant\ncircumstances and applicable law into account.\n\n3.5 Teamwork and Cooperation Standard\nThe Employee shall work cooperatively and professionally with co-employees\nand direct supervisors, follow reasonable and lawful instructions from\nauthorized supervisors in a timely and willing manner, and contribute\nconstructively to the team's operational performance. Documented incidents of\ninsubordination, deliberate interference with co-employees' work performance,\nor conduct that creates a hostile working environment shall trigger a\ndocumented assessment against this standard, taking relevant circumstances\nand applicable law into account.\nPREPARER NOTE\nAdd additional standards specific to your role and business type if needed.\nExamples for food businesses: a food safety and hygiene compliance standard; a\nspecific food preparation accuracy or speed standard. Examples for retail: a\nstock accuracy standard; a visual merchandising compliance standard. The more\nrole-specific and measurable the standards, the stronger your legal position in a\nnon-regularization proceeding.\n\n3.6 Employee Acknowledgment of Performance Standards\nThe Employee hereby expressly acknowledges that:\na. The performance standards set forth in Sections 3.1 through 3.5 above have\nbeen read to and by the Employee and have been fully explained in a language\nunderstood by the Employee on the date this Contract is signed;\nb. The Employee understands each standard, understands what is required to\nmeet each standard during the Probationary Period, and understands that\nfailure to meet any one or more of these standards may result in\nnon-regularization or termination of employment during or at the end of the\nProbationary Period;\nc. The Employee has had the opportunity to ask questions about these standards\nand any questions raised have been answered to the Employee's satisfaction\nbefore signing this Contract.","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 149","text":"TEMPLATE 3 / CONTINUED\n\nARTICLE IV - COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS\n4.1 Basic Wage\nThe Employee shall receive a basic wage of Philippine Peso [AMOUNT IN\nWORDS] (PhP [AMOUNT IN FIGURES]) per [DAY / MONTH], on a [DAILY-PAID /\nMONTHLY-PAID] basis. The applicable payroll basis, paid days, premiums and\ndeductions shall be set out in [PAYROLL SCHEDULE / ANNEX], consistent with\nlaw.\nPREPARER NOTE\nThe daily wage rate must meet or exceed the current daily minimum wage\napplicable to your region, industry classification, and establishment size as set\nby the Regional Tripartite Wages and Productivity Board. Verify the current\napplicable rate at nwpc.dole.gov.ph before completing this field. Include the\nspecific Regional Wage Order number and effectivity date in the next\nsub-section to document the compliance basis.\nThis basic wage is in compliance with [REGION NAME] Regional Wage Order\nNo. [WAGE ORDER NUMBER], effective [EFFECTIVITY DATE OF CURRENT\nWAGE ORDER].\n\n4.2 Payroll Schedule\nWages shall be paid on a semi-monthly basis - on the [DATE - e.g., 15th] and the\n[DATE - e.g., last] day of each calendar month - through [PAYMENT METHOD e.g., cash / bank transfer to the Employee's designated account].\n\n4.3 Mandatory Government Deductions\nThe following deductions shall be made from the Employee's wages in\naccordance with applicable law:\na. The Employee’s SSS share under the currently applicable contribution\nschedule;\nb. The Employee’s PhilHealth share under the currently applicable rules;\nc. The Employee’s mandatory Pag-IBIG contribution under the current fund\nsalary, rate and cap, plus any separately authorized voluntary contribution;\nd. Withholding tax on compensation, where applicable under current BIR rules.\nThe Employer shall separately pay its legally required shares and shall not\ncharge those shares to the Employee.\n\n4.4 13th Month Pay","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 150","text":"TEMPLATE 3 / CONTINUED\n\nFor covered employees, 13th month pay shall be computed as the actual total\nbasic salary earned during the calendar year divided by twelve (12), payable\nwithin the legally required deadline. The same actual-basic-salary formula\napplies to a partial year, subject to applicable rules and any more favorable\nbenefit.\n\nARTICLE V - WORK SCHEDULE\n5.1 The regular schedule shall be [NUMBER] working hours per day and\n[NUMBER] days per week, excluding a [DURATION] unpaid meal period, on the\nschedule provided to the Employee. For example, eight working hours plus a\none-hour unpaid meal period normally span nine hours. Rest periods, rest days,\ncompensable time and premium pay shall comply with applicable law.\n5.2 The Employer reserves the right to adjust shift assignments, rest days, and\nshift timings based on the operational requirements of the business, subject to\nreasonable advance notice to the Employee consistent with DOLE requirements.\n5.3 Overtime and other premium-pay work shall be paid at the legally applicable\nrates. The Employee must follow the Employer’s advance-authorization\nprocedure. That procedure does not remove the obligation to pay for\ncompensable work that the Employer required, permitted or otherwise must pay\nunder law.\n\nARTICLE VI - LEAVE BENEFITS\n6.1 Service Incentive Leave\nWhere Article 95 and its implementing rules apply, an employee who has\nrendered at least one year of service is entitled to five days of service incentive\nleave per year, subject to lawful coverage rules and exemptions. Unused\nstatutory SIL shall be handled and paid in accordance with law. Any more\nfavorable contractual leave is described in [LEAVE POLICY / ANNEX].\n\n6.2 Leave During Probationary Period\nLeave during probation shall be administered according to the applicable\nstatutory entitlement and the Employer’s lawful leave policy. Probationary\nstatus does not by itself remove maternity, paternity or other statutory benefits\nwhere their eligibility conditions are met. Specify any additional contractual\nleave in [LEAVE POLICY / ANNEX].\nPREPARER NOTE\nCheck SIL coverage and exemptions, including the rule concerning\nestablishments regularly employing fewer than ten employees. A promised\ncontractual benefit may still apply even where a statutory exemption exists. Do\nnot treat every leave request during probation as unpaid automatically.","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 151","text":"TEMPLATE 3 / CONTINUED\n\nARTICLE VII - DUE PROCESS FOR DISCIPLINARY ACTION AND\nTERMINATION\n7.1 Procedure for Just-Cause Termination\nFor termination based on a just cause, the Employer shall comply with the\napplicable written-notice and opportunity-to-be-heard requirements. Other\ndisciplinary actions and probationary failure-to-qualify decisions shall follow the\nprocess applicable to their actual grounds.\nFirst Notice - Notice to Explain: A written notice stating the specific facts and\ngrounds relied upon, giving the Employee a reasonable opportunity of at least\nfive (5) calendar days from receipt to submit an explanation, and satisfying the\nrequirements applicable to the case.\nOpportunity to Be Heard: After receipt of the Employee's written explanation or after the explanation period has elapsed without a response - the Employer\nshall provide the Employee an opportunity to be heard, which may take the form\nof a written explanation, a clarificatory conference, or both, as required by\napplicable law, including a conference where legally required.\nSecond Notice - Notice of Decision: A written notice informing the Employee of\nthe Employer's decision following the review of the Employee's explanation,\nstating clearly the specific ground for the action taken and the corresponding\ndisciplinary consequence.\n\n7.2 Non-Regularization for Failure to Meet Probationary Standards\nWhere the Employer determines at or before the Evaluation Date that the\nEmployee has failed to meet one or more of the specific standards in Article III,\nthe Employer shall:\na. Assess the Employee fairly against reasonable standards made known at\nengagement and retain the documented basis for the assessment;\nb. Apply any additional evaluation or response process required by law, this\nContract or an applicable policy;\nc. Serve the required written notice of failure to meet the disclosed probationary\nstandards, stating the reasons and effective date, within the time required by\nlaw. Have counsel confirm the timing and service before action.\n\n7.3 Termination for Just Cause\nFor termination based on a statutory just cause under Article 297 of the Labor\nCode, the Employer shall establish the required legal elements and comply with\nthe applicable notice and opportunity-to-be-heard requirements, including\nSection 7.1, regardless of probationary status.","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 152","text":"TEMPLATE 3 / CONTINUED\n\n7.4 Termination for Authorized Cause\nFor termination based on an authorized cause, the Employer shall establish the\napplicable statutory ground, provide the notices required by law to the\nEmployee and DOLE, and pay any separation pay legally required for that cause\nand the actual circumstances. Have counsel confirm the ground, notice period\nand computation before action.\nPREPARER NOTE\nA just-cause dismissal, failure to meet probationary standards and an\nauthorized-cause termination have different substantive and procedural\nrequirements. Identify the correct ground first. Following a notice template\nalone does not make a termination valid. Obtain labor-law advice before acting.\n\nARTICLE VIII - CONFIDENTIALITY\n8.1 The Employee acknowledges that in the course of employment, they may\nhave access to confidential and proprietary business information belonging to\nthe Employer, including but not limited to: customer lists and customer contact\ninformation; supplier identities and pricing terms; operational procedures,\nrecipes, and formulas; financial data; pricing strategies; and business systems\nand processes.\n8.2 The Employee agrees not to disclose, share, reproduce, transmit, or use for\npersonal benefit - during or after the term of employment - any confidential\ninformation obtained in the course of employment with the Employer, for a\nperiod of [NUMBER - e.g., one (1)] year following the separation of the\nEmployee from the Employer's service for any reason.\n8.3 The Employee shall not use any confidential information for the purpose of\ndirectly or indirectly competing with the Employer's business during the period\nspecified in Section 8.2.\nPREPARER NOTE\nHave counsel review the scope, duration and enforceability of confidentiality\nand any restrictive covenant. Do not use this clause to prevent lawful disclosures\nor create an unreviewed blanket restriction on earning a livelihood.\n\nARTICLE IX - GENERAL PROVISIONS\n9.1 Governing Law. This Contract shall be governed by the Labor Code of the\nPhilippines and all applicable DOLE regulations and issuances, as amended from\ntime to time. In the event of any conflict between this Contract and mandatory\nprovisions of Philippine labor law, the applicable law shall prevail.\n9.2 Entire Agreement. This Contract constitutes the entire agreement between\nthe parties with respect to the Employee's probationary employment and\nsupersedes all prior discussions, representations, negotiations, and\nunderstandings, whether oral or written, relating to the subject matter hereof.","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 153","text":"TEMPLATE 3 / CONTINUED\n\n9.3 Amendments. No amendment, modification, or waiver of any provision of this\nContract shall be effective unless made in writing and signed by both parties.\n9.4 Separability. If any provision of this Contract is held to be invalid, illegal, or\nunenforceable by a court or labor arbiter of competent jurisdiction, the validity,\nlegality, and enforceability of the remaining provisions shall not be affected or\nimpaired thereby.\n9.5 No Waiver. Failure by either party to enforce any provision of this Contract\nat any time shall not be construed as a waiver of that party's right to enforce\nsuch provision in the future.\nIN WITNESS WHEREOF, the parties have hereunto set their hands on the date\nand at the place first written above.\n\nTHE EMPLOYER:\nSignature over Printed Name: _________________________________ Name: [FULL\nNAME OF AUTHORIZED SIGNATORY] Position: [POSITION/TITLE] Business\nName: [FULL REGISTERED BUSINESS NAME] Date: ___________________________\n\nTHE EMPLOYEE:\nSignature over Printed Name: _________________________________ Name:\n[EMPLOYEE'S FULL LEGAL NAME] Date: ___________________________\n\nEMPLOYEE ACKNOWLEDGMENT OF PERFORMANCE STANDARDS AND\nRECEIPT OF CONTRACT\nI, [EMPLOYEE'S FULL LEGAL NAME], hereby acknowledge and confirm the\nfollowing:\nI have received a signed copy of this Probationary Employment Contract on\n[DATE];\nI have read the entire Contract, including Article III on Probationary\nPerformance Standards, in full;\nThe performance standards in Article III were explained to me in [LANGUAGE e.g., Filipino / English / Bisaya / Ilokano] on [DATE] by [NAME AND POSITION\nOF PERSON WHO EXPLAINED THE STANDARDS];\nI understand each performance standard, I understand what I am required to do\nto meet each standard, and I understand that failure to meet these standards\nduring the Probationary Period may result in my non-regularization or\ntermination of employment;\nI had the opportunity to ask questions about the Contract and the performance\nstandards before signing, and any questions I raised were answered to my\nsatisfaction;\nI am signing this Contract and this Acknowledgment freely, voluntarily, and\nwithout duress, coercion, or undue influence from any person.","weight":0.5},{"doc":"business-opening-checklist-ph","ref":"The Business Opening Checklist PH, Page 154","text":"TEMPLATE 3 / CONTINUED\n\nSignature over Printed Name: _________________________________ [EMPLOYEE'S\nFULL LEGAL NAME] Date: ___________________________\n\nWITNESSED BY:\nSignature over Printed Name: _________________________________ Name:\n[WITNESS FULL NAME] Position: [WITNESS POSITION] Date:\n___________________________\nPREPARER NOTE\nKeep the contract, acknowledgment and proof that standards were explained in\nthe personnel file using an appropriate retention and privacy policy. Give the\nEmployee a signed copy promptly. The acknowledgment does not waive\nstatutory rights.","weight":0.5}];
